John 1:1 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Years, centuries, ages, are passed over, place what beginning you will in your imagining, you grasp it not in time, for He, from Whom it is derived, still was. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As then when our ship is near shore, cities and port pass in survey before us, which on the open sea vanish, and leave nothing whereon to fix; the eye; so the Evangelist here, taking us with him in  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They say, however, if He is the Son, He was born. We allow it. They rejoin: if the Son was born to the Father, the Father was, before the Son was born to Him. This the Faith rejects. Then they say,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they say that In the beginning does not absolutely express in eternity: for that the same is said of the heaven and the earth: In the beginning God made the heaven and the earth. But are not mad | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The verb to be, has a double signification, sometimes expressing the motions which take place in time, as other verbs do; sometimes the substance of that one thing of which it is predicated, without | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Consider then the world, understand what is written of it. In the beginning God made the heaven en and the earth. Whatever therefore is created is made in the beginning, and you would contain in tim | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To refute those who inferred from Christ’s Birth in time, that He had not been from everlasting, the Evangelist begins with the eternity of the Word, saying, In the beginning was the Word. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because it is an especial attribute of God, to be eternal and without a beginning, he laid this down first: then, lest any one on hearing in the beginning was the Word, should suppose the Word Unbeg | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; From the beginning He is With God: and though independent of time, is not independent of an Author. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Basil the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Again he repeats this, was, because of men blasphemously saying, that there was a time when He was not. Where then was the Word? Illimitable things are not contained in space. Where was He then? Wit | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; It is worth while noting, that, whereas the Word is said to come [be made] to some, as to Hosea, Isaiah, Jeremiah, with God it is not made, as though it were not with Him before. But, the Word havin | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He has not said, was in God, but was with God: exhibiting to us that eternity which He had in accordance with His Person. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Sabellius is overthrown by this text. For he asserts that the Father, Son, and Holy Ghost are one Person, Who sometimes appeared as the Father, sometimes as the Son, sometimes as the Holy Ghost. But | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; You will say, that a word is the sound of the voice, the enunciation of a thing, the expression of a thought: this Word was in the beginning with God, because the utterance of thought is eternal, wh | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; But the title is absolute, and free from the offense of an extraneous subject. To Moses it is said, I have given you for a god to Pharaoh: but is not the reason for the name added, when it is said,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Basil the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Thus cutting off the cavils of blasphemers, and those who ask what the Word is, he replies, and the Word was God. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or combine it thus: From the Word being with God, it follows plainly that there are two Persons. But these two are of one Nature; and therefore it proceeds, In the Word was God: to show that Father  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:1 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; We must add too, that the Word illuminates the Prophets with Divine wisdom, in that He comes to them; but that with God He ever is, because He is God. For which reason he placed and the Word was wit | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:2 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not asserting, as Plato does, one to be intelligence, the other soul; for the Divine Nature is very different from this... But you say, the Father is called God with the addition of the article, the | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:2 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Whereas he had said, the Word was God, the fearfulness, and strangeness of the speech disturbed me; the prophets having declared that God was One. But, to quiet my apprehensions, the fisherman revea | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:2 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Again, to stop any diabolical suspicion, that the Word, because He was God, might have rebelled against His Father, as certain Gentiles fable, or, being separate, have become the antagonist of the F | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:2 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, lest hearing that In the beginning was the Word, you should regard It as eternal, but yet understand the Father’s Life to have some degree of priority, he has introduced the words, The Same was  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:2 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus, the Evangelist having begun with those propositions, reunites them into one, saying, The Same was in the beginning with God. For in the first of the three we learnt in what the Word was, th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Wherefore does he use the substantive verb, was? That you might understand that the Word, Which is coeternal with God the Father, was before all time. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; After speaking of the nature of the Son, he proceeds to His operations, saying, All things were made by him, i.e. every thing whether substance, or property. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus: [It is said], the Word indeed was in the beginning, but it may be that He was not before the beginning. But what says he; All things were made by him. He is infinite by Whom every thing, wh | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Moses indeed, in the beginning of the Old Testament, speaks to us in much detail of the natural world, saying, In the beginning God made the heaven and the earth; and then relates how that the light | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Since all things were made by him, it is evident that light was as also, when God said, Let there be light. And in like manner the rest. But if so, that which God said, viz. Let there be light, is e | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How then can the Word of God be made, when God by the Word made all things? For if the Word Itself were made, by what other Word was It made? If you say it was the Word of the Word by Which That was | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And if It is not made, It is not a creature; but if It is not a creature, It is of the same Substance with the Father. For every substance which is not God is a creature; and what is not a creature  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Arians are wont to say, that all things are spoken of as made by the Son, in the sense in which we say a door is made by a saw, viz. as an instrument; not that He was Himself the Maker. And so t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the preposition by perplex you, and you would learn from Scripture that the Word Itself made all thin as, hear David, You, Lord, in the beginning has laid the foundation of the earth, and the hea | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if you say that the prophet spoke this of the Father, and that Paul applied it to the Son, it comes to the same thing. For he would not have mentioned that as applicable to the Son, unless he fu | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Here too Valentines errs, saying, that the Word supplied to the Creator the cause of the creation of the world. If this interpretation is true, its should have been written that all things had their | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That you may not suppose, when he says, All things were made by Him, that he meant only the things Moses had;, spoken of, he seasonably brings in, And without Him was not any thing made, nothing, th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus; That all things were made by him, is pronouncing too much, it may be said. There is an Unbegotten Who is made of none, and there is the Son Himself begotten from Him Who is Unbegotten. The  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): : Or thus, that you might not think that the things made by the Word had a separate existence, and were not contained in the Word, he says, and without Him was not any thing made: that is, not any thi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, by saying, without Him was not any thing made, he tells us not to suspect Him in any sense to be a thing made. For how can He be a thing made, when God, it is said, made nothing without Him? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; If all things were made by the Word, and in the number of all things is wickedness, and the whole influx of sin, these too were made by the Word; which is false. Now ‘nothing’ and ‘a thing which is  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For sin was not made by Him; for it is manifest that sin is nothing, and that men become nothing when they sin. Nor was an idol made by the Word. It has indeed a sort of form of man, and man himself | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Valentinus excludes from the things made by the Word, all that were made in the ages which he believes to have existed before the Word. This is plainly false; inasmuch as the things which he account | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The folly of those men is not to be listened to, who think nothing is to be understood here as something because it is placed at the end of the sentence: as if it made so any difference whether it w | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; If ‘the word’ be taken for that which is in each man, inasmuch as it was implanted in each by the Word, which was in the beginning then also, we commit nothing without this ‘word’ [reason] taking th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The Evangelist having said that every creature was made by the Word, lest perchance any one might think that His will was changeable, as though He willed on a sudden to make a creature, which from e | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . ‘The passage can be read thus: What was made in Him was life. Therefore the whole universe is life: for what was there not made in Him? He is the Wisdom of God, as is said, In Wisdom have You made t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; It may also be divided thus: That which was made in him; and then, was life; the sense being, that all things that were made by Him and in Him, are life in Him, and are one in Him. They were, that i | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or it can be understood thus. In that he had said, without Him was not anything thing made, one might have been perplexed, and have asked, Was then any thing made by another, which yet was not made  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Or to give another explanation. We will not put the stop at without Him was not any thing made, as the heretics do. For they wishing to prove the Holy Ghost a creature, read, That which was made in Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus: Our Savior is said to be some things not for Himself, but for others; others again, both for Himself and others. When it is said then, That which was as made in Him was life; we must inquir | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had said, In him was life, that you might not suppose that the Word was without life. Now he shows that that life is spiritual, and the light of all reasonable creatures. And the life was the lig | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Life of itself gives illumination to men, but to cattle not: for they have not rational souls, by which to discern wisdom: whereas man, being made in the image of God, has a rational soul, by which  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:4 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said not, the Light of the Jews only, but of all men: for all of us, in so far as we have received intellect and reason, from that Word which created us, are said to be illuminated by Him. For th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; We must not omit to notice, that he puts the life before the light of men. For it would be a contradiction to suppose a being without life to be illuminated; as if life were an addition to illuminat | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whereas that life is the light of men, but foolish hearts cannot receive that light, being so encumbered with sins that they cannot see it; for this cause lest any should think there is no light nea | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; This kind of darkness however is not in men by nature, according to the text in the Ephesians, You were some time darkness, but now are you light in the Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus, The light shines in the darkness of faithful souls, beginning from faith, and drawing onwards to hope; but the deceit and ignorance of undisciplined souls did not comprehended the light of  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: throughout the whole foregoing passage he, had been speaking of creation; then he mentions the spiritual; benefits which the Word brought w with it: and the life was the light of men. He sa | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; But they ask, why is not the Word Itself called the light of men, instead of the life which is in the Word? We reply, that the life here spoken of is not that which rational and irrational animals h | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Life having come to us, the empire of death is dissolved; a light having shone upon us, there is darkness no longer: but there remains ever a life which death, a light which darkness cannot overcome | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; As the light of men is a word expressing two spiritual things, so is darkness also. To one who possesses the light, we attribute both the doing the deeds of the light, and also true understanding, i | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A certain Platonist once said, that the beginning of this Gospel ought to be copied in letters of gold, and placed in the most conspicuous place in every church. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The other Evangelists describe Christ as born in time; John witnesses that He was in the beginning, saying, In the beginning was the Word. The others describe His sudden appearance among men; he wit | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is said above, refers to the Divinity of Christ. He came to us in the form of man, but man in such sense, as that the Godhead was concealed within Him. And therefore there was sent before a gre | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not an Angel, as many have held. The Evangelist here refutes such a notion. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And how could he declare the truth concerning God, unless he were sent from God. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After this esteem nothing that he says as human; for he speaks not his own, but his that sent him. And therefore the Prophet calls him a messenger, I send My messenger, for it is the excellence of a | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What was he called? whose name was John? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, the grace of God, or one in whom is grace, who by his testimony first made known to the world the grace of the New Testament, that is, Christ. Or John may be taken to mean, to whom it is gi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherefore came he? The same came for a witness, to bear witness of the Light. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Some try to undo the testimonies of the Prophets to Christ, by saying that the Son of God had no need of such witnesses; the wholesome words which He uttered and His miraculous acts being sufficient | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not because the light wanted the testimony, but for the reason which John himself self gives, viz. that all might believe on Him. For as He put on flesh to save all men from death; so He sent before | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He says not, that all men should believe in him; for, cursed be the man that trusts in man; but, that all men through him might believe; i.e. by his testimony believe in the Light. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Though some however might not believe, he is not accountable for them. When a man shuts himself up in a dark room, so as to receive no light from the sun’s rays, he is the cause of the deprivation,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Forasmuch however as with us, the one who witnesses, is commonly a more important, a more trustworthy person, than the one to whom he bears witness, to do away with any such notion in the present ca | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it will be said, that we do not allow John or any of the saints to be or ever to have been light. The difference is this: If we call any of the saints light, we put light without the article. So | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What Light it is to which John bears witness, he shows himself, saying, That was the true Light. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; Having said above that John had come, and was sent, to bear witness of the Light, lest any from the recent coming of the witness, should infer the same of Him who is witnessed to, the Evang | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherefore is there added, true? Because man enlightened is called light, but the true Light is that which lightens. For our eyes are called lights, and yet, without a lamp at night, or the sun by da | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let the Manichean blush, who pronounces us the creatures of a dark and malignant creator: for we should never be enlightened, v ere we not the children of the true Light. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Where are those too, who deny Him to be very God? We see here that He is called very Light. But if He lightens every man that comes into the world, how is it that so many have gone on without light? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the words, lightens every man, may be understood to mean, not that there is no one who is not enlightened, but that no one is enlightened except by Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Including both natural and divine wisdom; for as no one can exist of himself, so no one can be wise of himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:9 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus: We must not understand the words, lightens every man that comes into the world, of the growth from hidden seeds to organized bodies, but of the entrance into the invisible world, by the spi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The intellect which is given in us for our direction, and which is called natural reason, is said here to be a light given us by God. But some by the ill use of their reason have darkened t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Light which lightens every man that comes into the world, came here in the flesh; because while He was here in His Divinity alone, the foolish, blind, and unrighteous could not discern Him; thos | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; For as, when a person leaves off speaking, his voice ceases to be, and vanishes; so if the Heavenly Father should cease to speak His Word, the effect of that Word, i.e. the universe which is created | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You must not suppose however, that He was in the world in tile same sense in w which the earth, cattle, men, are in the world; but in the sense in which an artificer controls his own work; whence th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And again, because He was in the world, but not coeval with the world, for this cause he introduced the words, and the world was made by Him: thus taking you back again to the eternal existence of t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here he overthrows at once the insane notion of the Manichaean, who says that the world is the work of a malignant creature, and the opinion of the Arian, that the Son of God is a creature. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what means this, The world was made by Him? The earth, sky, and sea, and all that are therein, are called the world. But in another sense, the lovers of the world are called the world, of whom h | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they who were the friends of God, knew Him even before His presence in the body; whence Christ said below, Your father Abraham rejoiced to see My day. When the Gentiles then interrupt us with th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When He said that the world knew Him not, he c referred to the times of the old dispensation, but what follows H has reference to the time of his preaching; He came to his own. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because all things were made by Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By his own, understand either the world, or Judea, which He had chosen for His inheritance. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He came then to His own, not for His own good, but for the good of others. But whence did He Who fills all things, and is every where present, come? He came out of condescension to us, though in rea | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): But if none at all received, none will be saved. For no one will be saved, but he who received Christ at His coming; and therefore he adds, As many as received Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whether they be bond or free, Greek or Barbarian, wise or unwise, women or men, the young or the aged, all are made meet for the honor, which the Evangelist now proceeds to mention. To them gave He  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O amazing goodness! He was born the Only Son, yet would not remain so; but grudged not to admit joint heirs to His inheritance. Nor was this narrowed by many partaking of it. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said not that He made them the sons of God, but gave them power to become the sons of God: showing that there is need of much care, to preserve the image, which is formed by our adoption in Bapti | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the meaning is, that the most perfect sonship will only be attained at the resurrection, as said the Apostle, Wailing for the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body. He therefore gave us th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because in the matter of these ineffable benefits, the giving of grace belongs to God, but the extending of faith to man, He subjoins, even to those who believe on his name. Why then declare you | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To be made then the sons of God, and brothers of Christ, they must of course be born; for if they are not born, how can they be sons? Now the sons of men are born of flesh and blood, and the will of | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; It should be understood that in holy Scripture, blood in the plural number, has the signification of sin: thus in the Psalms, Deliver me from blood-guiltiness. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In that which follows, Nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, the flesh is put for the female; because, when she was made out of the rib, Adam said, This is now bone of my bone, and f | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The carnal birth of men derives its origin from the embrace of wedlock, but the spiritual is dispensed by the grace of the Holy Spirit. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist makes this declaration, that being taught the vileness and inferiority of our former birth, which is through blood, and the will of the flesh, and understanding the loftiness and nobl | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, Born of God; to prevent surprise and trepidation at so great, so apparently incredible a grace, that men should be born of God; to assure us, he says, And the Word was as made flesh. Wh | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus, After saying that they were born of God, who received Him, he sets forth the cause of this honor, viz. the Word being made flesh, God’s own Son was made the son of man, that he might make t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As our word becomes the bodily voice, by its assumption of that voice, as a means of developing itself externally, so the Word of God was made flesh, by assuming flesh, as a means of manifesting Its | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Council of Ephesus, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The discourse which we utter, which we use in conversation with each other, is incorporeal, imperceptible, impalpable; but clothed in letters and characters, it becomes material, perceptible, tangib | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When we think how the incorporeal soul is joined to the body, so as that of two is made one man, we too shall the more easily receive the notion of the incorporeal Divine substance being joined to t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Apollinarius of Laodicea raised a heresy upon this text; saying, that Christ had flesh only, not a rational soul; in the place of which His divinity directed and controlled His body. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If men are disturbed however by its being said that the Word was made flesh, without mention of a soul; let them know that the flesh is put for the whole man, the part for the whole, by a figure of  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist intends by making mention of the flesh, to show the unspeakable condescension of God, and lead us to admire His compassion, in assuming for our salvation, what was so opposite and inc | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Cyril, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The Word uniting to Himself a body of flesh animated with a rational soul, substantially, was ineffably and incomprehensibly made Man, and called the Son of man, and that not according to the will o | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From the text, The Word was made flesh, we learn this farther, that the Word Itself is man, and being the Son of God was made the Son of a woman, who is rightly called the Mother of God, as having g | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Some, however, who think God the Only-Begotten, God the Word, Who was in the beginning with God, not to be God substantially, but a Word sent forth, the Son being to God the Father, what a word is t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lest from it being said, however, that the Word was made flesh, you should infer improperly a change of His incorruptible nature, he subjoins, And dwelt among us. For that which inhabits is not the  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or, dwelt among us, means, lived amongst men. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said that we are made the sons of God and in no other way than because the Word was made flesh; he mentions another gift, And we saw His glory. Which glory we should not have seen, had He not | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; in that the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us, His birth became a kind of ointment to anoint the eyes of our heart, that we might through His humanity discern His majesty; and therefor | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He subjoins, As of the Only-Begotten of the Father: for many prophets, as Moses, Elijah, and others, workers of miracles, had been glorified, and Angels also who appeared to men, shining with the br | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In Scripture language as, and as it were, are sometimes put not for likeness but reality; whence the expression, As of the Only-Begotten of the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if he said: We saw His glory, such as it was becoming and proper for the Only-Begotten and true Son to have. We have a form of speech, like it, derived from our seeing kings always splendidly rob | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:14 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Full of grace and truth. Of this the meaning is twofold. For it may be understood of the Humanity, and the Divinity of the Incarnate Word, so that the fullness of grace has reference to the Humanity | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, full of grace, inasmuch as His word was gracious, as said David, Full of grace are your lips; and truth, because what Moses and the Prophets spoke or did in figure, Christ did in reality. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He had said before that there was a man sent to bear witness; now he gives definitely the forerunner’s own testimony, which plainly declared the excellence of His Human Nature and the Eternity of Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or he introduces this, as if to say, Do not suppose that we bear witness to this out of gratitude, because we were with Him a long time, and partook of His table; for John who had never seen Him bef | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said, Who comes after me, that is, as to the time of His birth. John was six months before Christ, according to His humanity. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or this does not refer to the birth from Mary; for Christ was born, when this was said by John; but to His coming for the work of preaching. He then said, is made before me; that is, is more illustr | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Arians infer from this Word, that the Son of God is not begotten of the Father, but made like any other creature. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It does not mean - He was made before I was made, but He is preferred to me. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the words, made before me, referred to His coming into being, it was superfluous to add, For He was before me. For who would be so foolish as not to know, that if He was made before him, He was b | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:17 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; This is to be considered a continuation of the Baptist’s testimony to Christ, a point which has escaped the attention of many, who think that from this to, He has declared Him, St. John the Apostle  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; John the Evangelist here adds this testimony to that of John the Baptist, saying, And of his fullness have we all received. These are not the words of the forerunner, but of the disciple; a | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But whet have you received? Grace for grace. So that we are to understand that we have received a certain something from His fullness, and over and above this, grace for grace; that we have first re | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or we have received grace for grace; that is, the new in the place of the old. For as there is a justice and a justice besides, an adoption and another adoption, a circumcision and another circumcis | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, we may refer grace to knowledge, truth to wisdom. Amongst the events of time the highest grace is the uniting of man to God in One Person; in the eternal world the highest truth pertains to God  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Heracleon asserts, that this is a declaration of the disciple, not of the Baptist: an unreasonable supposition; for if the words, Of His fullness have we all received, are the Baptist’s, does not th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; the Evangelist after showing the great superiority of Christ’s gifts, compared with those dispensed by Moses, wishes in the next place to supply an adequate reason for the difference. The o | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is that then which Jacob said, I have seen God face to face; and that which is written of Moses, he talked with God face to face; and that which the prophet Isaiah said of himself, I saw the Lo | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is plainly given us to understand here, that while we are in this mortal state, we see God only through the medium of certain images, not, in the reality of His own nature. A soul influenced by t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the old fathers had seen That very Nature, they would not have contemplated It so variously, for It is in Itself simple and without shape; It sits not, It walks not; these are the qualities of bo | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now it is said, Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God; and again, When He shall appear, we shall be like to Him, for we shall see Him as He is. What is the meaning then of the words  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If however any, while inhabiting this corruptible flesh, can advance to such an immeasurable height of virtue, as to be able to discern by the contemplative vision, the eternal brightness of God, th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For unless any in some sense die to this life, either by leaving the body altogether, or by being so withdrawn and alienated from carnal perceptions, that he may well not know, as the Apostle says,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some hold that in the place of bliss, God is visible in His brightness, but not in His nature. This is to indulge in over much subtlety. For in that simple and unchangeable essence, no division can  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If we say, that the text, No one has seen God, at any time, applies only to men; so that, as the Apostle more plainly interprets it, Whom no man has seen nor can see, no one is to be understood here | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some however there are who conceive that not even the Angels see God. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That very existence which is God, neither Prophets, nor even Angels, nor yet Archangels, have seen. For inquire of the Angels; they say nothing concerning His Substance; but sing, Glory to God in th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which indeed is true so far, that no bodily or even mental vision of man has ever embraced the fullness of God; for it is one thing to see, another to embrace the whole of what you see. A thing is s | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In this complete sense only the Son and the Holy Ghost see the Father. For how can created nature see that which is uncreated? So then no man knows the Father as the Son knows Him: and hence what fo | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The Truth of His Nature did not seem sufficiently explained by the name of Son, unless, in addition, its peculiar force as proper to Him were expressed, so signifying its distinctness from all besid | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He adds, Which is in the bosom of the Father. To dwell in the bosom is much more than simply to see. For he who sees simply, has not the knowledge thoroughly of that which he sees; but he who dwells | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the bosom of the Father, i.e. in the secret Presence of the Father: for God has not the fold on the bosom, as we have; nor must be imagined to sit, as we do; nor is He bound with a girdle, so as  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what has He declared? That God is one. But this the rest of the Prophets and Moses proclaim: what else have we learnt from the Son Who was in the bosom of the Father? In the first place, that th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Farther, if the word declared have reference to the past, it must be considered that He, being made man, declared the doctrine of the Trinity in unity, and how, and by what acts we should prepare ou | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet have there been men, who, deceived by the vanity of their hearts, maintained that the Father is invisible, the Son visible. Now if they call the Son visible, with respect to His connection with  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The text then, No man has seen God at any time, applies not to the Father only, but also to the Son: for He, as Paul said, is the Image of the invisible God; but He who is the Image of the Invisible | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; This is the second testimony of John the Baptist to Christ, the first began with, This is He of Whom I spoke; and ended with, He has declared Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, after the introduction above of John’s testimony to Christ, is preferred before me, the Evangelist now adds when the above testimony was given, And this is the record of John, when the Jews sent | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The Jews of Jerusalem, as being of kin to the Baptist, who was of the priestly stock, send Priests and Levites to ask him who he is; that is, men considered to hold a superior rank to the rest of th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Such confidence had they in John, that they were ready to believe him on his own words: witness how it is said, To ask him, Who are you? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They would not have sent, unless they had been impressed by his lofty exercise of authority, in daring to baptize. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; John, as it appears, saw from the question, that the Priests and Levites had doubts whether it might not be the Christ, who was baptizing; which doubts however they were afraid to profess openly, fo | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He denied directly being what he was not, but he did not deny what he was: thus, by his speaking truth, becoming a true member of Him Whose name he had not dishonestly usurped. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or take this explanation: The Jews were influenced by a kind of human sympathy for John, whom they were reluctant to see made subordinate to Christ, on account of the many marks of greatness about h | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For they knew that Elias was to preach Christ; the name of Christ not being unknown to any among the Jews; but they did not think that our Lord was the Christ: and yet did not altogether imagine tha | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These words gave rise to a very different question. In another place, our Lord, when asked by His disciples concerning the coming of Elias, replied, If you will receive it, this is Elias. But John s | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Some one will say that John was ignorant that he was Elias; as those say, who maintain, from this passage the doctrine of a second incorporation, as though the soul took up a new body, after leaving | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if we examine the truth accurately, that which sounds inconsistent, will be found not really so. The Angel told Zacharias concerning John, He shall go before Him in the spirit and power of Elias | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He answers then the Levites and Priests, I am not, conjecturing what their question meant: for the purport of their examination was to discover, not whether the spirit in both was the same, but whet | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or because John was more than a prophet: for that the prophets announced Him afar off, but John pointed Him out actually present. Then said they to him, Who are you? that we may give an answer to th | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You see them here pressing him still more strongly with their questions, while he on the other hand quietly puts down their suspicions, where they are untrue, and establishes the truth in their plac | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So spoke Esaias: the prophecy was fulfilled in John the Baptist. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You know that the only-begotten Son is called the Word of the Father. Now we know, in the case of our own utterance, the voice first sounds, and then the word is heard. Thus John declares himself to | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Heracleon, in his discussion on John and the Prophets, infers that because the Savior was the Word, and John the voice, therefore the whole of the prophetic order was only sound. To which we reply,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or because he declared the truth plainly, while all who were under the law spoke obscurely. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John cries in the wilderness, because it is to forsaken and destitute Judea that he bears the consolatory tidings of a Redeemer. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:23 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; There is need of the voice crying in the wilderness, that the soul, forsaken by God, may be recalled to making straight the way of the Lord, following no more the crooked paths of the serpent. This  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The way of the Lord is made straight to the heart, when the word of truth is heard with humility; the way of the Lord is made straight to the heart, when the life is formed upon the precept. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The questions of the priests and Levites being answered, another mission comes from the Pharisees: And they that were sent were of the Pharisees. So far as it is allowable to form a conjecture from  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, those very same priests and Levies were of the Pharisees, and, because they could not undermine him by blandishments, began accusing, after they had compelled him to say what he was not. And the | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A saint, even when perversely questioned, is never diverted from the pursuit of goodness. Thus John to the words of envy opposes the words of life: John answered them, saying, I indeed baptize with  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; For how would the question, Why then baptize you, be replied to in any other way, than by setting forth the carnal nature of his own baptism? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John baptizes not with the Spirit, but with water; not being able to remit sins, he washes the bodies of the baptized with water, but not their souls with pardon. Why then cloth he baptize, when he  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . One among you. It was fitting that Christ should mix with the people, and be one of the many, showing every where His humility. Whom you know not; i.e. not, in the most absolute and certain sense; n | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In His low estate He was not seen; and therefore the candle was; lighted. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it was, that our Lord was in the midst of the Pharisees; and they not knowing Him. For they thought that they knew the Scriptures, and therefore, inasmuch as our Lord was pointed out there, He wa | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or thus; Having said, I indeed baptize with water,. in answer to the question, Why baptize you then? - to the next, If you be not Christ? he replies by declaring the preexistent substance of Christ; | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if he said, Do not think that every thing is contained in my baptism; for if my baptism were perfect, another would not come after me with another baptism. This baptism of mine is but an introduc | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Made before me, i.e. preferred before me. He comes after me, that is, He is born after me; He is made before me, that is, He is preferred to me. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But lest you should think this to be the result of comparison, he immediately shows it to be a superiority beyond all comparison; Whose shoe latchet I am not worthy to unloose: as if He said, He is  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To have pronounced himself worthy even of unloosing His shoe’s latchet, he would have been thinking too much of himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: It was a law of the old dispensation, that, if a man refused to take the woman, who of right came to him, to wife, he who by right of relationship came next to be the husband, should unloos | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John having preached the thing concerning Christ publicly and With becoming liberty, the Evangelist mentions the place of His preaching: These things were done in Bethany beyond Jordan, where John w | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or we must suppose two Bethanies; one over Jordan, the other on this side, not far from Jerusalem, the Bethany where Lazarus was raised from the dead. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He mentions this too for another reason, viz. that as He was relating events which had only recently happened, He might, by a reference to the place, appeal to the testimony of those who were presen | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The meaning of Bethany is, house of obedience; by which it is intimated to us, that all must approach to baptism, through the obedience of faith. ORIG. Bethabara means house of preparation; which ag | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; After this testimony, Jesus is seen coming to John, not only persevering in his confession, but also advanced in goodness: as is intimated by the second day. Wherefore it is said, The next day John  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or; Matthew relates directly Christ’s coming to His baptism, John His coming a second time subsequent to His baptism, as appears from what follows: I saw the Spirit descending, &c. The Evangelists h | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the Lamb of God is innocent, and John is the lamb, must he not be innocent? But all men come of that stock of which David sings sorrowing, Behold, I was conceived in wickedness. He then alone was | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; But whereas five kinds of animals are offered in the temple, three beasts of the field, a calf, a sheep, and a goat; and two fowls of the air, a turtle dove and a pigeon; and of the sheep kind three | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is called the Lamb of God, because God the Father accepted His death for our salvation, or, in other words, because He delivered Him up to death for our sakes. For just as we say, This is the off | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But then only will sin be entirely taken away from the human race, when our corruption has been turned to a glorious incorruption. We cannot be free from sin, so long as we are held in the death of  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why does he say the sin of the world, not sins? Because he wished to express sin universally: just as we say commonly, that man was cast out of paradise; meaning the whole human race. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or by the sin of the world is meant original sin, which is common to the whole world: which original sin, as well as the sins of every one individually, Christ by His grace remits. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For He Who took not sin from our nature, He it is Who takes away our sin. Some say, We take away the sins of men, because we are holy; for if he, who baptizes, is not holy, how can he take away the  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; As there was a connection between the other sacrifices of the law, and the daily sacrifice of the lamb, in the same way the sacrifice of this Lamb has its reflection in the pouring out of the blood  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John having said above to those who came from the Pharisees, that there stood one among them whom they knew not, he here points Him out to the persons thus ignorant: This is He of whom I said, After | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He comes after me, because he was born after me: He is made before me, because He is preferred to me. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He explains the reason of this superiority, in what follows: For He was before me; as if his meaning was; And this is the reason of His being superior to me, though born after me, viz. that He is no | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Attend, O Arius. He said not, He was created before me, but He was before me. Let the false sect of Paul of Samosata attend. They will see that He did not derive His original existence from Mary; fo | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That He might not seem however to give His testimony from any motive of friendship or kindred, in consequence of his being related to our Lord according to the flesh, he says, I knew Him not. John c | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now when our Lord became known, it was unnecessary to prepare a way for Him; for to those who knew Him, He became His own way. And therefore John’s baptism did not last long, but only so long as to  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John having made a declaration, so astonishing to all his hearers, viz. that He, whom he pointed out, did of Himself take away the sins of the world, confirms it by a reference to the Father and the | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was not however the first occasion of Christ’s receiving the unction of the Holy Spirit: viz. Its descent upon Him at His baptism; herein He condescended to prefigure His body, the Church, wher | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We do not attribute to Christ only the possession of a real body, and say that the Holy Spirit assumed a false appearance to men’s eyes: for the Holy Spirit could no more, in consistency with His na | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Holy Ghost was made to appear visibly in two ways: as a dove, upon our Lord at His baptism; and as a flame upon His disciples, when they were met together: the former shape denoting simplicity,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said, Abode upon Him: for the Holy Spirit visits all the faithful; but on the Mediator alone does He abide for ever in a peculiar manner; never leaving the Son’s Humanity, even as He proceeds Him | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Should any however think that Christ really wanted the Holy Spirit, in the way that we do, he corrects this notion also, by informing us that the descent of the Holy Ghost took place only for the pu | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But who sent John? If we say the Father, we say true; if we say the Son, we say true. But it would be truer to say, the Father and the Son. How then knew he not Him, by Whom he was sent? For if he k | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When he said, I knew Him not, he is speaking of time past, not of the time of his baptism, when he forbade Him, saying, I have need to be baptized of You. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let us turn to the other Evangelists, who relate the matter more clearly, and we shall find most satisfactorily, that the dove descended when our Lord ascended from the water. If then the dove desce | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father having sent forth a voice proclaiming the Son, the Holy Spirit came besides, bringing the voice upon the head of Christ, in order that no one present might think that what was said of Chr | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was necessary that the Only Son of God should baptize, not an adopted son. Adopted sons are ministers of the Only Son: but though they have the ministration, the Only one alone has the power. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Many not having attended to John’s words at first, he rouses them a second time: Again the next day after John stood, and two of his disciples. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; John stood, because he had ascended that citadel of all excellences, from which no temptations could cast him down: his disciples stood with him, as stout-hearted followers of their master. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But wherefore went he not all about, preaching in every place of Judea; instead of standing near the river, waiting for His coming, that he might point Him out? Because he wished this to be done by  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Looking he said, as if signifying by his looks his love and admiration for Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John was the friend of the Bridegroom; he sought not his own glory, but bore witness to the truth. And therefore he wished not his disciples to remain with him, to the hindrance of their duty to fol | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He makes not a long discourse, having only one object before him, to bring them and join them to Christ; knowing that they would not any further need his witness. John does not however speak to his  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For He alone and singly is the Lamb without spot, without sin; not because His spots are wiped off; but because He never had a spot. He alone is the Lamb of God, for by His blood alone can men be re | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Lamb therefore he calls Him; for that He was about to give us freely His fleece, that we might make of it a wedding garment; i.e. would leave us an example of life, by which we should be warmed  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:36 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John stands in a mystical sense, the Law having ceased, and Jesus comes, bringing the grace of the Gospel, to which that same Law bears testimony. Jesus walks, to collect disciples. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The walking of Jesus has a reference to the economy of the Incarnation, by means of which He has condescended to come to us, and give us a pattern of life. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John having borne witness that Jesus was the Lamb of God, the disciples who had been hitherto with him, in obedience to his command, followed Jesus: And the two disciples heard him speak, and they f | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe; when he said, He that comes after me is made before me, and, Whose shoe’s latchet I am not worthy to unloose, he gained over none; but when he made mention of the economy, and gave his disc | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe then, that it was upon those who followed Him, that our Lord turned His face and looked upon them. Unless you by your good works follow Him, you shall never be permitted to see His face, or  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples followed behind His back, in order to see Him, and did not see His face. So He turns round, and, as it were, lowers His majesty, that they might be enabled to behold His face. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perhaps it is not without a reason, that after six testimonies John ceases to bear witness, and Jesus asks seventhly, What seek you? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And besides following Him, their questions showed their love for Christ; They said to Him, Rabbi, (which is, being interpreted, Master,) where dwell You? They call Him, Master, before they have lear | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . An avowal, befitting persons who came from hearing John’s testimony. They put themselves under Christ’s teaching, and express their desire to see the dwelling of the Son of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They do not wish to be under His teaching for a time only, but inquire where He abides; wishing an immediate initiation in the secrets of His word, and afterwards meaning often to visit Him, and obt | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or perhaps come, is an invitation to action; see, to contemplation. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ does not describe His house and situation, but brings them after Him, showing that he had already accepted them as His own. He says not, It is not the time now, tomorrow you shall hear if you | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What a blessed day and night was that! Let us too build up in our hearts within, and make Him an house, whither He may come and teach us. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And it was about the tenth hour. The Evangelist mentions the time of day purposely, as a hint both to teachers and learners, not to let time interfere with their work. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It showed a strong desire to hear Him, since even at sunset they did not turn from Him. To sensual persons the time after meals is unsuitable for any grave employment, their bodies being overloaded  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The number here signifies the law, which was composed of ten commandments. The time had come when the law was to be fulfilled by love, the Jews, who acted from fear, having been unable to fulfill it | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . One of the two which heard John speak and followed Him was Andrew, Simon Peter’s brother. Why is the other name left out? Some say, because this Evangelist himself was that other. Others, that it wa | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it would seem that the two disciples who followed Jesus were Andrew and Philip. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Andrew kept not our Lord’s words to himself; but ran in haste to his brother, to report the good tidings: He first finds his own brother Simon, and said to him, We have found the Messias, which is,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is truly to find the Lord; viz. to have fervent love for Him, together with a care for our brother’s salvation. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist does not mention what Christ said to those who followed Him; but we may infer it from what follows. Andrew declares in few words what he had learnt, discloses the power of that Master | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Messias in Hebrew, Christus in Greek, Unctus in Latin. Chrism is unction, and He had a special unction, which from Him extended to all Christians, as appears in the Psalm, God, even Your God, has an | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And therefore he said not Messias, but the Messias. Mark the obedience of Peter from the very first; he went immediately without delay, as appears from the next words: And he brought him to Jesus. N | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He beheld him not with His natural eye only, but by the insight of His Godhead discerned from eternity the simplicity and greatness of his soul, for which he was to be elevated above the whole Churc | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There was nothing very great in our Lord saying whose son he was, for our Lord knew the names of all His saints, having predestinated them before the foundation of the world. But it was a great thin | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He changed the name too to show that He was the same who done so before in the Old Testament; who had called Abram Abraham, Sarai Sarah, Jacob Israel. Many He had named from their birth, as Isaac an | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The account A here of the two disciples on the Jordan, who follow Christ (before he had gone into Galilee) in obedience to John’s testimony; viz. of Andrew bringing his brother Simon to Jesus, who g | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or perhaps He does not actually give him the name now, but only fixes beforehand what He afterwards gave him when He said, You are Peter, and upon this rock will I build My Church. And while about t | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After gaining these disciples, Christ proceeded to convert others, viz. Philip and Nathanael: The day following Jesus would go forth into Galilee. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Leaving, that is, Judea, where John was baptizing, out of respect to the Baptist, and not to appear to lower his office, so long as it continued. He was going too to call a disciple, and wished to g | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, He did not call them, before some had of their own accord joined Him: for had He invited them, before any had joined Him, perhaps they would have started back: but now having determined to  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the voice of Christ sounded not like a common voice to some, that is, the faithful, but kindled in their inmost soul the love of Him. Philip having been continually meditating on Christ, and rea | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The power of Christ appears by His gathering fruit out of a barren country. For form that Galilee, out of which there arises no prophet, He takes His most distinguished disciples. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Bethsaida means house of hunters. The Evangelist introduces the name of this place by way of allusion to the characters of Philip, Peter, and Andrew, and their future office, i.e. catching and savin | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Philip is not persuaded himself, but begins preaching to others: Philip finds Nathanael, and said to him, We have found Him of whom Moses in the Law, and the Prophets, did write, Jesus of Nazareth,  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The person to whom our Lord’s mother had been betrothed. The Christians know from the Gospel, that He was conceived and born of an undefiled mother. He adds the place too, of Nazareth. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was bred up there: the place of His birth could not have been known generally, but all knew that He was bred up in Nazareth. And Nathanael said to him, Can there any good thing come out of Nazare | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . However you may understand these words, Philip’s answer wild suit. You may read it either as affirmatory, Something good can come out of Nazareth; to which the other says, Come and see: or you may r | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who alone is absolutely holy, harmless, undefiled; of whom the prophet said, There shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a branch (Nazaraus) shall grow out of his roots. Or the wor | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nathanael knew from the Scriptures, that Christ was to come from Bethlehem, according to the prophecy of Micah, And you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, - out of you shall come a Governor, that sha | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nathanael, in difficulty as to Christ coming out of Nazareth, showed the care with which he had read the Scriptures: his not rejecting the tidings when brought him, showed his strong desire for Chri | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What means this, In whom is no guile? Had he no sin? Was no physician necessary for him? Far from it. No one was ever born, of a temper not to need the Physician. It is guile, when we say one thing, | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nathanael however, notwithstanding this praise, does not acquiesce immediately, but waits for further evidence, and asks, Whence know You me? | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He asks as man, Jesus answers as God: Jesus answered and said to him, Before that Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you: not having, beheld him as man, but as God discerning | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Has this fig tree any meaning? We read of one fig tree which was cursed, because it had only leaves, and no fruit. Again, at the creation, Adam and Eve, after sinning, made themselves aprons of fig  | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When you were under the fig tree, I saw you; i.e. when you were yet under the shade of the law, I chose you. | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nathanael remembered that he had been under the fig tree, where Christ was not present corporeally, but only by His spiritual knowledge. Hence, knowing that he had been alone, he recognized our Lord | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That our Lord then had this knowledge, had penetrated into his mind, had not blamed but praised his hesitation, proved to Nathanael that He was the true Christ: Nathanael answered and said to Him, R | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let us recollect the Old Testament account. Jacob saw in a dream a ladder reaching from earth to heaven; the Lord resting upon it, and the angels ascending and descending upon it. Lastly, Jacob hims | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 1:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Good preachers, however, who preach Christ, are as angels of God; i.e. they ascend and descend upon the Son of man; as Paul, who ascended to the third heaven, and descended so far even as to give mi | src: CAJohn.htm#1
John 2:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord being known in Galilee, they invite Him to a marriage: And the third day there was a marriage in Cana of Galilee. | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Galilee is a province; Cana a village in it. | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They invite our Lord to the marriage, not as a great person, but merely as one they knew, one of the many; for which reason the Evangelist says, And the mother of Jesus was there. As they invited th | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let the proud man blush to see the humility of God. Lo, among other things, the Son of the Virgin comes to a marriage; He who, when He was with the Father, instituted marriage. | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His condescension in coming to the marriage, and the miracle He wrought there, are, even considering them in the letter only, a strong confirmation of the a faith. Therein too are condemned the erro | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What marvel, if He went to that house to a marriage, Who came into this world to a marriage. For here He has His spouse whom He redeemed with His own blood, to whom He gave the pledge of the Spirit, | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor is it without some mysterious allusion, that the marriage is related as taking place on the third day. The first age of the world, before the giving of the Law, was enlightened by the example of | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how came it into the mother’s mind to expect so great a thing from her Son? for he had done no miracle as yet: as we read afterwards This beginning of miracles did Jesus. His real nature, howeve | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She represents here the Synagogue, which challenges Christ to perform a miracle. It was customary with the Jews to ask for miracles. Jesus said to her, Woman, what have I to do with you? | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some who derogate from the Gospel, and say that Jesus was not born of the Virgin Mary, try to draw an argument for their error from this place; for, how, say they, could she be His mother to whom He | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That He greatly venerated His mother, we know from St. Luke, who tells us that He was subject unto His parents. For where parents throw no obstacle in the way of God’s commands, it is our duty to be | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To mark a distinction between His Godhead and manhood, that according to His manhood He was inferior and subject, but according to His Godhead supreme, He said, Woman, what have I to do with you? | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And for another reason, viz. to prevent any suspicion attaching to His miracles: for these it was proper should be asked for by those who wanted them, not by His mother. He wished to show them that  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it was because our Lord as God had not a mother, though as man He had, and the miracle He was about to work was the act of His Divinity, not of human infirmity. When therefore His mother demanded | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Although He had said, Mine hour is not yet come, He afterwards did what His mother told Him, in order to show plainly, that He was not under subjection to the hour. For if He was, how could He have  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; As if she said, Though He appear to refuse, He will do it nevertheless. She knew His pity and mercifulness. And there were set there six waterpots of stone, after the manner of the purifying of the  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Vessels to hold water were there, after the manner of the purifying of Jews. Among other traditions of the Pharisees, they observed frequent washings | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Palestine being a dry country, with few fountains or wells, they used to fill waterpots with water, to prevent the necessity of going to the river, if they were unclean, and to have materials for wash | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A firkin is a certain measure; as urn, amphora, and the like. Metron is the Greek for measure: whence metreta. Two or three, is not to be taken to mean some holding two, others three, but the same v | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did He not world the miracle before they had filled the waterpots, which would have been much more wonderful; inasmuch as it is one thing to change the quality of some existing substance, an | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Triclinium is a circle of three couches, cline signifying couch: the ancients used to recline upon couches. And the Architriclinus is the one at the head of the Triclinium, i.e. the chief of the | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Or thus; It might be said that the guests were drunken, and could not, in the confusion of their senses, tell whether it were water or wine. But this objection could not be brought against the attenda | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Water is poured into the waterpots; wine is drawn out into the chalices; the senses of the drawer out agree not with the knowledge of the pourer in. The pourer in thinks that water is drawn out; the | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord wished the power of His miracles to be seen gradually; and therefore He did not reveal what He had done Himself, nor did the ruler of the feast call upon the servants to do so; (for no cred | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This miracle of our Lord’s, turning the water into wine, is no miracle to those who know that God worked it. For the Same that day made wine in the waterpots, Who every year makes wine in the vine:  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was the King of glory, and changed the elements because He was their Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He manifests His glory, as far as related to His own act; and if at the time many knew it not, yet was it afterwards to be heard and known of all. And His disciples believed in Him. It was probable  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If now for the first time they believed on Him, they were not His disciples when they came to the marriage. This however is a form of speech, such as saying that the Apostle Paul was born in Tarsus  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But see the mysteries which lie hid in that miracle of our Lord. It was necessary that all things should be fulfilled in Christ which were written of Him: those Scriptures were the water. He made th | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; At the time of our Lord’s appearing in the flesh, the sweet vinous taste of the law had been weakened by the carnal interpretations of the Pharisees. | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now if He ordered the water to be poured out, and then introduced the wine from the hidden recesses of creation, He would seem to have rejected the Old Testament. But converting, as He did, the wate | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:13 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The servants are the doctors of the New Testament, who interpret the holy Scripture to others spiritually; the ruler of the feast is some lawyer, as Nicodemus, Gamaliel, or Saul. When to the former  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord being about shortly to go up to Jerusalem, proceeded to Capernaum, that He might not take His mother and brethren every where about with Him: After this he went down to Capernaum, He, and H | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Lord our God is He, high, that He might create us; low, that He might create us anew; walking among men, suffering what was human, hiding what was divine. So He has a mother, has brethren, has d | src: CAJohn.htm#2
John 2:13 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord’s brethren are the relations of Mary and Joseph, not the sons of Mary and Joseph. For not only the blessed Virgin, but Joseph also, the witness of her chastity, abstained from all conjugal  | src: CAJohn.htm#2
