John 3:13 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Or, His descending from heaven is the source of His origin as conceived by the Spirit: Mary gave not His body its origin, though the natural qualities of her sex contributed its birth and increase.  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But you wonder that He was at once here, and in heaven. Yet such power has He given to His disciples. Hear Paul, Our conversation is in heaven. If the man Paul walked upon earth, and had his convers | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That too which seems very lofty is still unworthy of His vastness. For He is not in heaven only, but every where, and fills all things. But for the present He accommodates Himself to the weakness of | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having made mention of the gift of baptism, He proceeds to the source of it, i.e. the cross: And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He introduces the teacher of the Mosaic law, to the spiritual sense of that law; by a passage from the Old Testament history, which was intended to be a figure of His Passion, and of man’s salvation | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Many dying in the wilderness from the attack of the serpents, Moses, by commandment of the Lord, lifted up a brazen serpent and those who looked upon it were immediately healed. The lifting up of th | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See then the aptness of the figure. The figure of the serpent has the appearance of the beast, but not its poison: in the same way Christ came in the likeness of sinful flesh, being free from sin. B | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherefore He does not say, The Son of man must be suspended, but lifted up, a more honorable term, but coming near the figure. He uses the figure to show that the old dispensation is akin to the new | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As then formerly he who looked to the serpent that was lifted up, was healed of its poison, and saved from death; so now he who is conformed to the likeness of Christ’s death by faith and the grace  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe; He alludes to the Passion obscurely, in consideration to His hearer; but the fruit of the Passion He unfolds plainly; viz. that they who believe in the Crucified One should not perish. And  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But there is this difference between the figure and the reality, that the one recovered from temporal death, the other from eternal. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, Even so must the Son of man be lifted up, alluding to His death; lest His hearer should be cast down by His words, forming some human notion of Him, and thinking of His death as an evil | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; If it were only a creature given up for the sake of a creature, such a poor and insignificant loss were no great evidence of love. They must be precious things which prove our love, great things mus | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): As He said above, that the Son of man came down from heaven, not meaning that His flesh did come down from heaven, on account of the unity of person in Christ, attributing to man what belonged to God: | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Note here, that the same which he before said of the Son of man, lifted up on the cross, he repeats of the only begotten Son of God: viz. That whosoever believes in Him, &c. For the same our Maker a | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Truly through the Son of God shall the world have life; for no other cause came He into the world, except to save the world. God sent not His Son into the world to condemn the world, but that the wo | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For why is He called the Savior of the world, but because He saves the world? The physician, so far as his will is concerned, heals the sick. If the sick despises or will not observe the directions  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because however He says this, slothful men in the multitude of their sins, and excess of carelessness, abuse God’s mercy, and say, There is no hell, no punishment; God remits us all our sins. But le | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who believes in Him, and cleaves to Him as a member to the head, will not be condemned. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What did you expect Him to say of him who believed not, except that he is condemned. Yet mark His words: He that believes not is condemned already. The Judgment has not appeared, but it is already g | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the meaning is, that disbelief itself is the punishment of the impenitent: inasmuch as that is to be without light, and to be without light is of itself the greatest punishment. Or He is announci | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: In the last judgment some perish without being judged, of whom it is here said, He that believes not is condemned already. For the day of judgment does not try those who for unbelief are al | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then gives the reason why he who believes not is condemned, viz. because he believes not in the name of the only begotten Son of God. For in this name alone is there salvation. God has not many s | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Where then do we place baptized children? Amongst those who believe? This is acquired for them by the virtue of the Sacrament, and the pledges of the sponsors. And by this same rule we reckon those  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here is the reason why men believed not, and why they are justly condemned; This is the condemnation, that light is come into the world. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, So far from their having sought for it, or labored to find it, light itself has come to them, and they have refused to admit it; Men loved darkness rather than light, Thus He leaves t | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He calls Himself the light, whereof the Evangelist speaks, That was the true light; whereas sin He calls darkness. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then because it seemed incredible that man should prefer light to darkness, he gives the reason of the infatuation, viz. that their deeds were evil. And indeed had He come to Judgment, there had bee | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Every one that does evil, hates the light; i.e. he who is resolved to sin, who delights in sin, hates the light, which detects his sin. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because they dislike being deceived, and like to deceive, they love light for discovering herself, and hate her for discovering them. Wherefore it shall be their punishment, that she shall manifest  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . No one reproves a Pagan, because his own practice agrees with the character of his gods; his life is in accordance with his doctrines. But a Christian who lives in wickedness all must condemn. If th | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Morally too they love darkness rather than light, who when their preachers tell them their duty, assail them with calumny. But he that does truth comets to the light, that his deeds may be made mani | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say this of those who are brought up under the Gospel, but of those who are converted to the true faith from Paganism or Judaism. He shows that no one will leave a false religion for the | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls the works of him who comes to the light, wrought in God; meaning that his justification is attributable not to his own merits) but to God’s grace. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if God has discovered all men’s works to be evil, how is it that any have done the truth, and come to the light, i.e. to Christ? Now what He said is, that they loved darkness rather than light;  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nothing is more open than truth, nothing bolder; it neither seeks concealment, or avoids danger, or fears the snare, or cares for popularity. It is subject to no human weakness. Our Lord went up to  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; After these things, is not immediately after His dispute with Nicodemus, which took place at Jerusalem; but on His return to Jerusalem after some. time spent in Galilee. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By Judea are meant those who confess, whom Christ visits; for wherever there is confession of sins, or the praise of God, thither comes Christ and His disciples, i. e. His doctrine and enlightenment | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the Evangelist says afterwards, that Jesus baptized not but His disciples, it is evident that he means the same here, i.e. that the disciples only baptized. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord did not baptize with the baptism wherewith He had been baptized; for He was baptized by a servant, as a lesson of humility to us, and in order to bring us to the Lord’s baptism, i.e. His ow | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; John still continues baptizing, though Christ has begun; for the shadow remains still, nor must the forerunner cease, till the truth is manifested. And John also was baptizing in Ænon, near to Salim | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Jerome, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; It matters not whether it is called Salem, or Salim; since the Jews very rarely use vowels in the middle of words; and the same words are pronounced with different vowels and accents, by different r | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; The same kind of benefit which catechumens receive from instruction before they are baptized, the same did John’s baptism convey before Christ’s. As John preached repentance, announced Christ’s bapt | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Notwithstanding the disciples of Jesus baptized, John did not leave off till his imprisonment; as the Evangelist’s language intimates, For John was not yet cast into prison. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He evidently here is relating what Christ did before John’s imprisonment; a part which has been passed over by the rest, who commence after John’s imprisonment. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did John baptize? | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because it was necessary that our Lord should be baptized. And why was it necessary that our Lord should be baptized? That no one might ever think himself at liberty to despise baptism. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , But why did he go on baptizing now? Because, had he left off, it might have been attributed to envy or anger: whereas, continuing to baptize, he got no glory for himself, but sent hearers to Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews then asserted Christ to be the greater person, and His baptism necessary to be received. But John’s disciples did not understand so much, and defended John’s baptism. At last they come to J | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Meaning, He, Whom you baptized, baptizes. They did not say expressly, Whom you baptized, for they did not wish to be reminded of the voice from heaven, but, He Who was with you, i.e. Who was in the  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Meaning, Passing by you, all men run to the baptism of Him Whom you baptized. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John, on this question being raised, does not rebuke his disciples, for fear they might separate, and turn to some other school, but replies gently, John answered and said, A man can receive nothing | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or perhaps John is speaking here of himself: I am a mere man, and have received all from heaven, and therefore think not that, because it has been given me to be somewhat, I am so foolish as to spea | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And see; the very argument by which they thought to have overthrown Christ, To whom you bare witness, he turns against them; You yourselves bear me witness, that I said, I am not the Christ; as if h | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; Who are you then, since you are not the Christ, and who is He to Whom you bear witness? John replies, He is the Bridegroom; I am the friend of the Bridegroom, sent to prepare the Bride for His appro | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ is the spouse of every soul; the wedlock, wherein they are joined, is baptism; the place of that wedlock is the Church; the pledge of it, remission of sins, and the fellowship of the Holy Gho | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; His Bride therefore our Lord committed to His friend, i.e. the order of preachers, who should be jealous of her, not for themselves, but for Christ; The friend of the Bridegroom which stands and hea | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, She is not My spouse. But do you therefore not rejoice in the marriage? Yes, I rejoice, he said, because I am the friend of the Bridegroom. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how does he who said above, Whose shoe’s latchet I am not worthy to unloose, call himself a friend? As an expression not of equality, but of excess of joy: (for the friend of the Bridegroom is a | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But wherefore does he stand? Because he fails not, by reason of his humility. A sure ground this to stand upon, Whose shoe’s latchet I am not worthy to unloose. Again; He stands, and hears Him. So t | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; The expression, which stands, is not without meaning, but indicates that his part is now over, and that for the future he must stand and listen. This is a transition from the parable to the | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For which cause I rejoice now, that all men follow Him. For had the bride, i.e. the people, not come forth to meet the Bridegroom, then I, as the friend of the Bridegroom, should have grieved. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; This my joy is fulfilled, i.e. my joy at hearing the Bridegroom’s voice. I have my gift; I claim no more, lest I lose that which I have received. He who would rejoice in himself, has sorrow | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; He rejoices at hearing the Bridegroom’s voice, who knows that he should not rejoice in his own wisdom, but in the wisdom which God gives him. Whoever in his good works seeks not his own glory, or pr | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He next dismisses the motions of envy, not only as regards the present, but also the future, saying, He must increase, but I must decrease: as if he said, My office has ceased, and is ended; but His | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What means this, He must increase? God neither increases, nor decreases. And John and Jesus, according to the flesh, were of the same age: for the six months’ difference between them is of no conseq | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; As, on the sun rising, the light of the other heavenly bodies seems to be extinguished, though in reality it is only obscured by the greater light: thus the forerunner is said to decrease;  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the worm gnaws wood, and rusts iron, so vainglory destroys the soul that cherishes it. But it is a most obstinate fault. John with all his arguments can hardly subdue it in his disciples: for aft | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ comes from above, as descending from the Father; and is above all, as being elected in preference to all. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, comes from above; i.e. from the height of that human nature which was before the sin of the first man. For it was that human nature which the Word of God assumed: He did not take upon Him man’s  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And yet he was not altogether of the earth; for he had a soul, and partook of a spirit, which was not of the earth. What means he then by saying that he is of the earth? Only to express his own wort | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, speaks of all the earth, he said of the man, i.e. of himself, so far as he speaks merely humanly. If he says ought divine, he is enlightened by God to say it: as said the Apostle; Yet not I, but | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having corrected the bad feeling of his disciples, he comes to discourse more deeply upon Christ. Before this it would have been useless to reveal the truths which could not yet gain a place in thei | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, from the Father. He is above all in two ways; first, in respect of His humanity, which was that of man before he sinned: secondly, in respect of the loftiness of the Father, to whom He is e | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But after this, high and solemn mention of Christ, his tone lowers: And what he has seen and heard, that he testifies. As our senses are our surest channels of knowledge, and teachers are most depen | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When you hear then, that Christ speaks what He saw and heard from the Father, do not suppose that He needs to be taught by the Father; but only that that knowledge, which He has naturally, is from t | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what is it, w which the Son has heard from the Father? Has He heard the word of the Father? Yes, but He is the Word of the Father. When you conceives a word, wherewith to name a thing, the very, | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, And what he has seen and beard, that he testifies, to prevent any from supposing, that what he said was false, because only a few for the present believed, he adds, And no man receives  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; There is a people reserved for the wrath of God, and to be condemned with the devil; of whom none receives the testimony of Christ. And others there are ordained to eternal life. Mark how m | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. has shown that God is true. This is to alarm them: for it is as much as saying, no one can disbelieve Christ without convicting God, Who sent Him, of falsehood: inasmuch as He speaks nothing bu | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, Has put to his seal, i.e. has put a seal on his heart, for a singular and special token, that this is the true God, Who suffered for the salvation of mankind. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is it, that God is true, except that God is true, and every man a liar? For no man can say what truth is, till he is enlightened by Him who cannot lie. God then is true, and Christ is God. Woul | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By Spirit here is meant the operation of the Holy Spirit. He wishes to show that all of us have received the operation of the Spirit by measure, but that Christ contains within Himself the whole ope | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said of the Son, God gives not the Spirit by measure to Him; he adds, The Father loves the Son, and farther adds, and has given all things into His hand; in order to show that the Father love | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father then has given all things to the Son in respect of His divinity; of right, not of grace. Or; He has given all things into His hand, in respect of His humanity: inasmuch as He is made Lord | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because all things are in His hand, the life everlasting is too: and therefore it follows, He that believes on the Son has everlasting life. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must understand here not a faith in words only, but a faith which is developed in works. | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He means not here, that to believe on the Son is sufficient to gain everlasting life, for elsewhere He says, Nor every one that said to Me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven. And th | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 3:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor does He say, The wrath of God comes to him, but, abides on him. For all who are born, are under the wrath of God, which the first Adam incurred. The Son of God came without sin, and was clothed  | src: CAJohn.htm#3
John 4:6 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist, after relating how John checked the envy of his disciples, on the success of Christ’s teaching, comes next to the envy of the Pharisees, and Christ’s retreat front the them. When the | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Truly had the Pharisees’ knowledge that our Lord was making more disciples, and baptizing more than John, been such as to lead them heartily to follow Him, He would not have left Judea, but would ha | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did it too to pacify the envy of men, and perhaps to avoid bringing the dispensation of the incarnation into suspicion. For had he been taken and escaped, the reality of His flesh would have been | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may perplex you, perhaps, to be told that Jesus baptized more than John, and then immediately after, Though Jesus Himself baptized not. What? Is there a mistake made, and then corrected? | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ Himself did not baptize, but those who reported the fact, in order to raise the envy of their hearers, so represented it as to appear that Christ Himself baptized. The reason why He baptized  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, both are true; for Jesus both baptized, and baptized not. He baptized, in that He cleansed: He baptized not, in that He dipped not. The disciples supplied the ministry of the body, He the aid of | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The question is often asked, whether the Holy Ghost was given by the baptism of the disciples; when below it is said, The Holy Ghost was not yet given, because Jesus was not yet glorified. We reply, | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But we must believe that the disciples of Christ were already baptized themselves, either with John’s baptism, or, as is more probable, with Christ’s. For He who had stooped to the humble service of | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ on withdrawing from Judea, joined those whom He was with before, as we react next, And departed again into Galilee. As the Apostles, when they were expelled by the Jews, went to the Gentiles, | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He must needs pass through Samaria; because that country lay between Judea and Galilee. Samaria was the principal city of a province of Palestine, and gave its name to the whole district connected w | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was the place where Simeon and Levi made a great slaughter for Dinah. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But after the sons of Jacob had desolated the city, by the slaughter of the Sychemites, Jacob annexed it to the portion of his son Joseph as we read in Genesis, I have given to you one portion above | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was a well. Every, well is a spring, but every spring is not a well. Any water that rises from the ground, and can be drawn for use, is a spring: but where it is ready at hand, and on the surface | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why does the Evangelist make mention of the parcel of ground, and the well? First, to explain what the woman says, Our father Jacob gave us this well; secondly, to remind you that what the Patri | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ prefers labor and exercise to ease and luxury, and therefore travels to Samaria, not in a carriage but on foot; until at last the exertion of the journey fatigues Him; a lesson to us, that so | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus, we see, is strong and weak: strong, because in the beginning was the Word; weak, because the Word was made flesh. Jesus thus weak, being wearied with his journey, sat on the well. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, not on a seat, or a couch, but on the first place He saw - upon the ground. He sat down because He was wearied, and to wait for the disciples. The coolness of the well would be refresh | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He mentions our Lord’s sitting and resting from His journey, that none might blame Him for going to Samaria Himself, after He had forbidden the disciples going. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord left Judea also mystically, i.e. He left the unbelief of those who condemned Him, and by His Apostles, went into Galilee, i.e. into the fickleness of the world; thus teaching His disciples  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His journey, is His assumption of the flesh for our sake. For whither does He go, Who is every where present? What is this, except that it was necessary for Him, in order to come to us, to take upon | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At the sixth hour then our Lord comes to the well. The black abyss of the well, methinks, represents the lowest parts of this universe, i.e. the earth, to which Jesus came at the sixth hour, that is | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That this conversation might not appear a violation of His own injunctions against talking to the Samaritans, the Evangelist explains how it arose; viz. for He did not come with the intention before | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman here is the type of the Church, not yet justified, but just about to be. And it is a part of the resemblance, that she comes from a foreign people. The Samaritans were foreigners, though t | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The argument with the woman arises naturally from the occasion: Jesus says to her, Give me to drink. As man. the labor and heat He had undergone had made Him thirsty. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus also thirsted after that woman’s faith? At He thirsts for their faith, for whom He shed His blood. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This shows us too not only our Lord’s strength and endurance as a traveler, but also his carelessness about food; for his disciples did not carry about food with them, since it follows, His disciple | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews would not even use their vessels. So it would astonish the woman to hear a Jew ask to drink out of her vessel; a thing so contrary to Jewish rule. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did Christ ask what the law allowed not? It is no answer to say that He knew she would not give it, for in that case, He clearly ought not to have asked for it. Rather His very reason for as | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who asked to drink, however, out of the woman’s vessel, thirsted for the woman’s faith: Jesus answered and said unto her, If you knew the gift of God, or Who it is that says to you, Give Me to dr | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it is as it were a doctrine, that no one receives a divine gift, who seeks not for it. Even the Savior Himself is commanded by the Father to ask, that He may give it Him, as we read, Require of  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He lets her know that it was not the water, which she meant, that He asked for; but that knowing her faith, He wished to satisfy her thirst, by giving her the Holy Spirit. For so must we interpret t | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Living water is that which comes out of a spring, in distinction to what is collected in ponds and cisterns from the rain. If spring water too becomes stagnant, i.e. collects into some spot, where i | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In Scripture the grace of the Holy Spirit is sometimes called fire, sometimes water, which shows that these words are expressive not of its substance but of its action. The metaphor of fire conveys  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The grace of the Holy Spirit then He calls living water; i.e. life-giving, refreshing, stirring. For the grace of the Holy Spirit is ever stirring him who does good works, directing the risings of h | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These words raised the woman’s notions of our Lord, and make her think Him no common person. She addresses Him reverentially by the title of Lord; The woman says to Him, Lord, you have nothing to dr | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She understands the living water to be the water in the well; and therefore says, You wish to give me living water; but You have nothing to draw with as I have: You can not then give me this living  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if she said, You can not say that Jacob gave us this spring, and used another himself; for he and they that were with him drank thereof; which would not have been done, had he had another better  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The addition, and his cattle, shows the abundance of the water; as if she said, Not only is the water sweet, so that Jacob and his sons drank of it, but so abundant, that it satisfied the vast multi | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): See how she thrusts herself upon the Jewish stock. The Samaritans claimed Abraham as their ancestor, on the ground of his having come from Chaldea; and called Jacob their father, as being Abraham’s gr | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:12 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or she calls Jacob their father, because she lived under the Mosaic law, and possessed the farm which Jacob gave to his son Joseph. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the mystical sense, Jacob’s well is the Scriptures. The learned then drink like Jacob and his sons; the simple and uneducated, like Jacob’s cattle. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To the woman’s question, Are you greater than our father Jacob? He does not reply, I am greater, lest He should seem to boast; but His answer implies it; Jesus answered and said to her, Whosoever dr | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which is true indeed both of material water, and of that of which it is the type. For the water in the well is the pleasure of the world, that abode of darkness. Men draw it with the waterpot of the | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The excellence of this water; viz. that he that drinks of it never thirsts, He explains in what follows, But the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlas | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the water which I give him is ever multiplying. The saints receive through grace the seed and principle of good; but they themselves make it grow by their own cultivation. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See how the woman is led by degrees to the highest doctrine. First, she thought He was some lax Jew. Then hearing of the living water, she thought it meant material water. Afterwards she understands | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; The woman as yet understands Him of the flesh only. She is delighted to be relieved for ever from thirst, and takes this promise of our Lord’s in a carnal sense. For God had once granted to | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman then being, urgent in asking for the promised water, Jesus says to her, Go call your husband; to show that he too ought to have a share in these things. But she was in a hurry to receive t | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Understand, that the woman had not a lawful husband, but had formed an irregular connection with some one. He tells her, you have had five husbands, in order to show her His miraculous knowledge. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . May not Jacob’s well signify mystically the letter of Scripture; the water of Jesus, that which is above the letter, which all are not allowed to penetrate into? That which is written was dictated b | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The five husbands some interpret to be the five books which were given by Moses. And the words, He whom thou now have is not your husband, they understand as spoken by our Lord of Himself; as if He  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus seeing that the woman did not understand, and wishing to enlighten her, says, Call your husband; i.e. apply your understanding. For when the life is well ordered, the understanding governs the | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And what more proper place than Jacob’s well, for exposing the unlawful husband, i.e. the perverse law? For the Samaritan woman is meant to figure to us a soul, that has subjected itself to a kind o | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman is not offended at Christ’s rebuke. She does not leave Him, and go away. Far from it: her admiration for Him is raised: The woman said to Him, Sir, I perceive that you are a Prophet: as if | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The husband was beginning to come to her, though He had not yet fully come. She thought our Lord a prophet, and He was a prophet: for He says of Himself, A prophet is not without honor, save in his  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And having come to this belief she asks no questions relating to this life, the health or sickness of the body: she is not troubled about thirst, she is eager for doctrine. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And she begins inquiries on a subject that perplexed her; Our fathers worshipped in this mountain; and you say that in Jerusalem is the place where men ought to worship. This was a great dispute bet | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By, our fathers, she means Abraham, who is said to have offered up Isaac here. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; The Samaritans regarded Mount Gerizim, near which Jacob dwelt, as sacred, and worshipped upon it; while the sacred place of the Jews was Mount Sion, God’s own choice. The Jews being the peo | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ however does not solve this question immediately, but leads the woman to higher things, of which He had not spoken till she acknowledged Him to be a prophet, and therefore listened with a mor | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Believe Me, our Lord says with fitness, as the husband is now present. For now there is one in thee that believes, you have begun to be present in the understanding, but if you will not believe, sur | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In saying, the hour comes, He refers to the Gospel dispensation, which was now approaching; under which the shadows of types were to withdraw, and the pure light of truth was to enlighten the minds  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There was no necessity for Christ to show why the fathers worshipped in the mountain, and the Jews in Jerusalem. He therefore was silent on that question; but nevertheless asserted the religious sup | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You, literally refers to the Samaritans, but mystically, to all who understand the Scriptures in an heretical sense. We again literally means the Jews, but mystically, I the Word, and all who confor | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Samaritans worshipped they knew not what, a local, a partial God, as they imagined, of whom they had the same notion that they had of their idols. And therefore they mingled the worship of God w | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is saying much for the Jews, to declare in their name, We worship what we know. But He does not spear; for the reprobate Jews, but for that party from whom the Apostles and the Prophets came. Suc | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jewish worship then was far higher than the Samaritan; but even it shall be abolished; The hour comes, and now is, when the true worshipers shall worship the Father in spirit and in truth. He sa | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or by saying, true, he excludes the Jews together with the Samaritans. For the Jews, though better than the Samaritans, were yet as much inferior to those who were to succeed them, as the type is to | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Twice it is said, The hour comes, and the first time without the addition, and now is. The first seems to allude to that purely spiritual worship which is suited only to a state of perfection; the s | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He speaks here of the Church; wherein there is true worship, and such as becomes God; and therefore adds, For the Father seeks such to worship Him. For though formerly He willed that mankind should  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if the Father seeks, He seeks through Jesus, Who came to seek and to save that which was lost, and to teach men what true worship was. God is a Spirit; i.e. He constitutes our real life, just as | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it signifies that God is incorporeal; and that therefore He ought to be worshipped not with the body, but with the soul, by the offering up a pure mind, i.e. that they who worship Him, must worsh | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, by saying that God being a Spirit ought to be worshipped in spirit, He indicates the freedom and knowledge of the worshipers, and the uncircumscribed nature of the worship: according to the sayi | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And that we are to worship in truth, means that whereas the former ordinances were typical; that is to say, circumcision, burnt offerings, and sacrifices; now, on the contrary, every thing is real. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, because many think that they worship God in the spirit, i.e. with the mind, who yet held heretical doctrines concerning Him, for this reason He adds, and in truth. May not the words too refer to | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O for a mountain to pray on, you cry, high and inaccessible, that I may be nearer to God, and God may hear me better, for He dwells on high. Yes, God dwells on high, but He has respect to the humble | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman was struck with astonishment at the loftiness of His teaching, as her words show: The woman said to Him, I know that Messias comes, which is called Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Unctus in Latin, Christ in Greek, in the Hebrew Messias. She knew then who could teach her, but did not know Who was teaching her. When He is come, He will tell us all things: as if she said, The Je | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what reason had the Samaritans for expecting Christ’s coming? They acknowledged the books of Moses, which foretold it. Jacob prophesies of Christ, The scepter shall not depart from Judah, nor a  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It should be known, that as Christ rose out of the Jews, not only declaring but proving Himself to be Christ; so among the Samaritans there arose one Dositheus by name, who asserted that he was the  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is a confirmation to discerning minds that the five senses were what were signified by the five husbands, to find the woman making five carnal answers, and then mentioning the name of Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ now reveals Himself to the woman: Jesus said to her, I that speak to you am He. Had He told the woman this to begin with, it would have appeared vanity. Now, having gradually awakened her to  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples arrive opportunely, and when the teaching is finished: And upon this came His disciples, and marveled that He talked with the woman. They marveled at the exceeding kindness and humilit | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who came to seek that which was lost, sought the lost one. This was what they marveled at: they marveled at His goodness; they did not suspect evil. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But notwithstanding their wonder, they asked Him no questions, No man said, What seek You? or, Why talk you with her? So careful were they to observe the rank of disciples, so great was their awe an | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman is almost turned into an Apostle. So forcible are His words, that she leaves her waterpot to go to the city, and tell her townsmen of them. The woman then left her waterpot, i.e. gave up l | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hydria answers to our word aquarium; hydor being Greek for water. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the Apostles, on being called, left their nets, so does she leave her waterpot, to do the work of an Evangelist, by calling not one person, but a whole city: She went her way into the city, and s | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She calls them together to see a man, whose words were deeper than man’s. She had had five husbands, and then was living with the sixth, not a lawful husband. But now she gives him up for a seventh, | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She was not prevented by shame-facedness from spreading about what had been said to her. For the soul, when it is once kindled by the divine flame, regards neither glory, nor shame, nor any other ea | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is only by degrees, however, that she comes to the preaching of Christ. First she calls Him a man, not Christ; for fear those who heard her might be angry, and refuse to come. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She then neither openly preaches Christ, nor wholly omits Him, but says, Is not this the Christ? This wakened their attention, Then they went out of the city, and came to Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The circumstance of the woman’s leaving her waterpot on going away, must not be overlooked. For the waterpot signifies the love of this world,) concupiscence, by which men from the dark depth, of wh | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She cast away therefore concupiscence, and hastened to proclaim the truth. Let those who wish to preach the Gospel, learn, that they should first leave their waterpots at the well. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman having become a vessel of wholesome discipline, lays aside as contemptible her former tastes and desires. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His disciples had gone to buy food, and had returned. They offered Christ some: In the mean while His disciples prayed Him, saying, Master, eat. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They all ask Him at once, Him so fatigued with the journey and heat. This is not impatience in them, but simply love, and tenderness to their Master. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They think the pre sent time convenient for dining; it being after the departure of the woman to the city, and before the coming of the Samaritans; so that they sit at meat by themselves. This expla | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, knowing that the woman of Samaria was bringing the whole town out to Him, tells His disciples, I have meat that you know not of: | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The salvation of men He calls His food, showing His great desire that we should be saved. As food is an object of desire to us, so was the salvation of men to Him. Observe, He does not express Himse | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That you know not of; i.e. know not that I call the salvation of men food; or, know not that the Samaritans are about to believe and be saved. The disciples however were in perplexity: Therefore sai | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What wonder that the woman did not understand about the water? Lo, the disciples do not understand about the meat. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They show, as usual, the honor and reverence in which they hold their Master, by talking among themselves, and not presuming to question Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From the question of the disciples, Has any man brought Him ought to eat, we may infer that our Lord was accustomed to receive food from others, when it was offered Him: not that He who gives food t | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord heard His doubting disciples, and answered them as disciples, i.e. plainly and expressly, not circuitously, as He answered the women; Jesus said to them, My meat is to do the will of Him th | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Fit meat for the Son of God, who was so obedient to the Father, that in Him was the t same will that was in the Father: not two wills, but one will in both. The Son is capable of first accomplishing | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He finished the work of God, i.e. man, He, the Son of God, finished it by exhibiting our nature in Himself without sin, perfect and uncorrupt. He finished also the work of God, i.e. the Law, (for Ch | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The matter of spiritual drink and living water being explained, the subject of meat follows. Jesus had asked the woman of Samaria, and she could give Him none good enough. Then came the disciples, h | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is the will of the Father He now proceeds to explain: Say you not, There are yet four months, and then comes harvest? | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now you are expecting a material harvest. But I say to you, that a spiritual harvest is at hand: lift up your eyes, and look on the fields; for they are white already to harvest. He alludes to the S | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He leads them, as his custom is, from low things to high. Fields and harvest here express the great number of souls, which are ready to receive the word. The eyes are both spiritual, and bodily ones | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was intent now on beginning the work, and hastened to send laborers: And he that reaps receives wages, and gathers fruit to life eternal, that both he that sows and he that reaps may rejoice toge | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Again He distinguishes earthly from heavenly things, for as above He said of the water, that he who drank of it should never thirst, so here He says, He that reaps gathers fruit to life eternal; add | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Apostles and Prophets had different labors, corresponding to the difference of times; but both will attain to like joy, and receive together their wages, even eternal life. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He confirms what He says by a proverb, And herein is that saying true, one sows and another reaps, i.e. one party has the labor, and another reaps the fruit. The saying is especially applicable here | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then He sent reapers, no sowers. The reapers went where the Prophets had preached. Read the account of their labors: they all contain prophecy of Christ. And the harvest was gathered on that occa | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I sent you to reap that whereon you bestowed no labor, i.e. I have reserved you for a favorable time, in which the labor is less, the enjoyment greater. The more laborious part of the work was laid  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How can we consistently give an allegorical meaning to the words, Lift up your eyes, &c. and only a literal one to the words, There are yet four months, and then comes harvest? The same principle of | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): After this conversation with the disciples, Scripture returns to those who had believed on the testimony of the woman, and were come to see Jesus. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is now, as it were, harvest time, when the corn is gathered, and a whole floor soon covered with sheaves; And many of the Samaritans of that city believed on Him, for the saying of the woman whic | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is natural to ask, why our Savior stays with the Samaritans, when He had given a command to His disciples not to enter into any city of the Samaritans. But we must explain this mystically. To go  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews disbelieved in spite of miracles, while these exhibited great faith, be fore even a miracle was wrought, and when they had only heard our Lord’s words. And many more believed because of His | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . With the aid of our former observations on Jacob’s well, and the water, it wills not be difficult to see, why, when they find the true word, they leave other doctrines, i.e. the city, for a sound fa | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They were not ready yet for the third day; having no anxiety to see a miracle, as those had who supped with Jesus in Cana of Galilee. (This supper was after He had been in Cana three days.) The woma | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then they knew Christ first by report of another, afterwards by His own presence; which is still the case of those that are without the fold, and not yet Christians. Christ is announced to them b | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it is impossible that the same impression should be produced by hearing from one who has seen, and seeing one’s self; walking by sight is different from walking by faith. The Samaritans now do n | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After staying two days in Samaria, He departed into Galilee, where He resided: Now after two days He departed thence, and went into Galilee. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why then does the Evangelist say immediately, For Jesus Himself testified, that a prophet has no honor in his own country. For He would seem to have testified more to the truth, had He remained in S | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or consider this the reason that He went, not to Capernaum, but to Galilee and Cana, as appears below, His country being, I think, Capernaum. As He did not obtain honor there, hear what He says; And | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Our Lord on leaving Samaria for Galilee, explains why He was not always in Galilee: viz. because of the little honor He received there. A prophet has no honor in his own country. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The country of the prophets was Judea, and every one knows how little honor they received from the Jews, as we read, Whom of the prophets have not your fathers persecuted? One cannot but wonder at t | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But do we not see many held in admiration by their own people? We do; but we cannot argue from a few instances. If some are honored in their own country, many more are honored out of it, and familia | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord by ejecting those who sold sheep and oxen from the temple, had impressed the Galileans with a strong idea of His Majesty, and they received Him. His power was shown no less in this act, tha | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:45 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They had seen Him at Jerusalem, For they also went to the feast. Our Lord’s return has a mystical meaning, viz. that, when the Gentiles have been confirmed in the faith by the two precepts of love,  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Galileans were allowed to keep the feast at Jerusalem, where they had seen Jesus. Thus they were prepared to receive Him, when He came: otherwise they would either have rejected Him; or He, know | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . On a former occasion our Lord attended a marriage in Cana of Galilee, now He goes there to convert the people, and confirm by His presence the faith which His miracle had produced. He goes there in  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There, we are told, His disciples believed on Him. Though the house was crowded with guests, the only persons who believed in consequence of this great miracle, were His disciples. He therefore visi | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist reminds us of the miracle in order to express the praise due to the Samaritans. For the Galileans in receiving Him were influenced as well by the miracle He had wrought with them, as  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some think that this was an officer of King Herod’s; others, that he was one of Caesar’s household, then employed on some commission in Judea. It is not said that He was a Jew. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is called a nobleman, either as being of the royal family, or as having some office of government. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some think that he is the same centurion, who is mentioned in Matthew. But that he is a different person is clear from this; that the latter, when Christ wished to come to his house, entreated Him n | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Did not he who made this request believe? Mark what our Lord says; Then said Jesus to him, Except you see signs and wonders, you will Not believe. This is to charge the man either with lukewarmness, | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord would have the mind of the believer so raised above all mutable things, as not to seek even for miracles. For miracles, though sent from heaven, are, in their subject matter, mutable. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Remember what He asked for, and you will plainly see that he doubted. He asked Him to come down and see his son: The nobleman said to him, Sir, come down, ere my child die. His faith was deficient;  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And mark his earthly mind, shown in hurrying Christ along with him; as if our Lord could not raise his son after death. Indeed it is very possible that be may have asked in unbelief. For fathers oft | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord in His answer implies that He is in a certain sense where He is invited present, even when He is absent from a place. He saves by His command simply, even as by His will He created all thin | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; In the centurion there was confirmed faith and true devotion, and therefore our Lord was ready to go. But the nobleman’s faith was still imperfect, as he thought our Lord could not heal in  | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His rank appears in the fact of his servants meeting him: And as he was now going down, his servants met him, and told him, saying, your son lives. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They met him, to announce what had happened, and prevent Christ from coming, as He was no longer wanted. That the nobleman did not fully believe, is shown by what follows: Then inquired he of them a | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If he only believed when he was told that his son was well again, and had compared the hour according to his servant’s account, with the hour predicted by Christ, he did not believe when he first ma | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So, we see, faith, like the other virtues, is formed gradually, and has its beginning, growth, and maturity. His faith had its beginning, when he asked for his son’s recovery; its growth, when he be | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Samaritans believed; on the strength of His words only: that whole house believed on the strength of the miracle which had been brought in it. The Evangelist adds, This is again the second mirac | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The second miracle, he says markedly. The Jews had not come to the more perfect faith of the Samaritans, who saw no miracle. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sentence is ambiguous. Taken one way, it means that Jesus after coming to Galilee, performed two miracles, of which that of healing the nobleman’s son was the second: taken another, it means, th | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it was the seventh hour, because all remission of sins is through the sevenfold Spirit; for the number seven divided into three and four, signifies the Holy Trinity, in the four seasons of the wo | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There may be an allusion in the two journeys to the two advents of Christ in the soul, the first supplying a spiritual banquet of wine, the second taking away all remains of weakness and death. | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 4:54 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The little king stands for man generally; man not only deriving his soul from the King of the universe, but having Himself dominion over all things. His son, i.e. his mind, labors under a fever of e | src: CAJohn.htm#4
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After the miracle in Galilee, He returns to Jerusalem: After this there was a feast of the Jews, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The feast of Pentecost. Jesus always went up to Jerusalem at the time of the feasts, that it might be seen that He was not an enemy to, but an observer of, the Law. And it gave Him the opportunity o | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The pool by the sheep-market, is the place where the priest washed the animals that were going to be sacrificed. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This pool was one among many types of that baptism, which was to purge away sin. First God enjoined water for the cleansing from the filth of the body, and from those defilements, which were not rea | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was a greater act in Christ, to heal the diseases of the soul, than the sicknesses of the perishable body. But as the soul itself did not know its Restorer, as it had eyes in the flesh to discern | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not, however, proceed immediately to heal him, but first tried by conversation to bring him into a believing state of mind. Not that He required faith in the first instance, as He did from th | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Three distinct biddings. Rise, however, is not a command, but the conferring of the cure. Two commands were given upon his cure, take up your bed, and walk. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Behold the richness of the Divine Wisdom. He not only heals, but bids him carry his bed also. This was to show the cure was really miraculous, and not a mere effect of the imagination; for the man’s | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; There is a wide difference between our Lord’s mode of healing, and a physician’s. He acts by His word, and acts immediately: the other’s requires a long time for its completion. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was wonderful, but what follows more so. As yet he had no opposition to face. It is made more wonderful when we see him obeying Christ afterwards in spite of the rage and railing of the Jews: A | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They did not charge our Lord with healing on the sabbath, for He would have replied that if an ox or an ass of theirs had fallen into a pit, would not they have taken it out on the sabbath day: but  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Had he been inclined to deal treacherously, he might have said, If it is a crime, accuse Him Who commanded it, and I will lay down my bed. And he would have concealed his cure, knowing, as he did, t | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Judging on low and human notions of this miracle, it is not at all a striking display of power, and only a moderate one of goodness. Of so many, who lay sick, only one was healed; though, had He cho | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is fitly described as a sheep pool. By sheep are meant people, according to the passage, We are your people, and the sheep of your pasture. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The water then, i.e. the people, was enclosed within five porches, i.e. the five books of Moses. But those books only betrayed the impotent, and did not recover them; that is to say, the Law convict | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lastly, many kinds of impotent folk lay near the pool: the blind, i.e. those who are without the light of knowledge; the lame, i.e. those who have not strength to do what they are commanded; the wit | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then Christ came to the Jewish people, and by means of mighty works, and profitable lessons, troubled the sinners, i.e. the water, and the stirring continued till He brought on His own passion. B | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What mean the words, Arise, and walk; except that you should raise yourself from your torpor and indolence, and study to advance in good works. Take up your bed, i.e. your neighbor by which you are  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Carry him then with whom you walk, that you may come to Him with Whom you desire to abide. As yet however he wist not who Jesus was; just as we too believe in Him though we see Him not. Jesus again  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The man, when healed, did not proceed to the market place, or give himself up to pleasure or vain glory, but, which was a great mark of religion, went to the temple: Afterward Jesus finds him in the | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Lord Jesus saw him both in the crowd, and in the temple. The impotent man does not recognize Jesus in the crowd; but in the temple, being a sacred place, he does. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For; if we would know our Maker’s grace, and attain to the sight of Him, we must avoid the crowd of evil thoughts and affections, convey ourselves out of the con | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ation of the wicked, and flee to the temple; in order that we may make ourselves the temple of God, souls whom God will visit, and in whom He will deign to dwell. And (He) said to him, Behold, you are | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here we learn in the first place, that his disease was the consequence of his sins. We are apt to bear with great indifference the diseases of our souls; but, should the body suffer ever so little h | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now that the man had seen Jesus, and knew Him to be the author of his recovers, ho was not slow in preaching Him to others: The man departed, and told the, Jews that it was Jesus which had made him  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was not so insensible to the benefit, and the advice he had received, as to have any malignant aim in speaking this news. Had it been done to disparage Christ, he could have concealed the cure, a | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This announcement enraged them, And therefore did the Jews persecute Jesus, because He had done these things on the sabbath day. A plain bodily work had been done before their eyes, distinct from th | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may be said then, that the observance of the sabbath was imposed on the Jews, as the shadow of something to come; viz. that spiritual rest, which God, by the figure of His own rest promised to al | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There will be a sabbath of the world, when the six ages, i.e. the six days, as it were, of the world, have passed: then will come that rest which is promised to the saints. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The mystery of which rest the Lord Jesus Himself sealed by His burial: fore He rested in His sepulcher on the sabbath, having on the sixth day finished all His work, inasmuch as He said, It is finis | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says then, as it were, to the Jews, Why think you that I should not work on the sabbath? The sabbath day was instituted as a type of Me. You observe the works of God: by Me all things were made.  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ defended His disciples, by putting forward the example of their fellow-servant David: but He defends Himself by a reference to the Father. We may observe too that He does not defend Himself a | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. not in the secondary sense in which it is true of all of us, but as implying equality. For we all of us say to God, Our Father, Which art in heaven. And the Jews say, You are our Father. They w | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The words, My Father works hitherto, and I work, suppose Him to be equal to the Father. This being understood, it followed from the Father’s working, that the Son worked: inasmuch as the Father does | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Were He not the Son by nature, and of the same substance, this defense would be worse than the former accusation made. For no prefect could clear Himself from a transgression of the king’s law, by u | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So, the Jews understood what the Arians do not. For the Arians say that the Son is not equal to the Father, and hence sprang up that heresy which afflicts the Church. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Those however who are not well-disposed to this doctrine, do not admit that Christ made Himself equal to the Father, but only that the Jews thought He did. But let us consider what has gone before.  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist here explains why the Jews wished to kill Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And again, had it been that our Lord Himself did not mean this, but that the Jews misunderstood Him, He would not have overlooked their mistake. Nor would the Evangelist have omitted to remark upon  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews however did not understand from our Lord that he was the Son of God, but only that He was equal with God; though Christ gave this as the result of His being the Son of God. It is from not s | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He refers to the charge of violating the sabbath, brought against Him. My Father works hitherto, and I work; meaning that He had a precedent for claiming the right He did; and that what He did was i | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some who would be thought Christians, the Arian heretics, who say that the very Son of God who took our flesh upon Him, was inferior to the Father, take advantage of these words to throw discredit u | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lest then that assertion of His equality, which must belong to Him, as by Name and Nature the Son, might throw doubt upon His Nativity, He says that the Son can do nothing of Himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said: Why are you offended that I called God My Father, and that I make Myself equal with God? I am equal, but equal in such a sense as is consistent with His having begotten Me; with My be | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the wholesome order of our confession, i.e. that we believe in the Father and the Son, might remain, He shows the nature of His birth; viz. that He derived the power of acting not from au acces | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If we understand this subordination of the Son to arise from the human nature, it will follow that the Father walked first upon the water, and did all the other things which the Son did in the flesh | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet that walking of the flesh upon the sea was done by the Father through the Son. For when the flesh walked, and the Divinity of the Son guided, the Father was not absent, as the Son Himself said b | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; All things and the same, He says, to show the virtue of His nature, its being the same with God’s. That is the same nature, which can do all the same things. And as the Son does all the sam | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; That the Son can do nothing of Himself, must be understood to mean, that He can do nothing contrary to, or displeasing to, the Father. And therefore He does not say that He does nothing con | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is not a sign of failing in Him, but of His abiding in His birth from the Father. And it is as high an attribute of the Almighty that He does not change, as it is that Ho does not die. The Son  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And this is confirmed by what follows: For whatsoever be does these also do the Son likewise. For it the Father does all things by Himself, so does the Son also, if this likewise is to stand good. Y | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said that He did the same A things that the Father did, and in a like way, He adds, For the Father loves the Son, and shows Him all things that Himself does. And shows Him all things that Him | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It must not be supposed that the Only Begotten God needed such showing on account of ignorance. For the showing here is only the doctrine of the nativity; the self-existing Son, from the self-existi | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For to see the Father is to see His Son. The Father so shows all His works to the Son, that the Son sees them from the Father. For the birth of the Son is in His seeing: He sees from the same source | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:20 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor did the heavenly discourse lack the caution, to guard against our inferring from these words any difference in the nature of the Son and the Father. For He says that the works of the Father were | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But now from Him whom we called co-eternal with the Father, who saw the Father’ and existed in that He saw, we return to the things of time, And He will show him greater works than these. But if He  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Having said that the Father would show the Son greater works than these, He proceeds to describe these greater works: For as the Father raises up the dead, and quickens them, even so the Son quicken | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:23 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For to will is the free power of a nature, which by the act of choice, rests in the blessedness of perfect excellence. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But who are these dead, whom the Father and Son raise to life? He alludes to the general resurrection which is to be; not to the resurrection of those few, who were raised to life, that the rest mig | src: CAJohn.htm#5
