John 5:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He did not say, He that hears My words, and believes in Me; as they would have thought this empty boasting and arrogance. To say, Believes in Him that sent Me, was a better way of making His dis | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But who is this favored Person? Will there be any one better than the Apostle Paul, who says, We must all appear before the judgment-seat of Christ? Now judgment sometimes means punishment, sometime | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We see the lovers of this present transitory life so intent on its welfare, that when in danger of death, they will take any means to delay its approach, though they can not hope to drive it off alt | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some one might ask you, The Father quickens him who believes in Him; but what of you? do you not quicken? Observe you that the Son also quickens whom He will; Verily, verily, I say to you, The hour  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After, The hour comes, He adds, and now is; to let us know that it will not be long before it comes. For as in the future resurrection we shall be roused by hearing His voice speaking to us, so is i | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here He speaks with a reference to those whom He was about to raise from the dead: viz. the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue, the son of the widow, and Lazarus. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He means to guard against our thinking, that the being passed from death to life, refers to the future resurrection; its meaning being, that he who believes is passed: and therefore He says, Ver | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the dead, i.e. unbelievers, shall hear the voice of the Son of God, i.e. the Gospel: and they that hear, i.e. who obey, shall live, i.e. be justified, and no longer remain in unbelief. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But some one will ask, Has the Son life, whence those who believe will fire? Hear His own words: As the Father has life in Himself, so has He given to the Son to have life in Himself. Life is origin | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The heretics, driven hard by Scripture proofs, are obliged to attribute to the Son at any rate a likeness, in respect of virtue, to the Father. But they do not admit a likeness of nature, not being  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father must he understand not to have given life to the Son, who was existing without life, but so to have begotten Him, independently of time, that the life which He gave Him in begetting, was  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Living born from living, has the perfection of nativity, without the newness of nature. For there is nothing new implied in generation from living to living, the life not coming at its birth from no | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Given to the Son, then, has the meaning of, begat the Son; for He gave Him tines life, by begetting. As He gave Him being, so He gave Him to have life in Himself; so that the Son did not stand in ne | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): The likeness is perfect in all but one respect, viz. that, in point of essence, one is the Father, the other the Son. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the person of the receiver, is distinct from that of the giver: it being inconceivable that one and the same person, should give to and receive from Himself. He who lives of Himself is one perso | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father granted the Son power not only to give life, but also to execute judgment. And has given Him authority to execute judgment. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why does He dwell so constantly on these subjects; judgment, resurrection, and life? Because these are the most powerful arguments for bringing men over to the faith, and the most likely ones to | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Inasmuch as the Word was in the beginning with God, the Father gave Him to have life in Himself; but inasmuch as the Word was made flesh of the Virgin Mary, being made man, He became the So | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At the judgment will appear the form of man, that form will judge, which was judged; He will sit a Judge Who stood before the judge; He will condemn the guilty, Who was condemned innocent. For it is | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . None if the, founders of false religious sects have been able to deny the resurrection of the soul, but many have denied the resurrection of the body; and, unless You, Lord Jesus, had declared it, w | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:29 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not add, And now is, here; because this hour would be at the end of the world. Marvel not, i.e. marvel not, men will all be judged by a man. But what men? Not those only, whom He will find a | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What can be plainer? Men’s bodies are in their graves, not their souls. Above when He said, The hour comes, and added, and now is; He proceeds, When the dead shall hear the voice of the Son of God.  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We were about to ask Christ, you will judge, and the Father not judge: will not you then judge according to the Father? He anticipates us by saying, I can of Mine own Self do nothing. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, nothing that is a departure from, or that is unlike to, what the Father wishes, shall you see done by Me, but as I hear, I judge. He is only showing that it was impossible He should ever wi | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When He spoke of the resurrection of the soul, He did not say, Hear, but, See. Hear implies a command issuing from the Father. He speaks as man, who is inferior to the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As I hear, I judge, is said with reference either to His human subordination, as the Son of man, or to that immutable and simple nature of the Sonship derived from the Father; in which nature hearin | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I seek not then Mine own will, i.e. the will of the Son of man, in opposition to God: for men do their own will, not God’s, when, to do what they wish, they violate God’s commands But when they so d | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He shows that the Father’s will is not a different one from His own, but one and the same, as a ground of defense. Nor marvel if being hitherto thought no more than a mere man, He defends Himself in | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The only Son says, I seek not Mine own will: and yet men wish to do their own will. Let us do the will of the Father, Christ, and Holy Ghost: for these have one will, power, and majesty. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He now brings proof of those high declarations respecting Himself. He answers an objection: If I bear witness of Myself, My witness is not true. These are Christ’s own words. But does not Christ in  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He knew Himself that His witness of Himself was true, but in compassion to the weak and unbelieving, the Sun sought for candles, that their weak sight might not be dazzled by His full blaze. And the | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; Christ, being both God and man, He shows the proper existence of both, by sometimes speaking according to the nature he took from man, sometimes according to the majesty of the Godhead. If  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But according to the former interpretation, they might say to Him, If your witness is not true, how say You, I know that the witness of John is true? But His answer meets the objection: You sent to  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But he bore witness not to himself, but to the truth: as the friend of the truth, he bore witness to the truth, i.e. Christ. Our Lord, on His part, does not reject the witness of John, as not being  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because I do not want it. John, though he bore witness, did it not that Christ might increase, but that men might be brought to the knowledge of Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Even the witness of John was the witness of God: for what he said, God taught him. But to anticipate their asking how it appeared that God taught John, as if the Jews had objected that John’s witnes | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John was a candle lighted by Christ, the Light, burning with faith and love, shining in word and deed. He was sent before, to confound the enemies of Christ, according to the Psalm, I have ordained  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I therefore direct you to John, not because I want this testimony, but that you may: for I have greater witness than that of John, i.e. that of my works; The works which the Father hash given Me to  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That He enlightens the blind, that He opens the deaf ear, looses the mouth of the dumb, casts out devils, raises the dead; these works hear witness of Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Only-begotten God shows Himself to be the Son, on the testimony not of man only, but of His own power. The works which He does, bear witness to His being sent from the Father. Therefore the obed | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By His mission we must understand His incarnation. Lastly, He shows that God is incorporeal, and cannot be seen by the bodily eye: You have neither heard His voice at any time, nor seen His shape. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews might say, We heard the voice of the Lord at Sinai, and saw Him under the appearance of fire. If God then bears witness of You, we should know His voice. To which He replies, I have the wit | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How then says Moses, Ask - whether there has been any such thing as this great thing is: did ever people hear the voice of God, speaking out of the midst of the fire, as you have heard and seen? Isa | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it is not by the carnal ear, but by the spiritual understanding, through the grace of the Holy Spirit, that God is heard. And they did not hear the spiritual voice, because they did not love or  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it was impossible for them to declare that they had received, and obeyed God’s commands: and therefore He adds, You have not His word abiding in you; i.e. the commandments, the law, and the prop | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; they cannot have abiding in them the Word which was in the beginning, who came not to keep in mind, or fulfill in practice, that word of God which they hear. Having mentioned the testimonie | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the connection may be given thus. They might say to Him, How, if we have never heard God’s voice, has God borne witness to you? So He says, Search the Scriptures; meaning that God had borne witne | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That coming is put for believing we know, Come to Him, and be lightened. He adds, That you might have life; For, if the soul which sin dies, they were dead in soul and mind. And therefore He promise | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having made mention of John, and the witness of God, and His own works, many, who did not see H that His motive was to induce them to believe, might suspect Him of a desire for human glory, | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, I receive not honor from men: i.e. I seek not human praise; for I came not to receive carnal honor from men, but to give spiritual honor to men. I do not bring forward this testimony then, becau | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, I said this to prove that it is not from your love of God, that you persecute Me; for He bears witness to Me, by My own works, and by the Scriptures. So that, if you loved God, as you  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, For this cause came I into the world, that through Me the name of the Father might be glorified; for I attribute all to Him. As then they would not receive Him, Who came to do His Fat | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hear John, As you have heard that Antichrist shall come, even now are there many Antichrists. But what do you dread in Antichrist, except that he will exalt his own name, and despise the name of the | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here is the crowning proof of their impiety. He says, as it were, If it was the love of God that made you persecute me, you would persecute Antichrist much more: for he does not profess to be sent b | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How faulty then is the boasting temper, and that eagerness for human praise, which likes to be thought to have what it has not, and would fain be thought to have all that it has, by its own strength | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The best way of guarding against this sin, is to bring to our consciences the remembrance, that we are dust, and should ascribe all the good that we have not to ourselves, but to God. And we should  | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For I am not come to condemn, but to save. There is one that accuses you, even Moses, in whom you trust. As He had said of the Scriptures above: In them you think you have eternal life. So now of Mo | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perhaps, He says, in accommodation to our way of speaking, not because there is really any doubting in God. Moses prophesied of Christ, A Prophet shall the Lord your God raise up from among your bre | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But, in fact, the whole that Moses wrote, was written of Christ, i.e. it has reference to Him principally; whether it point to Him by figurative actions, or expression; or set forth His grace and gl | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if I He said, He has even written, and has left his books among you, as a constant memento to you, lest you forget His words. And since you believe not his writings, how can you believe My unwrit | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From this we may infer too, that he who knows the commandments against stealing, and other crimes, and neglects them, will never fulfill the more perfect and refined precepts of the Gospel. | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 5:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Indeed had they attended to His words, they ought and would have tried to learn from Him, what the things were which Moses had written of Him. But they are silent. For it is the nature of wickedness | src: CAJohn.htm#5
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As missiles rebound with great force from a hard body, and fly off in all directions, whereas a softer material retains and stops them; so violent men are only excited to greater rage by violence on | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This sea has different names, from the different places with which it is connected; the sea of Galilee, from the province; the sea of Tiberias, from the city of that name. It is called a sea, though | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He goes from place to place to try the dispositions of people, and excite a desire to hear Him: And a great multitude followed Him, because they saw His miracles which He did on them that were disea | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . viz. His giving sight to the blind, and other like miracles. And it should be understood, that all, whom He healed in body, He renewed likewise in soul. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Though favored with such teaching, they were influenced less by it, than by the miracles; a sign of their low state of belief: for Paul says of tongues, that they are for a sign, not to them that be | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The persecutions of the Jews gave Him reason for retiring, and thus setting aside the Law. The truth being now revealed, types were at an end, and He was under no obligation to keep the Jewish feast | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If we compare the accounts of the different Evangelists, we shall find very clearly, that there was an interval of a year between the beheading of John, and our Lord’s Passion. For, since Matthew sa | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor did He only sit with His disciples, but conversed with them familiarly, and gained possession of their minds. Then He looked, and saw a crowd advancing. But why did He ask Philip that question?  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . One kind of temptation leads to sin, with which God never tempts any one; and there is another kind by which faith is tried. In this sense it is said that Christ proved His disciple. This is not mea | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He asks him this question, not for His own information, but in order to show His yet unformed disciple his dullness of mind, which he could not perceive of himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or to show others it. He was not ignorant of His disciple’s heart Himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if our Lord, according to John’s account, on seeing the multitude, asked Philip, tempting him, whence they could buy food for them, it is difficult at first to see how it can be true, according  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or they are two different occasions altogether. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus tried by our Lord, Philip was found to be possessed which human notions, as appears from what follows, Philip answered Him, Two hundred pennyworth of bread is not sufficient for them, that ever | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein he shows his dullness: for, had he perfect ideas of his Creator, he would not be thus doubting His power. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The reply, which is attributed to Philip by John, Mark puts in the mouth of all the disciples, either meaning us to understand that Philip spoke for the rest, or else putting the plural number for t | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Andrew is in the same perplexity that Philip is; only he has rather higher notions of our Lord: There its a lad here which has five barley loaves and two small fishes. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Probably He had some reason in his mind for this speech. He would know of Elijah’s miracle, by which a hundred men were fed with twenty loaves. This was a great step; but here he stopped. He did not | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This passage confounds the Manicheans, who say that bread and all such things were created by an evil Deity. The Son of the good God, Jesus Christ, multiplied the loaves. Therefore they could not ha | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Andrew’s suggestion about the five loaves and two fishes, is given as coming from the disciples in general, in the other Evangelists, and the plural number is used. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And let those of us, who are given to pleasure, observe the plain and abstemious eating of those great and wonderful men. He made the men sit down before the loaves appeared, to teach us that with H | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Sit down, i.e. lie down, as the ancient custom was, which they could do, as there was much grass in the place. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. green grass. It was the time of the Passover, which was kept the first month of the spring. So the men sat down in number about five thousand. The Evangelist only counts the men following the d | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why when He is going to heal the impotent, to raise the dead, to calm the sea, does He not pray, but here does give thanks? To teach us to give thanks to God, whenever we sit down to eat. And He | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Five loaves are then set before the multitude, and broken. The broken portions pass through into the hands of those who break, that from which they are broken all the time not at all diminishing. An | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He multiplied in His hands the five loaves, just as He produces harvest out of a few grains. There was a power in the hands of Christ; and those five loaves were, as it were, seeds, not indeed commi | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe the difference between the servant and the lord. The Prophets received grace, as it were, by measure, and according to that measure performed their miracles: whereas Christ, working this by  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We learn too from this miracle, not to be pusillanimous in the greatest straits of poverty. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the multitude saw the miracle our Lord had done, they marveled; as they did not know yet that He was God. Then those men, the Evangelist adds, i.e. carnal men, whose understanding was carnal, w | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their faith being as yet weak, they only call our Lord a Prophet not knowing that He was God. But the miracle had produced considerable effect upon them, as it made them call our Lord that Prophet,  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ is a Prophet and the Lord of Prophets; as He is an Angel, and the Lord of Angels. In that He came to announce something, He was an Angel; in that He foretold the future, He was a Prophet; in  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their expression, that should come into the world, shows that they expected the arrival of some great Prophet. And this is why they say, This is of a truth that Prophet: the article being put in the | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But let us reflect a little here. Forasmuch as the Divine Substance is not visible to the eye, and the miracles of the divine government of the world, and ordering of the whole creation, are overloo | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, the sea signifies this tumultuous world. In the fullness of time, when Christ had entered the sea of our mortality by His birth, trodden it by His death, passed over it by His resurrecti | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord went up to the mountain, when He ascended to heaven, which is signified by the mountain. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His leaving the multitude below, and ascending the heights with His disciples, signifies, that lesser precepts are to be given to beginners, higher to the more matured. His refreshing the people sho | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The five barley loaves signify the old law; either because the law was given to men not as yet spiritual, but carnal, i.e. under the dominion of the five senses, (the multitude itself consisted of f | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Barley is the food of cattle and slaves: and the old law was given to slaves and cattle, i.e. to carnal men. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The two fishes again, that gave the pleasant taste to the bread, seem to signify the two authorities by which the people were governed, the Royal, viz. and the Priestly; both of which prefigure our  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, by the two fishes are meant the saying or writings of the Prophets, and the Psalmist. And whereas the number five refers to the five senses, a thousand stands for perfection. But those who striv | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The boy who had these is perhaps the Jewish people, who, as it were, carried the loaves and fishes after a servile fashion, and did not eat them. That which they carried, while shut up, was only a b | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And well is it said, But what are these among so many? The Law was of little avail, till He took it into His hand, i.e. fulfilled it, and gave it a spiritual meaning. The Law made nothing perfect. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By the act of breaking He multiplied the five loaves. The five books of Moses, when expounded by breaking, i.e. unfolding them, made many books. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord by breaking, as it were, what was hard in the Law, and opening what was shut, that time when He opened the Scriptures to the disciples after the resurrection, brought the Law out in its ful | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord’s question proved the ignorance of His disciples, i.e. the people’s ignorance of the Law. They lay on the grass, i.e. were carnally minded, rested in carnal things, for all flesh is grass.  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And what are the fragments, but the parts which the people could not eat? An intimation, that those deeper truths, which the multitude cannot take in, should be entrusted to those who are capable of | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Baskets are used for servile work. The baskets here are the Apostles and their followers, who, though despised in this present life, are within filled with the riches of spiritual sacraments. The Ap | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The multitude concluding, from so great a miracle, that He was merciful and powerful, wished to make Him a king. For men like having a merciful king to rule over them, and a powerful one to protect  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is not at all inconsistent with what we read, that He went up into a mountain apart to pray: the object of escape being quite compatible with that of prayer. Indeed our Lord teaches us here, th | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet He who feared to be made a king, was a king; not made king by men, (for He ever reigns with the Father, in that He is the Son of God,) but making men kings: which kingdom of His the Prophets had | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See what the belly can do. They care no more for the violation of the Sabbath; all their zeal for God is fled, now that their bellies are filled: Christ has become a Prophet, and they wish to enthro | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist now returns to explain why they went, and relate what happened to them while they were crossing the lake: And it was dark, he says, and Jesus was not come to them. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The mention of the time is not accidental, but meant to show the strength of their love. They did not make excuses, and say, It is evening now, and night is coming on, but in the warmth of their lov | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The way of speaking we use, when we are in doubt; about five and twenty, we say, or thirty. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And at last He appears quite unexpectedly: They see Jesus walking upon the sea, drawing nigh. He reappears after His retirement, teaching them what it is to be forsaken, and stirring them to greater | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, I am Jesus, but only I am. He trusts to their easily recognizing a c voice, which was so familiar to them, or, as is more probable, He shows that He was the same who said to Moses,  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He appeared to them in this way, to show His power; for He immediately calmed the tempest: Then they wished to receive Him into tile ship; and immediately the ship was at the land, whither they went | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe the three miracles here; the first, His walking on the sea; the second, His stilling the waves; the third, His putting them immediately on shore, which they were some distance off, when our  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Jesus does not show Himself to the crowd walking on the sea, such a miracle being too much for them to hear. Nor even to the disciples did He show Himself long, but disappeared immediately. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mark’s account does not contradict this. He says indeed that our Lord told the disciples first to enter the ship, and go before Him over the sea, while He dismissed the crowds, and that when the cro | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or take another explanation. This miracle seems to me to be a different one, from the one given in Matthew: for there they do not receive Him into the ship immediately, whereas here they do: and the | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There is a mystical meaning in our Lord’s feeding the multitude, and ascending the mountain: for thus was it prophesied of Him, So shall the con | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ation of the people come about You: for their sake therefore lift up Yourself again: i.e. that the con | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ation of the people may come about You, lift up Yourself again. But why is it fled; for they could not have detained Him against His wild? This fleeing has a meaning; viz. that His flight is above our | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When either men or devils try to terrify us, let us hear Christ saying, It is I, be not afraid, i.e. I am ever near you, God unchangeable, immovable; let not any false fears destroy your faith in Me | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This ship, however, does not carry an idle crew; they are all stout rowers; i.e. in the Church not the idle and effeminate, but the strenuous and persevering in good works, attain to the harbor of e | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, though He did not actually show Himself to the multitude walking on the sea, yet gave them the opportunity of inferring what had taken place; The day following, the people which stood on t | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Knowledge of the miracle was conveyed to them indirectly. Other ships had come to the place where they had eaten bread; in these they went after Him; However there came other boats from Tiberias, ni | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet after so great a miracle, they did not ask Him how He had passed over, or show any concern about it: as appears from what follows; And when they had found Him on the other side of the sea, they  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So He Who had fled to the mountain, mixes and converses with the multitude. Only just now they would have kept Him, and made Him king. But after the sacrament of the miracle, He begins to discourse, | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who set an example of declining praise, and earthly power, sets teachers also an example of deliverance in preaching. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Kindness and lenity are not always expedient. To the indolent or insensible disciple the spur must be applied; and this the Son of God does. For when the multitude comes with soft speeches, Rabbi, w | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, you seek Me to satisfy the flesh, not the Spirit. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After the rebuke, however, He proceeds to teach them: Labor not for the meat which perishes, but for that meat which endures to everlasting life; meaning, you seek for temporal food, whereas I only  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Bodily food only supports the flesh of the outward man, and must be taken not once for all, but daily; whereas spiritual food remains for ever, imparting perpetual fullness, and immortality. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Under the figure of food He alludes to Himself you seek Me, He said, for the sake of something else; seek Me for My own sake. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But, inasmuch much as some who wish to live in sloth, pervert this precept: Labor not, &c. it is well to notice what Paul says, Let him that stole steal no more, but rather let him labor, working wi | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As He told the woman of Samaria above, If you knew Who it is that said to you, Give me to drink, you would have asked of Him, an He would have given you living water. So He says here, Which the Son  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When, through the hand of the priest, you receive the Body of Christ, think not of the priest which you see, but of the Priest you do not see. The priest is the dispenser of this food, not the autho | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A seal throws out a perfect impression of the stamp, at the same time that it takes in that impression. This is not a perfect illustration of the Divine nativity: for sealing supposes matter, differ | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or sealed, i.e. sent Him for this purpose, viz. to bring us food; or, sealed, was revealed the Gospel by means of His witness. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To take the passage mystically: on the day following, i.e. after the ascension of Christ, the multitude standing in good works, not lying in worldly pleasures, expects Jesus to come to them. The one | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How many there are who seek Jesus, only to gain some temporary benefit. One man has a matter of business, in which he wants the assistance of the clergy; another is oppressed by a more powerful neig | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In their persons too our Lord condemns all those within the holy Church, who, when brought near to God by sacred Orders, do not seek the recompense of righteousness, but the interests of this presen | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They too seek Jesus, not for Jesus’ sake, but for something else, who ask in their prayers not for eternal, but temporal blessings. The mystical meaning is, that the conventicles of heretics are wit | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They understood that the meat, which remains to eternal life, was the work of God: and therefore they ask Him what to do to work the work of God, i.e. obtain the meat: Then said they to Him, What sh | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. By keeping what commandments shall we be able to fulfill the law of God? | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they said this, not that they might learn, and do them, but to obtain from Him another exhibition of His bounty. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ, though He saw it would not avail, yet for the good of others afterwards, answered their question; and showed them, or rather the whole world, what was the work of God: Jesus answered and sai | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, That you believe Him, but, that you believe in Him. For the devils believed Him, and did not believe in Him; and we believe Paul, but do not believe in Paul. To believe in Him is be | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To eat then that meat which endures to everlasting life, is to believe in Him. Why do you make ready your tooth and your belly? Only believe, and you have eaten already. As He called on them to beli | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nothing can be more unreasonable than their asking for another miracle, as if none had been given already. And they do not even leave the choice of the miracle to our Lord; but would oblige Him to g | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And to exalt the miracle of the manna they quote the Psalm, As it is written, He gave them bread from heaven to eat. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whereas many miracles were performed in Egypt, at the Red Sea, and in the desert, they remembered this one the best of any. Such is the force of appetite. They do not mention this miracle as the wor | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; Our Lord sets Himself above Moses, who did not dare to say that He gave the meat which perishes not. The multitude therefore remembering what Moses had done, and wishing for some greater mi | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord might have replied, that He had done miracles greater than Moses: but it was not the time for such a declaration. One thing He desired, viz. to bring them to taste the spiritual meat: then  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, That manna was the type of this food, of which I just now spoke; and which all my. miracles refer to. You like my miracles, you despise what is signified by them. This bread which God | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not to the physical world, but to men, its inhabitants. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls Himself the true bread, because the only-begotten Son of God, made man, was principally signified by the manna. For manna means literally, what is this? The Israelites were astonished at fi | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Who by the humanity, which was assumed, came down from heaven, and by the divinity, which assumed it, gives life to the world. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But this bread, being essentially life, (for He is the Son of the living Father,) in quickening all things, does but what is natural to Him to do. For as natural bread supports our weak flesh, so Ch | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not only to the Jews, but to the whole world. The multitude, however, still attached a low meaning to His words: Then said they to Him, Lord, evermore give us this bread. They say, Give us this brea | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the woman of Samaria, when our Lord told her, Whosoever drinks of this water shall never thirst, thought He meant natural water, and said, Sir, give me this water, that she might never be in want | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord now proceeds to set forth mysteries; and first speaks of His Divinity: And Jesus said to them, I am the bread of life. He does not say this of His body, for He speaks of that at the end; Th | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, I am the bread of nourishment, but of life, for, whereas all things brought death, Christ has quickened us by Himself. But the life here, is not our common life, but that which is n | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that comes to Me, i.e. that believes in Me, shall never hunger, has the same meaning as shall never thirst; both signifying that eternal society, where there is no want. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, shall never hunger or thirst, i.e. shall never be wearied of hearing the word of God, and shall never thirst as to the understanding: as though He had not the water of baptism, and the sanctific | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You desire bread from heaven: but, though you have it before you, you eat it not. This is what I told you: But I said to you, that you also have seen Me, and believe not. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, I did not say what I did to you about the bread, because I thought you would eat it, but rather to convict you of unbelief. I say, that you see Me, and believe not. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, I said to you, refers to the testimony of the Scriptures, of which He said above, They are they which testify of Me; and again, I am come in My Father’s name, and you receive Me not. That you ha | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But, because you have seen Me, and believed not, I have not therefore lost the people of God: All that the Father gives Me, shall come to Me; and him that comes to Me, I will in no wise cast out. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All, He said, absolutely, to show the fullness of the number who should believe. These are they which the Father gives the Son, when, by His secret inspiration, He makes them believe in the Son. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whomsoever therefore the Father draws to belief in Me, he, by faith, shall come to Me, that he may be joined to Me. And those, who in the steps of faith and good works, shall come to Me, I will in n | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That inner place, whence there is no casting out, is a great sanctuary, a secret chamber, where is neither weariness, or the bitterness of evil thoughts, or the cross of pain and temptation: of whic | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The expression, that the Father gives Me, shows that it is no accident whether a man believes or not, and that belief is not the work of human cogitation, but requires a revelation from on high, and | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is the reason why He does not cast out those who come to Him. For I came down from, heaven not to do Mine own will, but the will of Him that sent Me. The soul departed from God, because it was  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not that He does what He does not wish. He fulfills obediently His Father’s will, wishing also Himself to fulfill that will. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For this very reason therefore, I will not cast out Him that comes to Me; because I came not to do Mine own will. I came to teach humility, by being humble Myself. He that comes to Me, is made a mem | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They therefore who by God s unerring providence are foreknown, and predestined, called, justified, glorified, even before their new birth, or before they are born at all, are already the sons of God | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I should lose nothing; He lets them know, he does not desire his own honor, but their salvation. After these declarations, I will in no wise cast out, and I should lose nothing, He adds, But should  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See how the twofold resurrection is expressed here. He who comes to Me, shall forthwith rise again; by becoming humble, and a member of Me. But then He proceeds; But I will raise him up at the last  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews, so long as they thought to get food for their carnal eating, had no misgivings; but when this hope was taken away, then, we read, the Jews murmured at Him because He said, I am the bread w | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they were far from being fit for that heavenly bread, and did not hunger for it. For they had not that hunger of the inner man. CHYRS. It is evident that they did not yet know of His miraculous  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He took man’s flesh upon Him, but not after the manner of men; for, His Father being in heaven, He chose a mother upon earth, and was born of her without a father. The answer to the murmurers next f | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , But here the Manichees attack us, asserting that nothing is in our own power. Our Lords words however do not destroy our free agency, but only show that we need Divine assistance. For He is speaking | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now if we are drawn to Christ without our own will, we believe without our own will; the will is not exercised, but compulsion is applied. But, though a man can enter the Church involuntarily, he ca | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the Father draws to the Son, by the works which He did by Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Great indeed is the Son’s dignity; the Father draws men, and the Son raises them up. This is no division of works, but an equality of power. He then shows the way in which the Father draws. It is wr | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus, When a schoolmaster is the only one in a town, we say loosely, This man teaches all here to read; not that all learn of him, but that he teaches all who do learn. And in the same way we say | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All the men of that kingdom shall be taught of God; they shall hear nothing from men: for, though in this world what they hear with the outward ear is from men, yet what they understand is given the | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He uses the plural, In the Prophets, because all the Prophets being filled with one and the same spirit, their prophecies, though different, all tended to the same end; and with whatever any one of  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These words are not found in Joel, but something like them; Be glad then you children of Sion, and rejoice in the Lord your God, for He has given you a Teacher. And more expressly in Isaiah, And all | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . An important distinction. All men before learnt the things of God through men; now they learn them through the Only Son of God, and the Holy Spirit. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All that are taught of God come to the Son, because they have heard and learnt from the Father of the Son: wherefore He proceeds, Every man that has heard, and has learned of the Father, comes to Me | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Behold then how the Father draws; not by laying a necessity on man, but by teaching the truth. To draw, belongs to God: Every one that has heard, and has learned of the Father, comes to Me. What the | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We are all from God. That which belongs peculiarly and principally to the Son, He omits the mention of, as being unsuitable to the weakness of His hearers. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord wishes to reveal what He is; Verily, verily, I say to you, He that believes in Me, has everlasting life. As if He said; He that believes in Me has Me: but what is it to have Me? It is to ha | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The multitude being urgent for bodily food, and reminding Him of that which was given to their fathers, He tells them that the manna was only a type of that spiritual food which was now to be tasted | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls Himself the bread of life, because He constitutes one life, both present, and to come. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because they had taunted Him with the manna, He adds, Your fathers did eat manna in the wilderness, and are dead. Your fathers they are, for you are like them; murmuring sons of murmuring father | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The addition, In the wilderness, is not put in without meaning, but to remind them how short a time the manna lasted; only till the entrance into the land of promise. And because the bread which Chr | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was the bread the manna typified, this was the bread the altar typified. Both the one and the other were sacraments, differing in symbol, alike in the thing signified. Hear the Apostle, They di | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then gives them a strong reason for believing that they were given for higher privileges than their fathers. Their fathers eat manna and were dead; whereas of this bread He says, that a man may e | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But are we, who eat the bread that comes down from heaven, relieved from death? From visible and carnal death, the death of the body, we are not: we shall die, even as they died. But from spiritual  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Therefore I say, He that eats this bread, dies not: I am the living bread which came down from heaven. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By becoming incarnate, He was not then first man, and afterwards assumed Divinity, as Nestorius fables. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The manna too came down from heaven; but the manna was shadow, this is substance. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But men must be quickened by my life: If any man eat of this bread, he shall live, not only now by faith and righteousness, but for ever. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord pronounces Himself to be bread, not only in respect of that Divinity, which feeds all things, but also in respect of that human nature, which was assumed by the Word of God: And the bread,  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This bread our Lord then gave, when He delivered to His disciple the mystery of His Body and Blood, and offered Himself to God the Father on the altar of the cross. For the life of the world, i.e. n | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:51 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which I shall give: this shows His power; for it shows that He was not crucified as a servant, in subjection to the Father, but of his own accord; for though He is said to have been given up by the  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But when does flesh receive the bread which He calls His flesh? The faithful know and receive the Body of Christ, if they labor to be the body of Christ. And they become the body of Christ, if they  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews not understanding what was the bread of A peace, strove among themselves, saying, How can this man give us His flesh to eat? Whereas they who eat the bread strive not among themselves, for  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews thought that our Lord would divide His flesh into pieces, and give it them to eat: and so mistaking Him, strove. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . AS they thought it impossible that He should do as He said, i.e. give them His flesh to eat, He shows them that it was not only possible, but necessary: Then said Jesus to them, Verily, verily, I sa | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, The sense in which that bread is eaten, and the mode of eating it, you know not; but, Except you eat the flesh of the Son of man, and drink His blood, you have no life in you. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): And that this might not seem addressed to them alone, He declares universally, Whoso eats My flash, and drinks My blood, has eternal life. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And that they might not understand him to speak of this life, and make that an occasion of striving, He adds, has eternal life. This then he has not who eats not that flesh, nor drinks that blood. T | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it is not the flesh of man simply, but of God: and it makes man divine, by inebriating him, as it were, with divinity. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:54 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There are some who promise men deliverance from eternal punishment, if they are washed in Baptism and partake of Christ’s Body, whatever lives they live. The Apostle however contradicts them, where  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By this meat and drink then, He would have us understand the society of His body, and His members, which is the Church, in the predestined, and called, and justified, and glorified saints and believ | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had said above, Whoso eats My flesh and drinks My blood, has eternal life: and now to show the great difference between bodily meat and drink, and the spiritual mystery of His body and blood, Ho  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. this is no enigma, or parable, but you must really eat the body of Christ; or He means to say that the true meat was He who saved the soul. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Whereas men desire meat and drink to satisfy hunger and thirst, this effect is only really produced by that meat and drink, which makes the receivers of it immortal and incorruptible; i.e.  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, having given a promise of eternal life to those that eat Him, He says this to confirm it: He that eats My flesh, and drinks My blood, dwells in Me, and I in him. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As for those, as indeed there are many, who either eat that flesh and drink that blood hypocritically, or, who having eaten, become apostates, do they dwell in Christ, and Christ in them? Nay, but t | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is to say, such an one eats the body and drinks the blood of Christ not in the sacramental sense, but in reality. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because I live, it is manifest that he will live also: As the living Father has sent Me, and I live by the Father, even so he that eats Me, even he shall live by Me. As if He said, As the Father | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said not, As I eat the Father, and live by the Father, so he that eats Me, even he shall live by Me. For the Son does not grow better by partaking of the Father, as we do by partaking of the Son, | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Of the truth then of the body and blood of Christ, no room for doubting remains: for, by the declaration of our Lord Himself, and by the teaching of our own faith, the flesh is really flesh, and the | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That we who cannot obtain eternal life of ourselves, might live by the eating that bread, He descended from heaven: This is the bread which comes down from heaven. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls Himself the bread, because He is the origin of His own body. And lest it should be thought that the virtue and nature of the Word had given way to the flesh, He calls the bread His flesh, t | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For we do not eat God simply, God being impalpable and incorporeal; nor again, the flesh of man simply, which would not profit us. But God having taken flesh into union with Himself; that flesh is q | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And to show the wide interval between the shadow and the light, the type and the reality, He adds, Not as your fathers did eat manna, and are dead: he that eats of this bread shall live for ever. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The death here meant is death eternal. For even those who eat Christ are subject to natural death; but they live for ever, because Christ is everlasting life. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:59 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For if it was possible without harvest or fruit of the earth, or any such thing, to preserve the lives of the Israelites of old for forty years, much more will He be able to do this with that spirit | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, Capernaum, which means beautiful town, stands for the world: the synagogue, for the Jewish people. The meaning is, that our Lord has, by the mystery of the incarnation, manifested Himsel | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Such is our Lord’s discourse. The people did not perceive that it had a deep meaning or, that grace went along with it: but receiving the matter in their own way, and taking His words in a human sen | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. difficult to receive, too much for their weakness. They thought He spoke above Himself, and more loftily than He had a right to do; and so said they, Who can bear it? which was answering in fac | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And if His disciples thought that saying hard what would His enemies think? Yet it was necessary to declare a thing, which would be unintelligible to men. God’s mysteries should draw men’s attention | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When you hear, however, of His disciples murmuring, understand not those really such, but rather some who, as far as their air and behavior went, seemed to be receiving instruction from Him. For amo | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They spoke, however, so as not to be heard by Him. But He, who knew what was in them, heard within Himself: When Jesus knew within Himself that His disciples murmured at it, He said to them, Does th | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. that I said, you should eat My flesh, and drink My blood. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The revelation however of these hidden things was a mark of His Divinity: hence the meaning of what follows; And if you shall see the Son of man ascend up where He was before; supply, What will you  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, these words are an answer to their mistake. They supposed that He was going to distribute His body in bits: whereas He tells them now, that He should ascend to heaven whole and entire: What and  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Do not suppose from this that the body of Christ came down from heaven, as the heretics Marcion and Apollinarius say; but only that the Son of God and the Son of man are one and the same. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He tries to remove their difficulties in another way, as follows, It is the spirit that quickens, the flesh profits nothing: that is to say, You ought to understand My words in a spiritual sense: he | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus, the flesh profits nothing. They had under stood by His flesh, as it were, of a carcass, that was to be cut up, and sold in the shambles, not of a body animated by the spirit. Join the spiri | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the flesh does not cleanse of itself, but by the Word who assumed it: which Word, being the principle of life in all things, having taken up soul and body, cleanses the souls and bodies of those | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. are spiritual, have nothing carnal in them, produce no effects of the natural sort; not being under the dominion of that law of necessity, and order of nature established on earth. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If then you understand them spiritually, they are life and spirit to you: if carnally, even then they are life and spirit, but not to you. Our Lord declares that in eating His body, and drinking His | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having spoken of His words being taken carnally, He adds, But there are some of you that believe not. Some, He says, not including His disciples in the number. This insight shows His high nature. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says not, There are some among you who understand not; but gives the reason why they do not understand. The Prophet said, Except you believe, you shall not understand. For how can he who opposes  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To let you know that it was before these words, and not after, that the people murmured and were offended, the Evangelist adds, For Jesus knew from the beginning, who they were that believed not, an | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist wishes to show us, that He knew all things before the foundation of the world: which was a proof of His divinity. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And after distinguishing those who believed from those who did not believe, our Lord gives the reason of the unbelief of the latter, And He said, Therefore said I to you, that no man can come to Me, | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Men’s unbelief does not disturb or astonish Me: I know to whom the Father has given to come to Me. He mentions the Father, to show first that He had no eye to His own glory; secondly, | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then (our) faith is given to us: and no small gift it is. Wherefore rejoice if you believe; but be not lifted up, for what have you which you did not receive? And that this grace is given to some | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, withdrew, but went back, i.e. from being good hearers, from the belief which they once had. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Being cut off from the body, their life was gone. They were no longer in the body; they were created among the unbelieving. There went back not a few, but many after Satan, not after Christ; as the  | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it may be asked, what reason was there for speaking words to them which did not edify, but might rather have injured them? It was very useful and necessary; for this reason, they had been just n | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And perhaps this took place for our consolation; since it sometimes happens that a man says what is true, and what He says is not understood, and they which hear are offended and go. Then the man is | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord knew well the intentions of the other disciples which stayed, as to staying or going; but yet He put the question to them, in order to prove their faith, and hold it up to imitation: Then s | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was the right way to retain them. Had He praised them, they would naturally, as men do, have thought that they were conferring a favor upon Christ, by not leaving Him: by showing, as He did, th | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if he said, You cast us from You: give us another to whom we shall go, if we leave You. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A speech of the greatest love: proving that Christ was more precious to them than father or mother. And that it might not seem to be said, from thinking that there was no one whose guidance they cou | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For we believed, in order to know. Had we wished first to know, and then to; believe, we could never have been able to believe. This we believe, and know, that You are the Christ the Son of God; i.e | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peter however having said, We believe, our Lord excepts Judas from the number of those who believed: Jesus answered them, Have not I chosen you twelve, and one of you is a devil? i.e. Do not suppose | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or we must say, that He elected the eleven for one purpose, the twelfth for another: the eleven to fill the place of Apostles, and persevere in it to the end; the twelfth to the service of betraying | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was elected to be an involuntary and unconscious instrument of producing the greatest good. For as the wicked turn the good works of God to an evil use, so reversely God turns the evil works of m | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . One of you is a devil: the body is here named after its head. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 6:71 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mark the wisdom of Christ: He neither; by exposing him, makes him shameless and contentious; nor again emboldens him, by allowing him to think himself concealed. | src: CAJohn.htm#6
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the believer in Christ would have in time to, come to hide himself from persecution, that no guilt might attach to such concealment, the Head began with doing Himself, what He sanctioned in the m | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The connection of this passage admits of much taking place in the interval previously. Judea and Galilee are divisions of the province of Palestine. Judea has its name from the tribe of Judah; but i | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is not meant that our Lord could not walk among the Jews, and escape being killed; for He had this power, whenever He chose to show it: but He set the example of so doing, as an accommodation to  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is to say, He displayed the attribute both of divinity and humanity. He fled from His persecutors as man, He remained and appeared amongst them as God; being really both. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He withdrew too now to Galilee, because the hour of His passion was not yet come; and He thought it useless to stay in the midst of His enemies, when the effect would only have been to irritate them | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What the feast of tabernacles is, we read in the Scriptures. They used to make tents on the festival, like those in which they lived during their journey in the desert, after their departure from Eg | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It appears here, that a considerable time had passed since the last events. For when our Lord sat upon the mount, it was near the feast of the Passover and now it is the feast of tabernacles: so tha | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His brethren saw that He was not preparing to go to the feast: His brethren therefore said to him, Depart hence, and go into Judea. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Meaning to say, You do miracles, and only a few see them: go to the royal city, where the rulers are, that they may see Your miracles, and so you obtain praise. And as our Lord had not brought all H | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. the multitudes that follow You. They do not mean the twelve, but the others that had communication with Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When you hear of our Lord’s brethren, you must understand the kindred of Mary, not her offspring after our Lord’s birth. For as the body of our Lord once only lay in the sepulcher, and neither befor | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is striking to observe the great sincerity of the Evangelists; that they are not ashamed to mention things which appear to be to our Lord’s disadvantage, but take particular care to tell us of th | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is no contradiction to what the Apostle says, But when the fullness of time was come, God sent forth His Son. Our Lord referring here to the time not of His nativity, but of His glorification. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They gave Him advice to pursue glory, and not allow Himself to remain in concealment and obscurity; appealing altogether to worldly and secular motives. But our Lord was laying down another road to  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Or there seems to be another meaning concealed in the words; perhaps they intended to betray Him to the Jews; and therefore He says, My time is not yet come, i.e. the time of My cross and death: but | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord brings two arguments in answer to their two charges. To the charge of fear He answers, that He reproves the deeds of the world, i.e. of those who love worldly things; which He would not do, | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is to show too, that, while He does not wish to humor them, He still allows them to observe the Jewish ordinances. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He seems to say, Go you up to this feast, and seek for human glory, and enlarge your carnal pleasures, and forget heavenly things. I go not up to this feast; | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. not with you, for My time is not yet full come. It was at the next passover that He was to be crucified. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or My time, i.e. the time of My glory, is not yet come. That will be My feast day; not a day which passes and is gone, like holidays here: but one which remains for ever. Then will be festivity; joy | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord at first declares that He will not go up to the feast, (I go not up with you,) in order not to expose Himself to the rage of the Jews; and therefore we read, that, When He had said these wo | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He went up, however, not to get temporary glory, but, to teach wholesome doctrine, and remind men of the eternal feast. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He goes up, not to suffer, but to teach. And He goes up secretly; because, though He could have gone openly, and kept the violence and impetuosity of the Jews in check, as He had often done before;  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He went up in secret, because He did not seek the favor of men, and took no pleasure in pomp, and being followed about with crowds. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The mystical meaning is, that to all those carnal persons who seek human glory, the Lord remains in Galilee; the meaning of which name is, “passing over;” applying to those his members who pass from | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the meaning is, that all the ceremonial of the ancient people was the figure of what was to be; such as the feast of tabernacles. Which figure is now unveiled to us. Our Lord went up in secret, t | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then the Jews sought Him at the feast, and said, Where is He? out of hatred and enmity; for they would not call Him by His name. There was not much reverence or religion in this observance of the fe | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And there was much murmuring in the people concerning Him. A murmuring arising from disagreement. For some said, He is a good man: others said, Nay; out He seduces the people. Whoever had any spark  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The former, I think, was the opinion of the multitude, the one, viz. who pronounced Him a good man; the latter the opinion of the priests and rulers; as is strewn by their saying, He deceives the pe | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Howbeit no man spoke openly of Him, for fear of the Jews; none, that is, of those who said, He is a good man. They who said, He deceives the people, proclaimed their opinion openly enough; while the | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, the corruption is in the rulers: the common people are sound in their judgment, but have not liberty of speech, as is generally their case. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord delays His visit, in order to excite men’s attention, and goes up not the first day, but about the middle of the feast: Now about the midst of the feast Jesus went up into the temple, and t | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At the commencement of the feast, men would be attending more to the preachings of the festival itself; and afterwards would be better disposed to hear Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The feast seems, as far as we can judge, to have lasted several days. And therefore it is said, “about the middle of the feast day:” i.e. when as many days of that feast had passed, as were to come. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In going there too, He went up, not to the feast day, but to the light. They had gone to enjoy the pleasures of the festival, but Christ’s feast day was that on which by His Passion He redeemed the  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who had before concealed Himself, taught and spoke openly, and was not laid hold on. The one was intended for an example to us, the other to testify His power. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What His teaching is, the Evangelist does not say; but that it was very wonderful is shown by its effect even upon those who had accused Him of deceiving the people, who turned round and began to ad | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All, it would appear, admired, but all were not converted. Whence then the admiration? Many knew where here He was born, and how He had been educated; but had never seen Him learning letters. Yet no | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their wonder might have led them to infer, that our Lord became possessed of this learning in some divine way, and not by any human process. But they would not acknowledge this, and contented themse | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mine is not mine, appears a contradiction; why did He not say, This doctrine is not Mine? Because the doctrine of the Father being the Word of the Father, and Christ Himself being that Word, Christ  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He calls it His own, inasmuch as He taught it; not His own, inasmuch as the doctrine was of the Father. If all things however which the Father has are His, the doctrine for this very reason is Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: In one sense He calls it His, in another sense not His; according to the form of the Godhead His, according to the form of the servant not His. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Should any one however not understand this, let him hear the advice which immediately follows from our Lord: If any man will do His will, he shall know of the doctrine, whether it be of God, or whet | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is as much as to say, Put away the anger, envy, and hatred which you have towards Me, and there will be nothing to prevent your knowing, that the words which I speak are from God. Then He bring | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, I speak the truth, because My doctrine contains the truth there is no unrighteousness in Me, because I usurp not another’s glory. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who seeks his own glory is Antichrist. But our Lord set us an example of humility, in that being found in fashion as a man, He sought His Father’s glory, not His own. You, when you do good, take  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, the reason why He spoke so humbly of Himself, is to let men know, that He does not aim at glory, or power; and to accommodate Himself to their weakness, and to teach them moderation, and a  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews brought two charges against Christ; one, that He broke the sabbath; the other, that He said God was His Father, making Himself equal with God. The latter He confirmed first by showing, that | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He means to say, that if they kept the law, they would see Him pointed to in every part of it, and would not seek to kill Him, when He came. The people return an answer quite away from the subjec | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein He left us an example to take it patiently, whenever wrong censures are passed upon us, and not answer them by asserting the truth, though able to do so, but rather by some wholesome advice  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, What if you saw all My works? For all that they saw going on in the world was of His working, but they saw not Him Who made all things. But He did one thing, made a man whole on the s | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You marvel, i.e. are disturbed, are in commotion. Observe how well He argues with them from the law. He wishes to prove that this work was not a violation of the law; and shows accordingly that ther | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, you have done well to receive circumcision from Moses, not because it is of Moses, but of the fathers; for Abraham first received circumcision from the Lord. And you circumcise on the | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Circumcision was given for three reasons; first, as a sign of Abraham’s great faith; secondly, to distinguish the Jews from other nations; thirdly, that the receiving of it on the organ of virility, | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which is as much as to tell them, The breaking of the sabbath in circumcision is a keeping of the law; and in the same way I by healing on the sabbath have kept the law. You, who are not the legisla | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Circumcision also was perhaps s a type of our or Lord Himself. For what is circumcision but a robbing of the flesh, to signify the robbing the heart of its carnal lusts. And therefore it was not wit | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, however, I have done a greater work than circumcision; but only states the matter of fact, and leaves the judgment to them, saying, Judge not according to the appearance, out judge  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What our Lord here tells us to avoid, in judging by the person, is very difficult in this world not to do. His admonition to the Jews is an admonition to us as well; for every sentence which our Lor | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was said above that our Lord went up to the feast secretly, not because He feared being taken, (for He had power to prevent it,) but to show figuratively, that even in the very feast which the Je | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist adds, from Jerusalem: for there had been the greatest display of miracles, and there the people were in the worst state, seeing the strongest proofs of His divinity, and yet willing t | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So, not fully understanding Christ’s power, they supposed that it was owing to the knowledge of the rulers that He was spared: Do the rulers know indeed that this is the very Christ? | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they do not follow the opinion of the rulers, but put forth another most perverse and absurd one; Howbeit we know this Man, whence He is; but when Christ comes, no man knows whence He is. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This notion did not arise without foundation. We find indeed that the Scriptures said of Christ, He shall be called a Nazarene, and thus predicted whence He would come. And the Jews again told Herod | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By which He discloses what was in their minds. I am not, He seems to say, of the number of those who have come without reason, but He is true that sent Me; and if He is true, He has sent Me in truth | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Every man, ever born in the flesh, is in a certain sense from God. How then could He say that they were ignorant who He was, and whence He was.? Because our Lord is here referring to His own peculia | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the ignorance, He here speaks of, is the ignorance of a bad life; as Paul said, They profess that they know God, but in words they deny Him. Our Lord’s reproof is twofold: He first published what | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lastly, to show whence they could get to know Him (who had sells Him), He adds, I know Him: so if you would know Him, inquire of Me. No one knows the Father, save the Son, and he to whom the Son wil | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which is impossible for He that sent Me is true, and therefore He that is sent must be true likewise He every where attributes the knowledge of the Father to Himself, as being from the Father: thus  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I ask however does the being from Him express a work of creation, or a birth by generation? If a work of creation, then every thing which is created is from Him. And how then does not all creation k | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I am from Him, He says, i.e. as the Son from the Father: but that you see Me in the flesh is because He has sent Me. Wherein understand not a difference of nature, but the authority of a father. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His saying however, Whom you know not, irritated the Jews, who professed to have knowledge; and they sought to take Him, but no man laid hands on Him. Mark the invisible check which is kept upon the | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, because He was not so pleased; for our Lord was not born subject to fate. You must not believe this even of yourself, much less of Him by Whom you were made. And if your hour is in His will | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And many of the people believed in Him. Our Lord; brought the poor and humble to be saved. The common people, who soon saw their own infirmities, received His medicine without hesitation. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Neither had these however a sound faith; but took up a low way of speaking, after the manner of the multitude: When Christ comes, will He do more miracles than this Man has done? Their saying, When  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or they mean, If there are not to be two Christs, this is He. The rulers however, possessed with madness, not only refused to acknowledge the physician, but even wished to kill Him: The Pharisees he | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had discoursed often before, but they had never so treated Him. The praises of the multitude however now irritated them; though the transgression of the sabbath still continued to be the reason p | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not being able to take Him against His will, they sent men to hear Him teach. Teach what? Then said Jesus to them, Yet a little while I am with you. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He speaks with the greatest humility: as if to say, Why do you make such haste to kill Me? Only wait a little time. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That which you wish to do now, you shall do sometime, but not now: because it is not My will. For I wish to fulfill My mission in due course, and so to come to My passion. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In this way He astonished the bolder part of the multitude, and made the earnest among them more eager to hear Him; so little time being now left, during which they could have the benefit of His tea | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He were going to complain of them to the Father: for if they reviled Him who was sent, no doubt they did an injury to Him that sent. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I go to Him that sent Me: i.e. I return to My Father, at whose command I became incarnate. He is speaking of that departure, from which He has never returned. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That they wanted His presence, appears from His saying, You seek Me, and shall not find Me. But when did the Jews seek Him? Luke relates that the women lamented over Him: and it is probable that man | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here He foretells His resurrection: for the search for Him was to take place after His resurrection, when men were conscience stricken. They would not acknowledge Him, when present; afterward they s | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then lest any should think that His death would take place in the common way, He adds, And where I am, thither you cannot come. If He continued in death, they would be able to go to Him: for we all  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, Where I shall be, but Where I am. For Christ was always there in that place, whither He was about to return: He returned in such a way, as that He did not forsake us. Visibly and ac | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He wants them to think seriously how little time longer He should be with them, and what regret they will feel when He is gone, and they are not able to find Him. I go to Him that sent Me; this show | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whither I go, i.e. to the bosom of the Father. This they did not at all understand: and yet even their mistake is an unwitting prophecy of our salvation; i.e. that our Lord would go to the Gentiles, | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They did not mean, that our Lord was going to the Gentiles for their hurt, but to teach them. Their anger had subsided, and they believed what He had said. Else they would not have thought of asking | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The feast being over, and the people about to return home, our Lord gives them provisions for the way: On the last day, that great day of the feast, Jesus stood and cried, saying, If any man thirst, | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The feast was then going on, which is called scenopegia, i.e. building of tents. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which lasted seven days. The first and last days were the most important; In the last day, that great day of the feast, says the Evangelist. Those between were given chiefly to amusements. He did no | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To make Himself audible, inspire confidence in others, and show an absence of all fear in Himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If any thirsts: as if to say, I use no compulsion or violence: I but if any have the desire strong enough, let him come. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For there is an inner thirst, because there is an inner man: and the inner man of a certainty loves more than the outer. So then if we thirst, let us go not on our feet, but on our affections, not b | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is speaking of spiritual drink, as His next words show: He that believes in Me, as the Scripture truth said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. But where here does the Scripture  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Jerome, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or this testimony is taken from the Proverbs, where it is said, Let your fountains be dispersed abroad, and rivers of waters in the streets. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The belly of the inner man, is the heart’s conscience. Let him drink from that water, and his conscience is quickened and purified; he drinks in the whole fountain, nay, becomes the very fountain it | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When sacred preaching flows from the soul of the faithful, rivers of living water, as it were, run down from the bellies of believers. For what are the entrails of the belly but the inner part of th | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, rivers, not river, to show the copious and overflowing power of grace: and living water, i.e. always moving; for when the grace of the Spirit has entered into and settled in the mind, it fl | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What kind of drink it was, to which our Lord invited them, the Evangelist next explains; But this He spoke of the Spirit, which they that believe on Him should receive. Whom does the Spirit mean, bu | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He promised the Holy Spirit to the Apostles before the Ascension; He gave it to them in fiery tongues, after the Ascension. The Evangelist’s words, Which they that believe in Him should receive, ref | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Spirit of God was, i.e. was with God, before now; but was not yet given to those who believed on Jesus; for our Lord had determined not to give them the Spirit, till He was risen again: The Holy | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Apostles indeed cast out devils by the Spirit before, but only by the power which they had from Christ. For when He sent them, it is not said, He gave them the Holy Spirit, but, He gave to them  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet we read of John the Baptist, He shall be filled with the Holy Ghost even from his mother’s womb. And Zacharias was filled with the Holy Ghost, and prophesied. Mary was filled with the Holy Ghost | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the Holy Spirit then is received now, why is there no one who speaks the tongues of all nations? Because now the Church herself speaks the tongues of all nations. Whoso is not in her, neither doe | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:39 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If this then is the cause why the Holy Spirit was not yet given; viz. because Jesus was not yet glorified; doubtless, the glorification of Jesus when it took place, was the cause immediately of its  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; By the glory of Christ, He means the cross. For, whereas we were enemies, and gifts are not made to enemies, but to friends, it was necessary that the victim should be first offered up, and | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having invited those, who believed in Him, to drink of the Holy Spirit, a dissension arose among the multitude: Many of the people therefore, when they heart these saying, said, Of a truth  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The one, that is, who was expected. Others, i.e. the people said, This is the Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These had now begun to drink in that spiritual thirst, and had laid aside the unbelieving thirst. But others still remained dried up in their unbelief: But some said, Shall Christ come out of Galile | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But be it so, they knew not His birth-place: were they ignorant also of His extraction? that He was of the house and family of David? Why did they ask, Has not the Scripture said, that Christ comes  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not among the rulers; for they were resolved one way, viz. not to acknowledge Him as Christ. The more moderate of them only used malicious words, in order to oppose Christ’s path to glory; but the m | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist says this to show, that they had no concern for, and no anxiety to learn, the truth. But no man laid hands on Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, because He Who had the power to control their designs, did not permit it. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This were sufficient to have raised some compunction in them; but no, such malignity believes nothing; it looks only to one thing, blood. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They; however who were sent to take Him, returned guiltless of the offense, and full of admiration: Then came the officers to the chief priests and Pharisees; and they said to them, Why have you not | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They who wished to take and stone Him, reprove the officers for not bringing Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Pharisees and Scribes profited nothing by seeing the miracles, and reading the Scriptures; but their officers, who had done neither, were captivated with once hearing Him; and they who went to t | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He spoke thus, because He was both God and man. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not only is their wisdom to be admired, for not wanting miracles, but being convinced by His teaching only, (for they do not say, Never man did such miracles as this Man, but, Never man spoke like t | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Pharisees however rejected their testimony: Then answered them the Pharisees, Are you also led away? As if to say, We see that you are charmed by His discourse. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And so they were led away; and laudably too, for they had left the evil of unbelief, and were gone over to the faith. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They make use of the most foolish argument against them: Have any of the rulers or of the Pharisees believed on Him? but this people who knows not the law are cursed? This then was their ground of a | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They who knew not the law, believed on Him who had given the law, and they who taught the law condemned Him; thus fulfilling our Lord’s words, I am come, that they which see not might see, and that  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How then are they cursed, who are convinced by the law? Rather are you cursed, who have not observed the law. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Pharisees answer the officers courteously and gently, because they are afraid of their forthwith separating from them, and joining Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As they said that none of the rulers believed on Him, the Evangelist contradicts them: Nicodemus said to them, (he that came to Jesus by night, being one of them.) | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was not unbelieving, but fearful; and therefore came by night to the light, wishing to be enlightened, but afraid of being known to go. He replies, Does our law judge any man before it hear him,  | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls the law of God, our law; because it was given to men. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nicodemus shows that they knew the law, and did not act according to the law. They, instead of disproving this, take to rude and angry contradiction: They answered and said to him, Are you also of G | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. led away by a Galilean. Our Lord was called a Galilean, because His parents were of the town of Nazareth; I mean by parents, Mary. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then, by way of insult, they direct Him to the Scriptures, as if He were ignorant of them; Search and look, for out of Galilee arises no prophet: as if to say, Go, learn what the Scriptures say. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They knew the place where He had resided, but never thought of inquiring where He was born; and therefore they not only denied that He was the Messiah, but even that He was a prophet. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . No prophet indeed arises out of; Galilee, but the Lord of prophets arose thence. And every man went to his own house. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 7:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having effected nothing, devoid of faith, and therefore incapable of being benefited, they returned to their home of unbelief and ungodliness. | src: CAJohn.htm#7
John 8:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord at the time of His passion used to spend the day in Jerusalem, preaching in the temple, and performing miracles, and return in the evening to Bethany, where He lodged with the sisters of La | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And where ought Christ to teach, except on the mount of Olives; on the mount of ointment, on the mount of chrism. For the name Christ is from chrism, chrism being the Greek word for unction. He has  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The anointing with oil is a relief to the limbs, when wearied and in pain. The mount of Olives also denotes the height of our Lord’s pity, olive in the Greek signifying pity. The qualities of oil ar | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And next it is signified, that after He began to dwell by grace in His temple, i.e. in the Church, men from all nations would believe in Him: And all the people came to Him, and He sat down and taug | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sitting down, represents the humility of His incarnation. And the people came to Him, when He sat down, i.e. after taking up human nature, and thereby becoming visible, many began to hear and be | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They had remarked upon, Him already, as being over lenient. Of Him indeed it had I been prophesied, Ride on because of the word of truth, of meekness, and of righteousness. So as a teacher He exhibi | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to signify that such persons were to be written in earth, not in heaven, where He told His disciples they should rejoice they were v written. Or His bowing His head (to write on the ground), i | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The ground denotes the human heart, which yields the fruit either of good or of bad actions: the finger jointed and flexible, discretion. He instructs us then, when we see any faults in our neighbor | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His writing with His finger on the ground perhaps showed, that it was He who had written the law on stone. So when they continued asking Him, He lifted Himself up. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not say, Stone her not, lest He should seem to speak contrary to the law. But God forbid that He should say, Stone her; for He came not to destroy that which He found, but to seek that which  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For he who judges not himself first, cannot know how to judge correctly in the case of another. For though He know what the offense is, from being told, yet He cannot judge of another’s deserts, who | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having with the weapon of justice smitten them, He deigned not even to look on the fallen, but averted His eyes: And again He stooped down, and wrote on the ground. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is like our Lord; while His eyes are fixed, and He seems attending to something else, He gives the bystanders an opportunity of retiring: a tacit admonition to us to consider always both before | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus smitten then with the voice of justice, as with a weapon, they examine themselves, find themselves guilty, and one by one retire: And they which heard it, went out one by one, beginning at the  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:11 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The more guilty of them, perhaps, or those who were more conscious of their faults. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There were left however two, the pitiable, and the pitiful, And Jesus was left alone, and the woman standing in the midst: the woman, you may suppose, in great alarm, expecting punishment from one i | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having absolved the woman from her Sill, lest some should doubt, seeing that He was really man, His power to forgive sins, He deigns to give further disclosure of His divine nature; Then spoke Jesus | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Where it is to be observed, He does not say, I am the light of Angels, or of heaven, but the Light of the world, i.e. of mankind who live in darkness, as we read, To give light to them that sit in dar | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As they had brought Galilee as an objection against Him, and doubted His being one of the Prophets, as if that was all He claimed to be, Me wished to show that He was not one of the Prophets, but th | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Manichaeans suppose the sun of the natural world to be our Lord Christ; but the Catholic Church reprobates such a notion for our Lord Christ was not made the sun, but the sun was made by Him: in | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You may bring these words against Nestorius: for our Lord does not say, In Me is the light of the world, but, I am the Light of the world: He who appeared man, was both the Son of God, and the Light | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He withdraws you however from the eyes of the flesh, to those of the heart, in that He adds, He that follows Me shall not walk in darkness, but shall have the light of life. He thinks it not enough  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Walk not in darkness, i.e. spiritually abide not in error. Here He tacitly praises Nicodemus and the officers, and censures those who had plotted against Him; as being in darkness and error, and una | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having said, I am the Light of the world; and, he that follows Me, walks not in darkness, the Jews wish to overthrow what He has said: The Pharisees therefore said to Him, you bear record o | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if our Lord Himself were the only (one that bore) witness to Himself; whereas the truth was that He had, before His incarnation, sent many witnesses to prophesy of His Sacraments. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord however overthrew their argument: Jesus answered and said, Though I bear record of Myself, yet My record is true. This is an accommodation to those who thought Him no more than a mere man.  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The witness of light is true, whether the light show itself, or other things. The Prophet spoke the truth, but whence had he it, but by drawing from the fount of truth? Jesus then is a competent wit | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say: You judge untruly, according to the flesh, thinking, because I am in the flesh, that I am flesh only, and not God. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Understanding Me not as God, and seeing Me as man, you think Me arrogant in bearing witness of Myself. For any man who bears high testimony to himself, is thought proud and arrogant. But men are fra | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As to live according to the flesh is to live amiss, so to judge according to the flesh, is to judge unjustly. They might say, however, If we judge wrongly, why do you not convict us, why do you not  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which may be understood in two ways; judge no man, i.e. not now: as He says elsewhere, God sent not His Son into the world to condemn the world, but that the world through Him might be saved: not th | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say: In saying, I judge no man, I meant that I did not anticipate judgment. If I judged justly, I should condemn you, but now is not the time for judging. He alludes however to the future j | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if the Father is with You, how did He send You? O Lord, Your mission is Your incarnation. Christ was here according to the flesh without withdrawing from the Father, because the Father and the S | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Is this made a bad use of by the Manichaeans, that our Lord does not say, in the law of God, but, in your law? Who does not recognize here a manner of speaking customary in Scripture? In your law, i | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There is much difficulty, and a great mystery seems to be contained, in God’s words, In the mouth of two or three witnesses, let every word be established. It is possible that two may speak false. T | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In many places the Father bears witness of the Son; as, This day have I begotten You; also, This is My beloved Son. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is written in your law, that the testimony of two men is true. If this is to be taken literally, in what respect does our Lord differ from men? The rule has been laid down for men, on the ground  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it is as if He said, If your law admits the testimony of two men who may be deceived, and testify to more than is true; on what grounds can you reject Mine and My Father’s testimony, the highest  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Those who had heard out Lord say, You judge after the flesh, showed that they did so; for they understood what He said of His Father in a carnal sense: Then said they to Him; Where is Your Father? m | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some remark that this is said in contumely and contempt; to insinuate either that He is born of fornication, and knows not who His Father is; or as a slur on the low situation of His father, i.e. Jo | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, You ask where is Your Father? As if you knew Me already, and I were nothing else but what you see. But you know Me not, and therefore I tell you nothing of My Father. You think Me ind | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He tells them, it is of no avail for them to say they know the Father, if they do not know the Son. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You neither know Me, nor My Father: this seems inconsistent with what was said above, You both know Me, and know whence I am. But the latter is spoken in reply to some from Jerusalem, who asked, Do  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What does this mean: If you knew Me, you would know My Father also, but, I and My Father are one? It is a common expression, when you see one man very like another, If you have seen him, you have se | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let the Arian blush: for if, as he says, the Son be a creature, how does it follow that he who knows the creature, knows God? For not even by knowing the substance of Angels, does one know the Divin | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This word perhaps is used only by way of rebuke, though it seems to express doubt. As used by men indeed it is the expression of doubt, but He who knew all things could only mean by that doubt to re | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is proper to observe, that the followers of other sects think this text proves clearly, that the God, whom the Jews worshipped, was not the Father of Christ. For if, say they, our Savior said thi | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Treasury (Gazophylacium): Gaza is the Persian for wealth: phylattein is to keep. It was a place in the temple, where the money was kept. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He spoke in the temple magisterially, and now He was speaking to those who railed at and accused Him, for making Himself equal to the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Great however is His confidence and fearlessness: it not being possible that He should undergo any suffering, but that which He voluntarily undertook. Wherefore it follows, And no man laid hands on  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When ever it is added, Jesus spoke these words in such a place, you will, if you attend, discover a meaning in the addition. The treasury was a place for keeping the money, which was given for the h | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; Christ speaks in the treasury; i.e. He had spoken in parables to the Jews; but now that He unfolded heavenly things to His disciples, His treasury began to be opened, which was the meaning  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In accordance with what was just, He said that no man laid hands on Him, because His hour was not yet come; He now speaks to the Jews of His passion, as a free, and not a compulsory sacrifice on His | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The connection of these words is such, that they might have been spoken at one place and one time, or at another place and another time: as either nothing at all, or some things, or many may have in | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But some one will object: If this was spoken to men who persisted in unbelief, how is it He says, You shall seek Me? For to seek Jesus is to seek truth and wisdom. You will answer that it was said o | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You shall seek Me, then, He says, not from compassionate regret, but from hatred: for after He had departed from the eyes of men, He was sought for both by those who hated, and those who loved Him:  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Note: sin is in the singular number, your in the plural; to express one and the same wickedness in all. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But I ask, as it is said below that many believed on Him, whether He speaks to all present, when He says, You shall die in your sins? No: He speaks to those only, whom He knew would not believe, and | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This He tells His disciples in another place; without saying to them, however, You, shall die in your sin, He only says Whither I go, you cannot follow Me now; not preventing, but only delaying thei | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Word, while still present, yet threatens to depart. So long as we preserve the seeds of truth implanted in our minds, the Word of God does not depart from us. But if we fall into wickedness then | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They take these words, as they generally do, in a carnal sense, and ask, Will He kill Himself, because He said, Whither I go, you cannot come? A foolish question. For why? Could they not go where He | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He shows here that He will rise again in glory, and sit at the right hand of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . May they not however have a higher meaning in saying this? For they had opportunities of knowing many things from their apocryphal books or from tradition. As then there was a prophetical tradition, | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, No wonder that you think as you do, seeing you are carnal, and understand nothing spiritually. I am from above. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From whom above? From the Father Himself, Who is above all. You are of this world, I am not of this world. How could He be of the world, by Whom the world was made? | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And Who was before the world, whereas they were of the world, having been created after the world had begun to exist. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He says, I am not of this world, with reference to worldly and vain thoughts. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I affect nothing worldly, nothing earthly: I could never come to such madness as to kill Myself. Apollinarius, however, falsely infers from these words, that our Lord’s body was not of this world, b | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Beneath, and, of this world, are different things. Beneath, refers to a particular place; this material world embraces a different tracts, which all are beneath, as compared with things immaterial a | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord expresses His meaning in the words, You are of this world, i.e. you are sinners. All of us are born in sin; all have added by our actions to the sin in which we were born. The misery of the | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For if He came in order to take away sin, and a man cannot put that off, except by washing, and cannot be baptized except he believe; it follows, that he who believers not must pass out of this life | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His saying, If you believe not that I am, without adding any thing, proves a great deal. For thus it was that God spoke to Moses, I am that I am. But how do I understand, I am that I am, and, If you | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is manifest, that he, who dies in his sins, though he say that he believes in Christ, does not really believe. For he who believes in His justice does not do injustice; he who believes in His wis | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having said, You believe not that I am, you shall die in your sins; they inquire of Him, as if wishing to, know in whom they are to believe, that they might not die in their sin: Then said  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In some copies we find, Who also speak to you; but it is more consistent to read for (quia), not, who (qui): in which case the meaning is: Believe Me to be the beginning, for your own sakes have I c | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See here the madness of the Jews; asking after so long time, and after all His miracles and teaching, Who are You? What is Christ’s answer? From the beginning I speak with you; as if to say, you do  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Above He said, I judge no man but, I judge not, is one thing, I have to judge another. I judge not, He says, with reference to the present time. But the other, I have many things to say, and to judg | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says this, that they may not think that He allows them to talk against Him with impunity, from inability to punish them, or that He is not alive to their contemptuous designs. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or having said, I have many things to say, and to judge of you, thus reserving His judgment for a future time, He adds, But He that sells Me is true: as If to say, Though you are unbelievers, My Fat | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: As My Father has sent Me not to judge the world, but to save the world, and My Father is true, I accordingly judge no man now; but speak thus for your salvation, not your condemnation: And  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And do hear from the Father is the same as to be from the Father; He has the hearing from the same sense that He has the being. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The coequal Son gives glory to the Father: as if to say, I give glory to Him whose Son I am: how proudly you detract from Him, whose servant you are. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They did not understand however what He meant by saying, He is true that sent Me: they understand not that He spoke to them of the Father. For they had not the eyes of their mind yet opened, to unde | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When our Lord said, He is true that sent Me, the Jews did not understand that He spoke to them of the Father. But He saw some there, who, He knew, would believe on Him after His passion. Then said J | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or the connection is this: When His miracles and teaching had failed to convert men, He spoke of the cross; When you have lifted up the Son of man, then you shall know that I am He: as if to say, yo | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Having said, Then shall you know that I am, and in this, I am, implied the whole Trinity: lest the Sabellian error should creep in, He immediately adds, And I do nothing of Myself; as if to | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He gives now a humbler turn to the discourse: And He that sent Me. That this might not be thought however to imply inferiority, He says, Is with Me. The former is His dispensation, the latter His di | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And though both are together, yet one is sent, the other sends. For the mission is the incarnation; and the incarnation is of the Son only, not of the Father. He says then, He that sent Me, meaning, | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He means it as an answer to those who were constantly saying that He was not from God, and that because He did not keep the sabbath; I do always, He says, do those things that please Him; showin | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord wished to try the faith of those who believed, that it might not be only a superficial belief: Then said Jesus to those Jews which believed in Him, If you continue in My word, then are you  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We have all one Master, and are fellow disciples under Him. Nor because we speak with authority, are we therefore masters; but He is the Master of all, Who dwells in the hearts of all. It is a small | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say: Whereas you have now belief, by continuing, you shall have sight. For it was not their knowledge which made them believe, but rather their belief which gave them knowledge. Faith is to | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, You shall know the truth, i.e. Me: for I am the truth. The Jewish was a typical dispensation; the reality you can only know from Me. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some one might say perhaps, And what does it profit me to know the truth? So our Lord adds, And the truth shall free you; as if to say, If the truth does not delight you, liberty, will. To be freed  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As He said to the unbelievers alone, You shall die in your sin, so now to them who continue in the faith He proclaims absolution. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From what shall the truth free us, but from death, corruption, mutability, itself being immortal, uncorrupt, immutable? Absolute immutability is in itself eternity. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Men who really believed could have borne to he rebuked. But these men began immediately to show anger. Indeed if they had been disturbed at His former saying, they had much more reason to be so now. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or it was not those who believed, but the unbelieving multitude that made this answer. But how could they say with truth, taking only secular bondage into account, that we have never been in bondage | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ then, who speaks for their good, not to gratify their vainglory, explains His meaning to have been that they were the servants not of men, but of sin, the hardest kind of servitude, from whic | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This asseveration is important: it is, if one may say so, His oath. Amen means true, but is not translated. Neither the Greek nor the Latin Translator have dared to translate it. It is a Hebrew word | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because whoever yields to wrong desires, puts his hitherto free soul under the yoke of the evil one, and takes him for his master. But we oppose this master, when we struggle against the wickedness  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And the more freely men follow their perverse desires, the more closely are they in bondage to them. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O miserable bondage! The slave of a human master when wearied with the hardness of his tasks, sometimes takes refuge in flight. But whither does the slave of sin flee? He takes it along with him, wh | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not from the barbarians, but from the devil; not from the captivity of the body, but from the wickedness of the soul. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The first stage of freedom’ is, the abstaining from sin. But that is only incipient, it is not perfect freedom: for the flesh still lusts against the spirit, so that you do not do the things that yo | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Or thus: Having said that whosoever commits sin, is the servant of sin, He anticipates the answer that their sacrifices saved them, by saying, The servant abides not in the house for ever, but the S | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Do not then abuse your freedom, for the purpose of sinning freely; but use it in order not to sin at all. Your will will be free, if it be merciful: you will be free, if you become the servant of ri | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews had asserted they were free, because they were Abraham’s seed. Our Lord replies, I know that you are Abraham’s seed; as if to say, I know that you are the sons of Abraham, but according to  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says this, that they might not attempt to answer, that they had no sin. He reminds them of a present sin; a sin which they had been meditating for some time past, and which was actually at this m | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, has not place in your hearts, because your heart does not take it in. The word of God to the believing, is like the hook to the fish; it takes when it is taken: and that not to the injury o | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): He does not say, you do not take in My word, but My word has not room in you; showing the depth of His doctrines. But they might say; What if you speak of yourself? So He adds, I speak that which I ha | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord by His Father wishes us to understand God: as if to say, I have seen the truth, I speak the truth, because I am the truth. If our Lord then speaks the truth which He saw with the Father, it | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is proof that our Savior was witness to what was done with the Father: whereas men, to whom the revelation is made, were not witnesses. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But when you hear, I speak that which I have seen, do not think it means bodily vision, but innate knowledge, sure, and approved. For as the eyes when they see an object, see it wholly and correctly | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As yet He has not named their father; He mentioned Abraham indeed a little above, but now He is going to mention another father, viz. the devil: whose sons they were, in so far as they were wicked,  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Another reading has, And do you do that which you have seen with your father; as if to say, As I both in word and deed declare to you the Father, so do you by your works show forth Abraham. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Also another reading has; And, do you do what you have heard from the Father. All that was written in the Law and the Prophets they had heard from the Father. He who takes this reading, may use it t | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, What are you going to say against Abraham? They seem to be inviting Him to say something in disparagement of Abraham; and so to give them an opportunity of executing their purpose. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Savior denies that Abraham is their father: Jesus said to them, If you were Abraham’s children, you would do the works of Abraham. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And yet He says above, I know that you are Abraham’s seed. So He does not deny their origin, but condemns their deeds. Their flesh was from him; their life was not. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or we may explain the difficulty thus. Above it is in the Greek, I know that you are Abraham’s seed. So let us examine whether there is not a difference between a bodily seed and a child. It is evid | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This truth, that is, that He was equal to the Father: for it was this that moved the Jews to kill Him. To show, however, that this doctrine is not opposed to the Father, He adds, Which I have heard  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because He Himself, Who is the truth, was begotten of God the Father, to hear, being in fact the same with to be from the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To kill Me, He says, a man. I say nothing now of the Son of God, nothing of the Word, because the Word cannot die; I speak only of that which you see. It is in your power to kill that which you see, | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, By this you prove that you are not the sons of Abraham; that you do works contrary to those of Abraham. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It might seem to some, that it were superfluous to say that Abraham did not this; for it were impossible that it should be; Christ was not born at that time. But we may remind them, that in Abraham’ | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say as yet who is their father. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord says this with a view to put down their vain boasting of their descent; and persuade them to rest their hopes of salvation no longer on the natural relationship, but on the adoption. For th | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews had begun to understand that our Lord was not speaking of sonship according to the flesh, but of manner of life. Scripture often speaks of spiritual fornication, with many gods, and of the  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if their motive against Him was a desire to avenge God’s honor. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or their sonship to Abraham having been disproved, they reply by bitterly insinuating, that our Savior was the offspring of adultery. But perhaps the tone of the answer is disputatious, more than an | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what say you? Have you God for your Father, and do you blame Christ for speaking thus? Yet true it was, that many of them were born of fornication, for people then used to form unlawful connecti | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was not that the Son of God condemned the assumption of so religious a name; that is, condemned them for professing to be the sons of God, and calling God the Father; but that He blamed the rash  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This then is the eternal procession, the proceeding forth of the Word from God: from Him. It proceeded as the Word of the Father, and came to us: The Word was made flesh. His advent is His humanity: | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In what follows, He teaches that His origin is not in Himself; Neither came I of Myself, but He sent Me. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was said, I think, in allusion to some who came without being sent by the Father, of whom it is said in Jeremiah, I have not sent these prophets, yet they ran. Some, however, use this passage t | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because they were ever inquiring, What is this which He said, Whither I go you cannot come? He adds here, Why do you not understand My speech? even because you cannot hear My word. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And they could not hear, because they would not believe, and amend their lives. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Fist then, that virtue must be sought after, which hears the divine word; that by degrees we may be strong enough to embrace the whole teaching of Jesus. For so long as a man has not his hearing res | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, having already cut off the Jews from relationship to Abraham, overthrows now this far greater claim, to call God their Father, You are of your father the devil. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here we must guard against the heresy of the Manicheans, who hold a certain original nature of evil, and a nation of darkness with princes at their head, whence the devil derives his existence. And  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And this seems to be the same mistake, as if one said, that an eye which saw right was different in kind from an eye which saw wrong. For just as in these there is no difference of kind, only one of | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews then were children of the devil by imitation, not by birth: And the lusts of your father you will do, our Lord says. You are his children then, because you have such lusts, not because you  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Consider too, it was not one man only that he killed, but the whole human race, inasmuch as in Adam all die; so that he is truly called a murderer from the beginning. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, his works, but his lusts you will do, meaning that both the devil and the Jews were bent on murder, to satisfy their envy. And stood not in the truth. He shows whence sprang their c | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it will be objected perhaps, that if from the beginning of his existence, the devil stood not in the truth, he was never in a state of blessedness with the holy angels, refusing, as he did, to b | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There is only one way of standing in the truth; many and various of not standing in it. Some try to stand in the truth, but their feet tremble and shake so, they cannot. Others are not come to that  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or when our Lord says, The truth is not in him, He intends it as an index: as if we had asked Him, how it appeared that the devil stood not in the truth; and He said, Because the truth is not in him | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some have thought from these words that the devil had a father, and asked who was the father of the devil This is the error of the Manichaeans. But our Lord calls the devil the father of a lie for t | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For he accused God to man, saying to Eve, But of envy He has forbidden you the tree: and to God he accused man, as in Job, Does Job serve God, for wrought? | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Note however this word, liar, is applied to man, as well as to the devil, who begat a lie, as we read in the Psalm, All men are liars. If a man is not a liar, he is not an ordinary man, but one of t | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The devil is not a singular, but a common name. In whomsoever the works of the devil are found, he is to be called the devil. It is the name of a work, not of a nature. Here then our Lord m | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord being the truth, and the Son of the true God, spoke the truth; but the Jews, being the sons of the devil, were averse to the truth; and this is why our Lord says, Because I tell you the tru | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how is this said to the Jews, who believed in Him? Consider: a man may believe in one sense, not believe in another; e.g. that our Lord was crucified by Pontius Pilate, but not that He was born  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You wish to kill Me then, because you are enemies of the truth, not that you have any fault to find in Me: for, which of you convinces Me of sin? | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say: If you are the sons of God, you ought to hold sinners in hatred. If you hate Me, when you cannot convince Me of sin, it is evident that you hate Me because of the truth: i.e. because I | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A bold speech this; which none could have had the confidence to utter, but he Who did no sin; even our Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe here the condescension of God. He who in by virtue of His Divinity could justify sinners, deigns to show from reason, that He is not a sinner. It follows: He that is of God hears God’s words | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:47 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Apply this not to their nature, but to their faults. They both are from God and are not from God at the same time; their nature is from God, their fault is not from God. This was spoken too to those | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let him then, who would understand God’s words ask himself whether he hears them with the ears of his heart. For there are some who do not deign to hear God’s commands even with their bodily ears; a | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whenever our Lord said any thing of lofty meaning, the Jews in their insensibility set it down madness: Then answered the Jews and said to Him, Say we not well that you are a Samaritan, and have a d | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how, we may ask, when the Samaritans denied a future life, and the immortality of the soul, could they dare to call our Savior, Who had preached so much on the resurrection and the judgment, a S | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Samaritans were hated by the Jews; they lived in the land that formerly belonged to the ten tribes, who had been carried away. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is not unlikely too, some may have thought that He held the Samaritan opinion of there being no future state really, and only put forth the doctrine of a resurrection and eternal life, in order g | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or they called Him a Samaritan because He transgressed the Hebrew ordinances, as that of the sabbath: the Samaritans not being correct observers of the law. And they suspected Him of having a devil, | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See; when God suffers a wrong, He does not reply reproachfully: Jesus answered, I have not a devil. An intimation this to us, that when reproached by our neighbors falsely, we should not retort upon | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And observe, when He had to teach them, and pull down their pride, He used roughness; but now that He has to suffer rebuke, He treats them with the utmost mildness: a lesson to us to be severe in wh | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And to imitate His patience first, if we would attain to His power. But though being reviled, He reviled not again, it was incumbent on Him to deny the charge. Two charges had been made against Him: | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, even more than Paul, wished to become all things to all men, that He might gain some and therefore He did not deny being a Samaritan. I have not a devil, is what Jesus alone can say; as He | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then after being so reviled, all that He says to vindicate His glory, is, But I honor My Father: as if to say, That you may not think Me arrogant, I tell you, I have One, Whom I honor. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He honored the Father, by revenging Him, and not suffering murderers or liars to call themselves the true sons of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ alone honored the Father perfectly. No one, who honors any thing which is not honored by God, honors God. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As all who have zeal toward God are liable to meet with dishonor from wicked men, our Lord has Himself set us an example of patience under this trial; And you do dishonor Me. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, I do my duty: you do not do yours. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And this was not addressed to them only, but to all who by unrighteous deeds inflict injury upon Christ, who is righteousness; or by scoffing at wisdom wrong Him who is wisdom: and the like. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How we are to take injuries, He shows us by His own example, when He adds, I seek not Mine own glory, there is one that seeks and judges. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, I have told you this on account of the honor which I have for My Father; and for this you dishonor Me. But I concern not myself for your reviling: you are accountable to Him, for whose | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . God seeks Christ’s s glory, in every one of those who receive Him: which glory He finds in those who cultivate the seeds of virtue implanted in them. And those in whom He finds not His Son’s glory,  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Meaning of course the Father. But is it then that He says in another place, The Father judges no man, but has committed all judgment to the Son. Judgment is sometimes put for condemnation, whereas h | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus; If that is true which our Savior says below, All men are yours, it is manifest that the judgment itself of the Son, is the Father’s. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the perversity of the wicked increases, preaching so far from giving way, ought even to become more active. Thus our Lord, after He had been accused of having a devil, imparts the treasures of pr | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See is put for experience. But since, about to die Himself, He spoke with those about to die, what means this, If a man keep My saying, he shall never see death? What, but that He saw another death  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:51 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must understand Him, as it were, to say, If a man keep My light, he shall not see darkness forever; forever being taken as common to both clauses, as if the sentence were, If a man keep My saying | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, keep, i.e. not by faith, but by purity of life. And at the same time too He means it as a tacit intimation that they can do nothing to Him. For if whoever keeps His word, shall never die, m | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As it is necessary that the good should grow better by contumely, so are the reprobate made worse by kindness On hearing our Lord’s words, the Jews again blaspheme: Then said the Jews to Him, Now we | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Those who believe the Holy Scriptures, understand that what men do contrary to right reason, is not done without the operation of devils. Thus the Jews thought that Jesus had spoken by the influence | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Again, they have recourse to the vainglorious argument of their descent: Are you greater than our father Abraham, which is dead? They might have said, Are you greater than God, whose words they are  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For they do not see that not Abraham only, but every one born of women, is less than He who was born of a Virgin. Now were the Jews right in saying that Abraham was dead? for he heard the word of Ch | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For being given over to eternal death, which death they saw not, and thinking only, as they did, of the death of the body, their minds were darkened, even while the Truth Himself was speaking. They  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, you a person of no account, a carpenter’s son of Galilee, to take glory to Yourself! | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whom make you Yourself? i.e. Of what merit, of what dignity would you be accounted? Nevertheless, Abraham only died in the body; his soul lived. And the death of the soul which is to live for ever,  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was the speech of persons spiritually blind. For Jesus did not make Himself what He was, but received it from the Father: Jesus answered and said, If I honor Myself, My honor is nothing. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is to answer their suspicions as above, If I bear witness of Myself My witness is not true. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He shows in these words that the glory of this present life is nothing. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is to answer those who said, Whom make you Yourself? He refers His glory to the Father, from Whom He is: It is My Father that honors Me. The Arians take occasion from those words to calumniate  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father glorified the Son, at His baptism, on the mount, at the time of His passion, when a voice came to Him, in the midst of the crowd, when He raised Him up again after His passion, and placed | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He adds, Of whom you say that He is your God; meaning to tell them that they were not only ignorant of the Father, but even of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For had they known the Father really, they would have reverenced the Son. But they even despise God, who in the Law forbade murder, by their clamors against Christ. Wherefore He says, You have not k | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, you call Him your God, after a carnal manner, serving Him for temporal rewards. You have not known Him, as He should be known; you are not able to serve Him spiritually. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some heretics say that the God proclaimed in the Old Testament is not the Father of Christ, but a kind of prince of bad angels. These He contradicts when He calls Him His Father, whom the Jews calle | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, As you, saying that you know Him, lie; so were I a liar, did I say I knew Him not. It follows, however, (which is the greatest proof of all that He was sent from God,) But I know Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having that knowledge by nature; for as I am, so is the Father also; I know Myself, and therefore I know Him. And He gives the proof that He knows Him: And I keep His saying, i.e. His commandments.  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He spoke the saying of the Father too, as being the Son; and He was Himself that Word of the Father, which He spoke to men. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In answer then to their question, Are you greater than our father Abraham, He shows them that He is greater than Abraham; Your father Abraham rejoiced to see My day: he saw it, and was glad; he must | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, He regarded My day, as a day to be desired, and full of joy; not as if I was an unimportant or common person. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not fear, but rejoiced to see: he rejoiced in hope, believing, and so by faith saw. It admits of doubt whether He is speaking here of the temporal day of the Lord, that, viz. of His coming in | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Abraham saw the day of the Lord even then, when he entertained the three Angels, a figure of the Trinity. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:56 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They are aliens from Abraham if they grieve over what he rejoiced in. By this day perhaps He means the day of the cross, which Abraham prefigured by the offering up of Isaac and the ram: intimating  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If they rejoiced to whom the Word appeared in the flesh, what was his joy, who beheld in spiritual vision the light ineffable, the abiding Word, the bright illumination of pious souls, the indefecti | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The carnal minds of the Jews are intent on the flesh only; they think only of His age in the flesh: Then said the Jews to Him, you are not fifty years old, and have you seen Abraham? that is to say, | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ was then thirty-three years old. Why then do they not say, You are not yet forty years old, instead of fifty? A needless question this: they simply spoke as chance led them at the time. Some  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Savior mildly draws them away from their carnal view, to the contemplation of His Divinity; Jesus said to them, Verily, verily, I say to you, Before Abraham was, I am. Before is a particle of pa | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Abraham being a creature, He did not say before Abraham was, but, before Abraham was made. Nor does He say, I am made; because that, in the beginning WAS the Word. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their unbelieving minds, however, were unable to support these indications of eternity; and not understanding Him, sought to destroy Him: Then they took up stones to cast at Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Such hardness of heart, whither was it to run, but to its truest likeness, even the stones? But now that He had done all that He could do as a teacher, and they in return wished to stone Him, since  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Who, had He chosen to exert the power of His Divinity, could, without a word, by His mere nod, have seized them, with the very stones in their hands, and delivered them to immediate death. But He wh | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For His part was more to exhibit patience than exercise power. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He fled, because His hour was not yet come; and because He had not chosen this kind of death. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then, as a man, He flies from the stones; but woe to them, from whose stony hearts God flies. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, a man throws a stone at Jesus, as often as he harbors an evil thought, and if he follows it up, so far as lies in him, he kills Jesus. | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 8:59 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What does our Lord mean by hiding Himself, but that the truth is hidden to them, who despise His words. The truth flies the company of an unhumbled soul. His example shows us, that we should in all  | src: CAJohn.htm#8
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews having rejected Christ’s words, because of their depth, He went out of the temple, and healed the blind man; that His absence might appease their fury, and the miracle soften their hard hea | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, our Lord, after being banished from the minds of the Jews, passed over to the Gentiles. The passage or journey here is His descent from heaven to earth, where He saw the blind man, i.e.  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the blind man here is the human race. Blindness came upon the first man by reason of sin: and from him we all derive it: i.e. man is blind from his birth. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Rabbi is Master. They call Him Master, because they wished to learn: they put their question to our Lord, as to a Master. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This question does not seem a proper one. For the Apostles had not been taught the fond notion of the Gentiles, that the soul has sinned in a previous state of existence. It is difficult to account  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They were led to ask this question, by our Lord having said above, on healing the man sick of the palsy, Lo, you are made whole; sin no more. Thinking from this that the man had been struck with the | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Was he then born without original sin, or had he never added to it by actual sin? Both this man and his parents had sinned, but that sin was not the reason why he was born blind. Our Lord gives the  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is not to be understood as meaning that others had become blind, in consequence of their parents’ sins: for one man cannot be punished for the sin of another. But had the man therefore suffered u | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . One stroke falls on the sinner, for punishment only, not conversion; another for correction; another not for correction of past sins, but prevention of future; another neither for correcting past, n | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the glory of God should be made manifest, He said of Himself, not of the Father; the Father’s glory was manifest already. I must work the works of Him that sent Me; i.e. I must manifest Myself, | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For when the Son declared that He worked the works of the Father, He proved that His and His Father’s works were the same: which are to heal the sick, to strengthen the weak, and enlighten man. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By His saying, Who sent Me, He gives all the glory to Him from Whom He is. The Father has a Son Who is from Him, but has none from whom He Himself is. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While it is day, He adds; i.e. while men have the opportunity of believing in Me; while this life lasts; The night comes, when none can work. Night here means that spoken of in Matthew, Cast him int | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if we work now, now is the day time, now is Christ present; as He says, As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world. This then is the day. The natural day is completed by the circu | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then confirms His words by deeds: When He had thus spoken, He spat on the ground, and made clay of the spittle, and anointed the eyes of the blind man with the clay. He who had brought greater su | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some think that the clay was not laid upon the eyes, but made into eyes. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord spat upon the ground, and made clay of the spittle, because He was the Word made flesh. The man did not see immediately as he was anointed; i.e. was, as it were, only made a catechumen. But | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: By His spittle understand the savor of inward contemplation. It runs down from the head into the mouth, and gives us the taste of revelation from the Divine splendor even in this life. The  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The suddenness of the miracle made men incredulous: The neighbors therefore, and they which had seen him that he was blind, said, Is not this he that sat and begged? Wonderful clemency and condescen | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His eyes being opened had altered his look. But he said, I am he. He spoke gratefully; a denial would have convicted Him of ingratitude. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was not ashamed of his former blindness, nor afraid of the fury of the people, nor averse to show himself, and proclaim his Benefactor. Therefore said they to him, How were your eyes opened? How  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo, he is become a proclaimer of grace, an evangelist, and testifies to the Jews. That blind man testified, and the ungodly were vexed at the heart, because they had not in their heart what appeared | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This they said, because they were meditating His death, having already begun to conspire against Him. Christ did not appear in company with those whom He cured; having no desire for glory, or displa | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here he is like one anointed, but unable yet to see: he preaches, and knows not what he preaches. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus he represents the state of the catechumen, who believes in Jesus, but does not, strictly speaking, know Him, not being yet washed. It fell to the Pharisees to confirm or deny the miracle. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews, whom they asked, Where is He? were desirous of finding Him, in order to bring Him to the Pharisees; but, as they could not find Him, they bring the blind man. They brought to the Pharisees | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some, not all: for some were already anointed. But they, who neither saw, nor were anointed, said, This man is not of God, because he keeps not the sabbath day. Rather He kept it, in that He was wit | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Passing over the miracle in silence, they give all the prominence they can to the supposed transgression; not charging Him with healing on the sabbath, but with not keeping the sabbath. Others said, | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was Christ, who divided the day into light Au and darkness. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Those who said, Can a man that is a sinner do such miracles? wishing to stop the others’ mouths, make the object of our Lord’s goodness again come forward; but without appearing to take part with Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:17 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See with what good intent they put the question. They do not say, What say you of Him that keeps not the sabbath, but mention the miracle, that He has opened your eyes; meaning, it would seem, to dr | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or they sought how they could throw reproach upon the man, and cast him out of their synagogue. he declares however openly what he thinks: He said, He is a Prophet. Not being anointed yet in heart,  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Pharisees being unable, by intimidation, to deter the blind man from publicly proclaiming his Benefactor, try to nullify the miracle through the parents. But the Jews did not believe concerning  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. had been blind, and now saw. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it is the nature of truth, to be strengthened by the very snares that are laid against it. A lie is its own antagonist, and by its attempts to injure the truth, sets it off to greater advantage: | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Either, say they, it is not true that he now sees, or it is untrue that he was blind before: but it is evident that he now sees; therefore it is not true that he was born blind. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Three things then being asked, - if he were their son, if he had been blind, and how he saw, - they acknowledge two of them: his parents answered them and said, We know that this is our son, and tha | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, We might justly be compelled to speak for an infant, that could not speak for itself: but he, though blind from his birth, has been always able to speak. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What sort of gratitude is this in the parents; concealing what they knew, from fear of the Jews? as we are next told; These words spoke his parents, because they feared the Jews. And then the Evange | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was no disadvantage to be put out of the synagogue: whom they cast out, Christ took in. Therefore said, his parents, He is of age, ask him. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:23 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist shows that it was not from ignorance, but feat, that they gave this answer. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For they were fainthearted; not like their son, that intrepid witness to the truth, the eyes of whose understanding had been enlightened by God. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The parents having referred the Pharisees to the healed man himself, they summon him a second time: Then again called they the man that was blind. They do not openly say now, Deny that Christ has he | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Deny that you have received the benefit. This is not to give God the glory, but rather to blaspheme Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They wished him to give glory to God, by calling Christ a sinner, as they did: We know that this man is a sinner. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why then did you not convict Him, when He said above, Which of you convinces Me of sin? | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The man, that he might neither expose himself to calumny, nor at the same time conceal the truth, answers not that he knew Him to be righteous, but, Whether He is a sinner or no, I know not. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how comes this, whether He be a sinner, I know not, from one who had said, He is a Prophet? Did the blind fear? far from it: he only thought that our Lord’s defense lay in the witness of the fac | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Will you also? i.e. I am already, do you wish to be? I see now, but do not envy. He says this in indignation at the obstinacy of the Jews; not tolerating blindness, now that he is no longer blind hi | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As then truth is strength, so falsehood is weakness: truth elevates and ennobles whomever it takes up, however mean before: falsehood brings even the strong to weakness and contempt. Then they revil | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A malediction only in the intention of the speakers, not in the words themselves. May such a malediction be upon us, and upon our children! It follows: But we are Moses’ disciples. We know that God  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You think sight less evidence than hearing; for what you say, you know, is what you have heard from your fathers. But is not He more worthy of belief, who has certified that He comes from God, by mi | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As yet however He speaks as one but just anointed, or God hears sinners too. Else in vain would the publican cry, God be merciful to me a sinner. By that confession he obtained justification, as the | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, that God hears not sinners, means, that God does not enable sinners to work miracles. When sinners however implore pardon for their offenses, they are translated from the rank of sinners to that | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe then, when he said above, Whether He be a sinner, I know not, it was not that he spoke in doubt; for here he not only acquits him of all sin, but holds him up as one well pleasing to God: Bu | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Freely, steadfastly, truly. For how could what our Lord did, be done by any other than God, or by disciples even, except when their Lord dwelt in them? | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then because speaking the truth he was in nothing confounded, when they should most have admired, they condemned him: You were altogether born in sins, and do you teach us? | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What means altogether? That he was quite blind. Yet He who opened his eyes, also saves him altogether. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, altogether, that is to say, from your birth you are in sins. They reproach his blindness, and pronounce his sins to be the cause of it; most unreasonably. So long as they expected him to deny th | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:34 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was they themselves who had made him teacher; themselves, who had asked him so many questions; and now they ungratefully cast him out for teaching. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): It is commonly the way with great persons to disdain learning any thing from their inferiors. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Those who suffer for the truth’s sake, and confession of Christ, come to greatest honor; as we see in the instance of the blind man. For the Jews cast him out of the temple, and the Lord of the temp | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If any mere confession whatsoever of Christ were the perfection of faith, it would have been said, Do you believe in Christ? But inasmuch as all heretics would have had this name in their mouths, co | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But the blind man did not yet know Christ, for before he went to Christ he was blind, and after his cure, he was taken hold of by the Jews: He answered and said, Who is He, Lord, that I might believ | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This He says to remind him of his cure, which had given him the power to see. And observe, He that speaks is born of Mary, and the son is the Son of God, not two different Persons, according to the  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): First, He washes the face of his heart. Then, his heart’s face being washed, and his conscience cleansed, he acknowledges Him as not only the Son of man, which he believed before, but as the Son of Go | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . An example to us, not to pray to God with uplifted neck, but prostrate upon earth, suppliantly to implore His mercy. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He adds the deed to the word, as a clear acknowledgment of fix. His divine power. The Lord replies in a way to confirm His faith, and at the same time stirs up the minds of His followers: And Jesus  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The day then was divided between light and darkness. So it is rightly added, that they which see not, may see; for He relieved men from darkness. But what is that which follows: And that they which  | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, for judgment, He said; i.e. for greater punishment, showing that they who condemned Him, were the very ones who were condemned. Respecting what He says, that they which see not might see, and th | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, Lo, he that saw not from his birth, now sees both in body and soul; whereas they who seem to see, hare had their understanding darkened. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For or there is a twofold vision, and a twofold blindness; viz. that of sense, and that of the understanding. But they were intent only on sensible things, and were ashamed only of sensible blindnes | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Overlooking the miracle wrought on the blind man, you deserve no pardon; since even visible miracles make no impression on you. | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What then they thought their great praise, He shows would turn to their punishment; and at the same time consoles him who had been afflicted with bodily blindness from his birth. For it is not witho | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 9:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, if you were blind, i.e. ignorant of the Scriptures, your offense would be by no means so heavy a one, as erring out of ignorance: but now, seeing you call yourselves wise and understanding in th | src: CAJohn.htm#9
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having reproached the Jews with blindness, they might have said, We are not blind, but we avoid you as a deceiver. Our Lord therefore gives the marks which distinguish a robber and deceiver | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Many go under the name of good men according to the standard of the world, and observe in some sort the commandments of the Law, who yet are not Christians. And these generally boast of the | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You have seen His description of a robber, now see that of the Shepherd: But he that enters in by the door is the shepherd of the sheep. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He enters by the door, who enters by Christ, who imitates the suffering of Christ, who is acquainted with the humility of Christ, so as to feel and know, that if God became man for us, man should no | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The porter perhaps is Moses; for to him the oracles of God were committed. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, the Holy Spirit is the porter, by whom the Scriptures are unlocked, and reveal the truth to us. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, the porter is our Lord Himself; for there is much less difference between a door and a porter, than between a door and a shepherd. And He has called Himself both the door and the shepherd. Why t | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As they had called Him a deceiver, and appealed to their own unbelief as the proof of it; (Which of the rulers believes in Him?) He shows here that it was because they refused to hear Him, that they | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , He knew the names of the predestinated; as He said to His disciples, Rejoice that your names are written in heaven. And leads them out. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He led out the sheep, when He sent them not out of the reach of, but into the midst of, the wolves. There seems to he a secret allusion to the blind man. He called him out of the midst of the Jews;  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And who is He who leads them out, but the Same who loosens the chain of their sins, that they may follow Him with free unfettered step? | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And when He puts forth His own sheep, He goes before them, He leads them out from the darkness of ignorance into light, while He goes before in the pillar of cloud, and fire. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Shepherds always go behind their sheep; but He, on the contrary, goes before, to show that He would lead all to the truth. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And who is this that goes before the sheep, but He who being raised from the dead, dies no more; and who said, Father, I will also that they, whom You have given Me, be with Me where I am? And the s | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The strangers are Theudas, and Judas, and the false apostles who came after Christ. That He might not appear one of this number, He gives many marks of difference between Him and them. First, Christ | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:5 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He alludes to Antichrist, who shall deceive for a time, but lose all his followers when he dies. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:6 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But here is a difficulty. Sometimes they who are not sheep hear Christ’s voice; for Judas heard, who was a wolf. And sometimes the sheep hear Him not; for they who crucified Christ heard not; yet so | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord feeds by plain words, exercises by obscure. For when two persons, one godly, the other ungodly, hear the words of the Gospel, and they happen to be such that neither can understand them; on | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, to waken the attention of the Jews, unfolds the meaning of what He has said; Then said Jesus to them again, Verily, verily, I say to you, I am the door of the sheep. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo, the very door which He had shut up, He opens; He is the Door: let us enter, and let us enter with joy. All that ever came before Me are thieves and robbers. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said not this of the Prophets, as the heretics think, but of Theudas, and Judas, and other agitators. So he adds in praise of the sheep, The sheep heard them not; but he no where praises those wh | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Understand, All that ever came at variance with Me. The Prophets were not at variance with Him. They came with Him, who came with the Word of God, who spoke the truth. He, the Word, the Truth, sent  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, The sheep hear not them, but Me they hear; for I am the Door, and whoever enters by Me not falsely but in sincerity, shall by perseverance be saved. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The door admits the sheep into the pasture; And shall go in and out, and find pasture. What is this pasture, but the happiness to come, the rest to which our Lord brings us? | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is this, shall go in and out? To enter into the Church by Christ the Door, is a very good thing, but to go out of the Church is not. Going in must refer to inward cogitation; going out to outwa | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, to go in is to watch over the inner man; to go out, to mortify the outward man, i.e. our members which are upon the earth. He that does this shall find pasture in the life to come. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He refers to the Apostles who went in and out boldly; for they became the masters of the world, none could turn them out of their kingdom, and they found pasture. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But He Himself explains it more satisfactorily to me in what follows: The thief comes not, but for to steal, and for to kill: I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more ab | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Shall go in, i.e. to faith: shall go out, i.e. to sight: and find pasture, i.e. in eternal fullness. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The thief comes not but for to steal, and to kill. As if He said, And well may the sheep not hear the voice of the thief; for he comes not but for to steal: he usurps another’s office, forming his f | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The thief comes not but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy; this was literally fulfilled in the case of those movers of sedition, whose followers were nearly all destroyed; deprived by the th | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, the thief is the devil, steals by wicked thoughts, kills by the assent of the mind to them, and destroys by acts. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord has acquainted us with two things which were obscure before; first, that He is the Door; and now again, that He is the Shepherd: I am the good Shepherd. Above He said that the shepherd ente | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And He adds what that goodness is, for our imitation: The good Shepherd gives His life for the sheep. He did what He bade, He set the example of what He commanded: He laid down His life for the shee | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ was not the only one who ho did this. And yet if they who did it are members of Him, one and the same Christ did it always. He was able to do it without them; they were not without Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All these however were good shepherds, not because they shed their blood, but because they did it for the sheep. For they shed it not in pride, but in love. Should any among the heretics suffer trou | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord shows here that He did not undergo His passion unwillingly; but for the salvation of the world. He then gives the difference between the shepherd and the hireling: But he that is an hirelin | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some there are who love earthly possessions more than the sheep, and do not deserve the name of a shepherd. He who feeds the Lord’s flock for the sake of temporal hire, and not for love, is an hirel | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He seeks therefore in the Church, not God, but something else. If he sought God he would be chaste; for the soul has but one lawful husband, God. Whoever seeks from God any thing beside God, seeks u | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But whether a man be a shepherd or an hireling, cannot be told for certain, except in a time of trial. In tranquil times, the hireling generally stands watch like the shepherd. But when the wolf com | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The wolf is the devil, and they that follow him; according to Matthew, Which come to you in sheep’s’ clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo, the wolf has seized a sheep by the throat, the devil has enticed a man into adultery. The sinner must be excommunicated But if he is excommunicated, he will be an enemy, he will plot, he will do | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The wolf too comes upon the sheep, whenever any spoiler and unjust person oppresses the humble believers. And he who seems to be shepherd, but leaves the sheep and flees, is he who dares not to resi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if the Apostles were shepherds, not hirelings, why did they flee in persecution? And why did our Lord say, When they persecute you in this city, flee you into another? Let us knock, then will co | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A servant of Christ, and minister of His Word and Sacraments, may flee from city to city, when he is specially aimed at by the persecutors, apart from his brethren; so that his flight does not leave | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . On the good side are the door, the porter, the shepherd, and the sheep; on the bad, the thieves, the robbers, the hirelings, the wolf. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:13 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must love the shepherd, beware of the wolf, tolerate the hireling. For the hireling is useful so long as he sees not the wolf, the thief, and the robber. When he sees them, he flees. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Indeed he would not be a hireling, did he not receive wages from the hirer. Sons wait patiently for the eternal inheritance of their father; the hireling looks eagerly for the temporal wages from hi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Two evil persons have been mentioned, one that kills, and robs the sheep, another that does not hinder: the one standing for those movers of seditions; the other for the rulers of the Jews, who did  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, I love My sheep, and they love and follow Me. For he who loves not the truth, is as yet very far from knowing it. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hence the difference of the hireling and the Shepherd. The hireling does not know his sheep, because he sees them so little. The Shepherd knows His sheep, because He is so attractive to them. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then that you may not attribute to the Shepherd and the sheep the same measure of knowledge, He adds, As the Father knows Me, even so know I the Father: i.e. I know Him as certainly as He knows Me.  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And I lay down My life for My sheep. As if to say, This is why I know My Father, and am known by the Father, because I lay down My life for My sheep; i.e. by My love for My sheep, to show how much I | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He gives it too as a proof of His authority. In the same way the Apostle maintains his own commission in opposition to the false Apostles, by enumerating his dangers and sufferings. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the deceivers did not expose their lives for the sheep, but, like hirelings, deserted their followers. Our Lord, on the other hand, protected His disciples: Let these go their way. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But as He came to redeem not only the Jews, but the Gentiles, He adds, And other sheep I have, which are not of this fold. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sheep hitherto spoken of are those of the stock of Israel according to the flesh. But there were others of the stock of Israel, according to faith, Gentiles, who were as yet out of the fold; pre | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What wonder that these should hear My voice, and follow Me, when others are waiting to do the same. Both these flocks are dispersed, and without shepherds; for it follows, And they shall hear My voi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Of two flocks He makes one fold, uniting the Jews and Gentiles in His faith. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For there is one sign of baptism for all, and one Shepherd, even the Word of God. Let the Manichean mark; there is but one fold and one Shepherd set forth both in the Old and New Testaments. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What does He mean then when He says, I am not sent but to the lost sheep of the house of Israel? Only, that whereas He manifested Himself personally to the Jews, He did not go Himself to the Gentile | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The word must here (I must bring) does not signify necessity, but only that the thing would take place. Therefore does My Father love Me, because I lay down My life, that I might take it again. They | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. Because I die, to rise again. There is great force in, I lay down. Let not the Jews, He says, boast; rage they may, but if I should not choose to lay down My life, what will they do by raging? | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father does not bestow His love on the Son as a reward for the death He suffered in our behalf; but He loves Him, as beholding in the Begotten His own essence, whence proceeded such love for man | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He says, in condescension to our weakness, Though there were nothing else which made Me love you, this would, that you are so loved by My Father, that, by dying for you, I shall win His love. Not | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein He showed that His natural death was not the consequence of sin in Him, but of His own simple will, which was the why, the when, and the how: I have power to lay it down. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As they had often plotted to kill Him, He tells them their efforts will be useless, unless He is willing. I have such power over My own life, that no one can take it from Me, against My will. This i | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He only means His perfect agreement with His Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the Word does not receive a command by word, but contains in Himself all the Father’s commandments. When the Son is said to receive what He possesses of Himself, His power is not lessened, but o | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After declaring Himself the Master of His own life and death, which was a lofty assumption, He makes a more humble confession; thus wonderfully uniting both characters; showing that He was neither i | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How does our Lord lay down His own life? Christ is the Word, and man, i.e. in soul and body. Does the Word lay down His life, and take it again; or does the human soul, or does the flesh? If it was  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But the light shined in darkness, and the darkness comprehended it not. There was a division among the Jews for these sayings. And many of them said, He has a devil, and is mad. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because He spoke as one greater than man, they said He had a devil. But that He had not a devil, others proved from His works: Others said, These are not the words of Him that has a devil. Can a dev | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We have heard of the patience of God, and of salvation preached amid revilings. They obstinately preferred tempting Him to obeying Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And it was at Jerusalem the feast of the dedication. Encænia is the feast of the dedication of the temple; from the Greek word signifying new. The dedication of any thing new was called encænia. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was the feast of the dedication of the temple, after the return from the Babylonish captivity. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, it was in memory of the dedication under Judas Maccabeus. The first dedication was that of Solomon in the autumn; the second that of Zorobabel, and the priest Jesus in the spring. This was in wi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Judas Maccabeus instituted an annual commemoration of this dedication. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist mentions the time of winter, to show that it was near His passion. He suffered in the following spring; for which reason He took up His abode at Jerusalem. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or because the season of cold was in keeping with the cold malicious hearts of the Jews. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ was present with much zeal at this feast, and thenceforth stayed in Judea; His passion being now at hand. And Jesus walked in the temple in Solomon’s porch. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is called Solomon’s porch, because Solomon went to pray there. The porches of a temple are usually named after the temple. If the Son of God walked in a temple where the flesh of brute animals wa | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Be you also careful, in the winter time, i.e. while yet in this stormy wicked world, to celebrate the dedication of your spiritual temple, by ever renewing yourself, ever rising upward in heart. The | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews cold in love, burning in their malevolence, approached Him not to honor, but persecute. Then came the Jews round about Him, and said to Him, How long do you make us to doubt? If You be the  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Being able to find no fault with His works, they tried to catch Him in His words. And mark their perversity. When He instructs by His discourse, they say, What sign show You? When He demonstrates by | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They accuse Him of keeping their minds in suspense and uncertainty, who had come to save their souls. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They wanted our Lord to say, I am the Christ. Perhaps, as they had human notions of the Messiah, having failed to discern His divinity in the Prophets they wanted Christ to confess Himself the Messi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And thus they intended to give Him into the hands of the Proconsul for punishment, as an usurper against the emperor. Our Lord so managed His reply as to stop the mouths of His calumniators, open th | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He reproves their malice, for pretending that a single word would convince them, whom so many words had not. If you do not believe My works, He says, how will you believe My words? And He adds why t | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He saw that they were persons predestinated to eternal death, and not those for whom He had bought eternal life, at the price of His blood. The sheep believe, and follow the Shepherd. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After He had said, You are not of My sheep, He exhorts them to become such: My sheep hear My voice. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. Obey My precepts from the heart. And I know them, and they follow Me, here by walking in gentleness and innocence, hereafter by entering the joys of eternal life And I give to them eternal life | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is the pasture of which He spoke before And shall find pasture. Eternal life is called a goodly pasture: the grass thereof wither not, all is spread with verdure. But these cavilers thought onl | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how then did Judas perish? Because he did not continue to the end. Christ speaks of them who persevere. If any sheep is separated from the flock, and wanders from the Shepherd, it incurs danger  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And He adds why they do not perish: Neither shall any man pluck them out of My hand. Of those sheep of which it is said, The Lord knows them that are His, the wolf robs none, the thief takes none, t | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is the speech of conscious power. Yet to show, that though of the Divine nature He has His nativity from God, He adds, My Father which gave Me them is greater than all. He does not conceal His  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Son, born from everlasting of the Father, God from God, has not equality with the Father by growth, but by birth. This is that greater than all which the Father gave Him b; viz. to be His Word,  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The hand of the Son is spoken of as the hand of the Father, to let you see, by a bodily representation, that both have the same nature, that the nature and virtue of the Father is in the Son also. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then that you may not suppose that the Father’s power protects the sheep, while He is Himself too weak to do so, He adds, I and My Father are one. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mark both those words, one and are, and you will be delivered from Scylla and Charybdis. In that He says, one the Arian, in we are the Sabellian, is answered. There are both Father and Son. And if o | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We are one. What He is, that am I, in respect of essence, not of relation. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The heretics, since they cannot gainsay these words, endeavor by an impious lie to explain them away. They maintain that this unity is unanimity only; a unity of will, not of nature, i.e. that the t | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At this speech, I and My Father are one, the Jews could not restrain their rage, but ran to take up stones, after their hardhearted way: Then the Jews took up stones again to stone Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The heretics now, as unbelieving and rebellious against our Lord in heaven, show their impious hatred by the stones, i.e. the words they cast at Him; as if they would drag Him down again from His th | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord remonstrates with them; Many good works have I showed you from My Father, strewing that they had no just reason for their anger. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Healing of the sick, teaching, miracles. He showed them of the Father, because He sought His Father’s glory in all of them. For which of these works do you stone Me? They confess, though reluctantly | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is their answer to the speech, I and My Father are one. Lo, the Jews understood what the Arians understand not. For they are angry, for this very reason, that they could not conceive but that b | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jew said, You being a man, the Arian, you being a creature: but both say, You make Yourself God. The Arian supposes a God of a new and different substance a God of another kind, or not a God at  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord did not correct the Jews, as if they misunderstood His speech, but confirmed and defended it, in the very sense in which they had taken it. Jesus answered them, Is it not written in your la | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. the Law given to you, I have said, you are Gods? God saith this by the Prophet in the Psalm. Our Lord calls all those Scriptures the Law generally, though elsewhere He spiritually distinguishes | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Before proving that He and His Father are one, He answers the absurd and foolish charge brought against Him, that He being man made Himself God. When the Law applied this title to holy men, and the  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or sanctified, i.e. in begetting, gave Him holiness, begat Him holy. If men to whom the word of God came were called gods, much more the Word of God Himself is God. If men by partaking of the word o | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, sanctified, i.e. set apart to be sacrificed for the world: a proof that He was God in a higher sense than the rest. To save the world is a divine work, not that of a man made divine by grace. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, we must consider this a speech of humility, made to conciliate men. After it he leads them to higher things; If I do not the works of My Father, believe Me not; which is as much as to say, that  | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:38 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What place has adoption, or the mere conception of a name then, that we should not believe Him to be the Son of God by nature, when He tells us to believe Him to be the Son of God, because the Fathe | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Son does not say, The Father is in Me, and I in Him, in the sense in which men who think and act aright may say the like; meaning that they partake of God’s grace, and are enlightened by His Spi | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews still persist in their madness; Therefore they sought again to take Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To lay hold of Him, not by faith and the understanding, but with blood thirsty violence. Do you so lay hold of Him, that you may have sure hold; they would fain have laid hold on Him, but they could | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ, after discoursing on some high truth, commonly retires immediately, to give time to the fury of people to abate, during His absence. Thus He did now: He went away again beyond Jordan, into t | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was followed there by many: And many resorted to Him, and said, John did no miracle. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Did not cast out devils, did not give sight to the blind, did not raise the dead. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mark their reasoning, John did no miracle, but this Man did; wherefore He is the superior. But lest the absence of miracles should lessen the weight of John’s testimony, they add, But all things tha | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These laid hold of Him while abiding, not, like the Jews, when departing. Let us approach by the candle to the day. John is the candle, and gave testimony to the day. | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 10:42 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We may observe that our Lord often brings out the people into solitary places, thus ridding them of the society of the unbelieving, for their furtherance in the faith: just as He led the people into | src: CAJohn.htm#10
John 11:5 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After our Lord had departed to the other side of Jordan, it happened that Lazarus fell sick: A certain man was sick, named Lazarus, of Bethany. In some copies the copulative conjunction precedes, to | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The resurrection of Lazarus is more spoken of than any of our Lord’s miracles. But if we hear in mind who He was who wrought this miracle, we shall feel not so much of wonder; as of delight. He who  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And as there were many women of this name, He distinguishes her by her well-known act: It was that Mary which anointed the Lord with ointment, and wiped His feet with her hair, whose brother Lazarus | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . First we are to observe that this was not the harlot mentioned in Luke, but an honest woman, who treated our Lord with marked reverence. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John here confirms the passage in Luke, where this is said to have taken place in the house of one Simon a Pharisee: Mary had done this act therefore on a former occasion. That she did it again at B | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A cruel sickness had seized Lazarus; a wasting fever was eating away the body of the wretched man day by day: his two sisters sat sorrowful at his bedside, grieving for the sick youth continually. T | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They did not say, Come and heal; they dared not say, Speak the word there, and it shall be done here; but only, Behold, he whom you love is sick. As if to say, It is enough that you know it, you are | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They hope to excite Christ’s pity by these words, Whom as yet they thought to be a man only. Like the centurion and nobleman, they sent, not went, to Christ; partly from their great faith in Him, fo | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because they were women and it did not become them to leave their home if they could help it. Great devotion and faith is expressed in these words, Behold, he whom you love is sick. Such was the | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When Jesus heard that, He said, This sickness is not to death. For this death itself was not to death, but to give occasion for a miracle; whereby men might be brought to believe in Christ, and so e | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That here signifies not the cause, but the event. The sickness sprang from natural causes, but He turned it to the glory of God. Now Jesus loved Martha, and her sister, and Lazarus. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is sick, they sorrowful, all beloved. Wherefore they had hope, for they were beloved by Him Who is the Comforter of the sorrowful, and the Healer of the sick. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein the Evangelist instructs us not to be sad, it sickness ever falls upon good men, and friends of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord heard of the sickness of Lazarus, but suffered four days to pass before He cured it; that the recovery might be a more wonderful one. When He had heard therefore that he was sick, He abode  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To give time for his death and burial, that they might say, he stinks, and none doubt that it was death, and not a trance, from which he was raised. Then after that; He says to His disciples, let us | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Where He had just escaped being stoned; for this was the cause of His leaving. He left indeed as man: He left in weakness, but He returns in power. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had not as yet told His disciples where He was going; but now He tells them, in order to prepare them beforehand, for they are in great alarm, when they hear of it: His disciples say to Him, Mast | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When men presumed to give advice to God, disciples to their Master, our Lord rebuked them: Jesus answered, Are there not twelve hours in the day? He showed Himself to be the day, by appointing twelv | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:10 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, the upright need fear no evil: the wicked only have cause to fear. We have done nothing worthy of death, and therefore are in no danger. Or, If any one sees this world’s light, he is s | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some understand the day to be the time preceding the Passion, the night to be the Passion. In this sense, while it is day, would mean, before My Passion; You will not stumble before My Passion, beca | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After He had comforted His disciples in one way, He comforts them in another, by telling them that they were not going to Jerusalem, but to Bethany: These things says He and after that He says to th | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was really true that he was sleeping. To our Lord, he was sleeping; to men who could not raise him again, he was dead. Our Lord awoke him with as much ease from his grave, as you awake a sleeper  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples however wished to prevent Him going to Judea: Then said His disciples, Lord, if he sleep, he shall do well. Sleep is a good sign in sickness. And therefore if he sleep, say they, what  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples replied, as they understood Him: Although Jesus spoke of his death; but they thought that He had spoken of taking rest in sleep. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if anyone say, that the disciples could not but have known that our Lord meant Lazarus’s death, when He said, that I may awake him; because it would have been absurd to have gone such a distance | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then declares His meaning openly: Then said Jesus to them plainly, Lazarus is dead. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But He does not add here, I go that I may awake him. He did not wish to anticipate the miracle by talking of it; a hint to us to shun vain glory, and abstain from empty promises. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had been sent for to restore Lazarus from sickness, not from death. But how could the death be hid from Him, into whose hands the soul of the dead had flown? And I am glad for your sakes that I w | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some have understood this place thus. I rejoice, He says, for your sakes; for if I had been there, I should have only cured a sick man; which is but an inferior sign of power. But since in My absenc | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples, all dreaded the Jews; end especially Thomas; Then said Thomas, which is called Didymus, to his fellow-disciples, Let us also go, that we may die with him. But he who was now the most  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples, checked by our Lord’s answer to them, dared no longer oppose; and Thomas, more forward than the rest, says, Let us also go that we may die with him. What an appearance of firmness! He | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord delayed His coming for four days, that the resurrection of Lazarus might be the more glorious: Then when Jesus came, He found that He had lain in the grave four days already. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Our Lord had stayed two days, and the messenger had come the day before; the very day on which Lazarus died. This brings us to the fourth day. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Of the four days many things may be said. They refer to one thing, but one thing viewed in different ways. There is one day of death which the law of our birth brings upon us. Men transgress the nat | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The first sin was elation of heart, the second assent, the third act, the fourth habit. Now Bethany was nigh to Jerusalem, about fifteen furlongs off. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Two miles. This is mentioned to account for so many coming from Jerusalem: And many of the Jews came to Martha and Mary, to comfort them concerning their brother. But how could the Jews be consoling | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord had not yet entered the town, when Martha met Him: Then Martha, as soon as she heard that Jesus was t coming, went and met Him: but Mary sat still in the house. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Martha does not take her sister with her, because she wants to speak with Christ alone, and tell Him what has happened. When her hopes had been raised by Him, then she went her way, and called Mary. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At first she does not tell her sister, for fear, if she came, the Jews, present might accompany her. And she did not wish them to know of our Lord’s coming. Then says Martha to Jesus, Lord, if You h | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She believed in Christ, but she believed not as she ought. She did not speak as if He were God: If You had been here, my brother had not died. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She did not know that He could have restored her brother as well absent as present. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor did she know that He wrought His miracles by His own independent power: But I know that even now, whatsoever You will ask of God, God will give it to you. She only thinks Him some very gifted ma | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She does not say to Him, Bring my brother to life again; for how could she know that it would be good for him to come to life again; she says, I know that You can do so, if You will but what You wil | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But our Lord taught her the truths which she did not know: Jesus says to her your brother shall rise again. Observe, He does not say, I will ask God, that he may rise again, nor on the other hand do | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Shall rise again, is ambiguous: for He does not say, now. And therefore it follows: Martha says to Him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection at the last day; of that resurrection I am | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She had often heard Christ speak of the resurrection. Jesus now declares His power more plainly: Jesus said to her, I am the resurrection and the life. He needed therefore none to help Him; for if H | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I am the resurrection, because I am the life; as through Me he will rise at the general resurrection, through Me he may rise now. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To Martha’s, Whatsoever You shall ask, He replies, He that believes in Me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: showing her that He is the Giver of all good, and that we must ask of Him. Thus He  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that believes in Me, though he were dead: i.e. though his flesh die, his soul shall live till the flesh rise again, never to die more. For faith is the life of the soul. And whomsoever lives, in  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because He has attained to the life of the Spirit, and to an immortal resurrection. Our Lord, from Whom nothing was hid, knew that she believed, but sought from her a confession to salvation: Do you | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She seems not to have understood His words; i.e. she saw that He meant something great, but did not see what that was. She is asked one thing, and answers another. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): When I believed that You were the Son of God, I believed that you were the resurrection, that You were life, and that he that believes in you, though he were dead, shall live. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ’s words had the effect of stopping Martha’s grief. In her devotion to her Master she had no time to think of her afflictions: And when she had so said, she went her way, and called Mary her s | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Silently, i.e. speaking in a low voice. For she did speak, saying, the Master is come, and calls for you. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She calls her sister secretly, in order not to let the Jews know that Christ was coming. For had they known, they would have gone, and not been witnesses of the miracle. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We may observe that the Evangelist has not said, where, or when, or how, the Lord called Mary, but for brevity’s sake has left it to be gathered from Martha’s words. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perhaps she thought the presence of Christ in itself a call, as if it were inexcusable, when Christ came, that she should not go out to meet Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While the rest sat around her in her sorrow, she did not wait for the Master to come to her, but, not letting her grief detain her, rose immediately to meet Him; As soon as she heard that, she arose | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So we see, if she had known of His arrival before, she would not have let Martha go without her. Now Jesus was not yet come into the town, but was in that place where Martha met Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He went slowly that He might not seem to catch at an occasion of working a miracle, but to have it forced upon Him by others asking Mary, it is said, arose quickly, and thus anticipated His coming.  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist mentions this to show how it was that so many were present at Lazarus’ resurrection, and witness of that great miracle. Then when Mary was come where Jesus was, and saw Him, she fell  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She is more fervent than her sister. Forgetful of the crowd around her, and of the Jews, some of whom were enemies to Christ, she threw herself at her Master’s feet. In His presence all earthly thin | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But her faith seems as yet imperfect: Lord, if You had been here, my brother had not died. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, Lord, while You were with us, no disease, no sickness dared to show itself, amongst those with whom the Life deigned to take up His abode. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O faithless assembly! While You are yet in the world, Lazarus your friend dies! If the friend cries, what will the enemy suppose? Is it a small thing that they will not serve You upon earth? Lo, hel | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary did not say so much as Martha, she could not bring out what she wanted for weeping, as is usual with persons overwhelmed with sorrow. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ did not answer Mary, as He had her; sister, on account of the people present. In condescension to them He humbled Himself, and let His human nature be seen, in order to gain them as witnesses | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For who but Himself could trouble Him? Christ was troubled, because it pleased Him to be troubled; He hungered, because it pleased Him to hunger. It was in His own power to be affected in this or th | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To prove His human nature He sometimes gives it free vent, while at other times He commands, and restrains it by, the power of the Holy Ghost. Our Lord allows His nature to be affected in these ways | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And said, Where have you laid him? He knew where but He asked to try the faith of the people. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not wish to thrust the miracle upon them, but to make them ask for it, and thus do away with all suspicions. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The question has an allusion too to our hidden calling. That, predestination by which we are called, is hidden; and the sign of its being so is our Lord asking the question. He being as it were in i | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He had not yet raised anyone from the dead; and seemed as if He came to weep, not to raise to life. Wherefore they say to Him, Come and see. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Lord sees when He pities, as we read, Look upon my adversity and misery, and forgive me all my sin. Jesus wept. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because He was the fountain of pity. He wept in His human nature for him whom He was able to raise again by His divine. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherefore did Christ weep, but to teach men to weep? | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is customary to mourn over the death of friends; and thus the Jews explained our Lord’s weeping: Then said the Jews, Behold how He loved him. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Loved him. Our Lord came not to call the righteous but sinners to repentance. And some of them said, Could not this Man which opened the eyes of the blind, have caused that even this man should not  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was His enemies who said this. The very works, which should have evidenced His power, they turn against Him, as if He had not really done them. This is the way that they speak of the miracle of o | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And do you too groan in yourself, if you would rise to new life. To every man is this said, who is weighed down by any vicious habit. It was a cave, and a stone lay upon it. The dead under the stone | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A cave is a hollow in a rock. It is called a monument, because it reminds us of the dead. Jesus said, Take away the stone. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did He not raise him without taking away the stone? Could not He who moved a dead body by His voice, much more have moved a stone? He purposely did not do so, in order that the miracle might | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Take away the stone; mystically, take away the burden of the law, proclaim grace. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perhaps those are signified who wished to impose the rite of circumcision on the Gentile converts; or men in the Church of corrupt life, who offend believers. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary and Martha, the sisters of Lazarus, though they had often seen Christ raise the dead, did not fully believe that He could raise their brother; Martha, the sister of him that was dead, said to H | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Martha said this from weakness of faith, thinking it impossible that Christ could raise her brother, so long after death. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, these are not words of despair, but of wonder. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus everything tends to stop the mouths of the unbelieving. Their hands take away the stone, their ears hear Christ’s voice, their eyes see Lazarus come forth, they perceive the smell of the dead b | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ reminds Martha of what He had told her before, which she had forgotten: Jesus said to her, Said I to you, that, if you would believe, you should see the glory of God? | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She did not remember what He said above, He that believes in Me, though he were dead, yet shall he live. To the disciples He had said That the Son of God might be glorified thereby; here it is the g | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:41 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Herein is the glory of God, that he that stinks and has been dead four days, is brought to life again. Then they took away the stone. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The delay in taking away the stone was caused by the sister of the dead, who said, By this time he stinks, for he has been dead four days. If she had not said this, it would not be said, Jesus said, | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ, as man, being inferior to the Father, prays to Him for Lazarus’s resurrection; and declares that He is heard: And Jesus lifted up His eyes, and said, Father, I thank You that You have heard  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He lifted up His eyes; mystically, He lifted up the human mind by prayer to the Father above. We should pray after Christ’s pattern, Lift up the eyes of our heart, and raise them above present thing | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. There is no difference of will between Me and You. You have heard Me, does not show any lack of power in Him, or that He is inferior to the Father. It is a phrase that is used between friends a | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not therefore need to pray: He prayed for our sakes, that we might know Him to be the Son: But because use of the people which stand by I said it, that they may believe that You have sent Me. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not say, That they may believe that I am inferior to You, in that I cannot do this without prayer, but, that You have sent Me. He says not, have sent Me weak, acknowledging subjection, doing  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ went to the grave in which Lazarus slept, as if He were not dead, but alive and able to hear, for He forthwith called him out of his grave. And when He had thus spoken, He cried with a loud v | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, Arise, but, Come forth, speaking to the dead as if he were alive. For which reason also He does not say, Come forth in My Father’s name, or, Father, raise him, but throwing off the  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The voice which roused Lazarus, is the symbol of that trumpet which will sound at the general resurrection. (He spoke loud, to contradict the Gentile fable, that the soul remained in the tomb. The s | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His cry and loud voice it was which awoke him, as Christ had said, I go to awake him. The resurrection of Lazarus is the work of the Father also, in that He heard the prayer of the Son. It is the jo | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He came forth bound, that none might suspect that he was a mere phantom. Besides, that this very fact, viz. of coming forth bound, was itself a miracle, as great as the resurrection. Jesus said to t | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord had said above, Because of the people that stand by I said it, that they may believe that You have sent Me. It would have been ignorance of the future, if He had said this, and none believe | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Although according to the Gospel history, we hold that Lazarus was really raised to life, yet I doubt not that his resurrection is an allegory as well. We do not, because we allegorize facts, lose o | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Everyone that sins, dies; but God, of His great mercy, raises the soul to life again, and does not suffer it to die eternally. The three miraculous resurrections in the Gospels, understand to testif | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The maiden is restored to life in the house, the young man outside the gate, Lazarus in his grave. She that lies dead in the house, is the sinner dying in sin: he that is carried out by the gate is  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, it is death within; when the evil thought has not come out into action. But if you actually do the evil thing, you have as it were carried the dead outside the gate. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And one there is who lies dead in his grave, with a load of earth upon him; i.e. who is weighed down by habits of sin. But the Divine grace has regard even to such, and enlightens them. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or we may take Lazarus in the grave as the soul laden with earthly sins. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And yet our Lord loved Lazarus. For had He not loved sinners, He would never have come down from heaven to save them. Well is it said of one of sinful habits, that He stinks. He has a bad report alr | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Well may she say, He has been dead four days For the earth is the last of the elements. It signifies the pit of earthly sins, i.e. carnal lusts. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Lord groaned, wept, cried with a loud voice. It is hard for Him to arise who is bowed down with the weight of evil habits. Christ troubles Himself, to signify to you that you should be troubled, | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lazarus is bid to come forth, i.e. to come forth and condemn himself with his own mouth, without excuse or reservation: that so he that lies buried in a guilty conscience, may come forth out of hims | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That Lazarus came forth from the grave, signifies the soul’s deliverance from carnal sins. That he came bound up in grave clothes means, that even we who are delivered from carnal things, and serve  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: When you despise, you lie dead; when you confess, you come forth. For what is to come forth, but to go out, as it were, of your hiding place, and show yourself? But you cannot make this con | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:46 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ awakes, because His power it is which quickens us inwardly: the disciples loose, because by the ministry of the priesthood, they who are quickened are absolved. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By those who went and told the Pharisees, are meant those who seeing the good works of God’s servants, hate them on that very account, persecute, and calumniate them. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Such a miracle as this should have drawn forth wonder and praise. But they make it a reason of plotting against His life: Then gathered the chief priests and, Pharisees a council, and said, What do  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): ; But they had no thought of believing. The miserable men only consulted how they might hurt and kill Him, not how themselves might be saved from death. What do we? For this Man does many miracles. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Him of whose divinity they had received such certain proofs, they call only a man. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This speech is an evidence of their audacity and blindness: of their audacity, because they testified that He had done many miracles, and yet thought that they could contend successfully against Him | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They say this to alarm the people; as if they were incurring the suspicion of setting up an usurper. If, say they, the Romans in crowds follow Him, they will suspect us of setting up a tyranny, and  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, they were afraid that, if all believed in Christ, none would remain to defend the city of God and the temple against the Romans: since they thought that Christ’s teaching was directed against th | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically: It was fit that the Gentiles should occupy the place of them of the circumcision; because by their fall salvation came to the Gentiles. The Romans represent the Gentiles, being the ruler | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When they hesitated, and asked, What do we? One of them gave most cruel and shameless advice, viz. Caiaphas, who was High Priest that same year. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How is it that he is called the High Priest of that year, when God appointed one hereditary High Priest? This was owing to the ambition and contention of parties among the Jews themselves, which had | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Of this Caiaphas Josephus relates, that he bought the priesthood for a year, for a certain sum. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The character of Caiaphas is strewn by his being called the High Priest of that same year; the year, viz. in which our Savior suffered. Being the High Priest that same year, he said to them, You kno | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said this with a bad intention, yet the Holy Spirit used his mouth as the vehicle of a prophecy: And this spoke he not of himself: but being high priest that year, he prophesied that Jesus should | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not everyone that prophesies is a prophet; as not everyone that does a just action is just, he, for example, that does one for vainglory. Caiaphas prophesied without being a prophet, as did Balaam.  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We learn hence that even bad men may foretell things to come by the spirit of prophecy, which power the Evangelist ascribes to a divine sacrament, he being Pontifex, i.e. High Priest. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See the great virtue of the Holy Spirit, in drawing forth a prophecy from a wicked man. And see too the virtue of the pontifical office, which made him, though an unworthy High Priest, unconsciously | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Caiaphas prophesied of the Jewish nation alone; in which nation were the sheep, of which our Lord says, I am not sent but to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. But the Evangelist knew that there | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His persecutors accomplished this wicked purpose, and put Him to death, thinking to extinguish the devotion of His followers; but faith grew from the very thing which these cruel and unbelieving men | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:53 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Inflamed by the speech of Caiaphas, they determined on killing our Lord: Then from that day forth they took counsel together to put Him to death. Was this then the work of the Holy Spirit, as well a | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They sought before to kill Him; now their resolution was confirmed. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): After this resolution of the Chief Priests and Pharisees, Jesus was more cautious in strewing Himself among the Jews, and retired to remote parts, and avoided populous places: Jesus therefore walked n | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Not that His power had failed Him; for, had He pleased He might still have walked openly among the Jews, and they done nothing to Him. But He wished to show the disciples, by His own example, that bel | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is praiseworthy, when struggles are at hand, not to avoid confession, or refuse to suffer death for the truth’s sake. And it is no less praiseworthy now to avoid giving occasion for such trial. W | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How must it have troubled the disciples to see Him save Himself by merely human means? While all were rejoicing and keeping the feast, they remained hidden, and in danger. Yet they continued with Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, Jesus walked openly among the Jews, when the Word of God used to come to them by the Prophets. But this Word ceased, i.e. Jesus went from there. And He went to that town near the wildern | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who came from heaven to suffer, wished to draw near the place of His Passion, His hour being now at hand: And the Jews’ passover was near at hand. That passover they had resolved to celebrate by  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They went up before the passover, to be purified. For whoever had sinned willingly or unwillingly could not keep the passover, unless they were first purified by washings, fastings, and shaving of t | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They lay in wait for Him at the passover, and made the feast time the time of His death. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherefore the Evangelist does not call it the Lord’s passover, but the Jews’ passover. For then it was that they plotted our Lord’s death. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They sought Jesus with bad intent. We seek Him, standing in God’s temple, mutually encouraging one another, and praying Him to come to our feast, and sanctify us by His presence. | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the common people only had done these things, the Passion would have seemed owing to men’s ignorance; but the Pharisees it is, who order Him to be taken: Now both the chief priests and the Pharis | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, they did not know where He was; they knew that He had gone away. Mystically, they did not know where He was, because, in the place of the divine commandments, they taught the doctrines and  | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 11:57 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let us at least show the Jews where He is; O that they would hear, that they would come to the Church, and take hold of Him for themselves! | src: CAJohn.htm#11
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the time approached in which our Lord had resolved to suffer, He approached the place which He had chosen for the scene of His suffering: Then Jesus six days before the passover came to Bethany.  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . On the tenth day of the month they took the lamb which was to be sacrificed on the passover, and from that time began the preparation for the feast. Or rather the ninth day of the month, i.e. six da | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He lived, talked, feasted; the truth was established, the unbelief of the Jews confounded. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary did not take part in serving the guests generally, but gave all her attention to our Lord, treating Him not as mere man, but as God: Then took Mary, a pound of spikenard, very costly, and anoin | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The word pistici seems to be the name of some place, from which this precious ointment came. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or pistici means genuine, non-adulterated. She is the woman that was a sinner, who came to our Lord in Simon’s house with the box of ointment. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That she did this on another occasion in Bethany is not mentioned in Luke’s Gospel, but is in the other three. Matthew and Mark say that the ointment was poured on the head, John says, on the feet.  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Remember the Apostle’s words: To the one we are the savor of death unto death; and to the other the savor of life unto life (2 Cor 11:16). | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then said one of His disciples, Judas Iscariot, Simon’s son, which should betray Him, Why was not this ointment sold for three hundred pence, and given to the poor? In the other Gospels it is the di | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He carried it as a servant, he took it out as a thief. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Judas did not perish at the time when he received money from the Jews to betray our Lord. He was already a thief, already lost, and followed our Lord in body, not in heart; wherein we are taught the | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why was a thief entrusted with the bags of the poor? Perhaps it was to give him no excuse of wanting), money, for of this he had enough in the bag for all his desires. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some suppose that Judas had the keeping of the money, as being the lowest kind of service. For that the ministry of money matters ranks below the ministry of doctrine, we know from what the Apostle  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ, with great forbearance, does not rebuke Judas for his thieving, in order to deprive him of all excuse for betraying Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then said Jesus, Let her alone: against the clay of My burying has she kept this: meaning that He was about to die, and that this ointment was suitable for His burial. So to Mary who was not able to | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Again, as if to remind His betrayer, He alludes to His burial; For the poor you have always with you, but Me you have not always: as if He said, I am a burden, a trouble to you; but wait a little, a | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was speaking of His bodily presence; for in respect of His majesty, providence, ineffable and invisible grace, those words are fulfilled, Lo, I am with you always, even to the end of the world (M | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They wished to see with their own eyes him who had been raised from the dead, and thought that Lazarus might bring back a report of the regions below. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the news of this great miracle had spread everywhere, and was supported by such clear evidence, that they could neither suppress or deny the fact, then, The chief priests consulted that they mi | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . No other miracle of Christ excited such rage as this. It was so public, and so wonderful, to see a man walking and talking after he had been dead four days. And the fact was so undeniable. In the ca | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, that He came to Bethany six days before the passover, means, that He who made all things in six days, who created man on the sixth, in the sixth age of the world, the sixth day, the sixt | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The ointment with which Mary anointed the feet of Jesus was justice. It was therefore a pound. It was ointment of spikenard (pistici) too very precious. Greek for faith. Do you seek to do justice? T | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And observe, on the first occasion of her anointing, she anointed His feet only, but now she anoints both His feet and head. The former denotes the beginnings of penitence, the latter the righteousn | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The house was filled with the odor; the world was filled With the good fame. l6. These things understood not his disciples at the first: but when Jesus was glorified, then remembered they that these | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Law enjoined, that on the tenth day of the first month a lamb or a kid should be shut up in the house, and be kept to the fourteenth day of the same month, on the evening of which day it was sac | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See how great was the fruit of His preaching and how large a flock of the lost sheep of the house of Israel heard the voice of their Shepherd: On the next day much people that were come to the feast | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They showed now at last that they thought Him greater than a prophet: And went forth to meet Him, and cried, Hosanna! Blessed is the King of Israel, that comes in the name of the Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hosanna is a simple exclamation, rather indicating some excitement of the mind, than having any particular meaning; like many interjections that we have in Latin. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is a compound of two words; Hosi is shortened into save; Anna a mere exclamation, complete. Blessed is He that comes in the name of the Lord. The name of the Lord here is the name of God the Fath | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is what more than anything made men believe in Christ, viz. the assurance, that He was not opposed to God, that He came from the Father. The words show us the divinity of Christ. Hosanna is, Sa | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It were a small thing to the King eternal to be made a human king. Christ was not the King of Israel, to exact tribute, and command armies, but to direct souls, and bring them to the kingdom of heav | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews, when they called Him King of Israel, dreamed of an earthly king. They expected a king to arise, of more that human greatness, who would deliver them from the government of the Romans. But  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John relates the matter briefly, the other Evangelists are more full. The ass, we read in them, was the foal of an ass on which no man had sat: i.e. the Gentile world, who had not received our Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did this prophetically, to figure the unclean Gentiles being brought into subjection to the Gospel; and also as a fulfillment of prophecy. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This act of our Lord’s is pointed to in the Prophets, though the malignant rulers of the Jews did not see in it any fulfillment of prophecy: As it is written, Fear not, daughter of Sion, behold your | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Whereas they had had wicked kings, who had subjected them to wars, He said to them, Trust Me, I am not such as they, but gentle and mild: which He showed by the manner of His entrance. For  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. When He showed the power of His resurrection, then they remembered that these things were written of Him, and that they had done these things to Him, i.e. those things that were written of Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord had not then revealed these things to them. Indeed it would have been a scandal to them had they known Him to be King at the time of His sufferings. Nor would they have understood the natur | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . See then the consequences of our Lord’s passion. It was not to no purpose that He had reserved His greatest miracle for the last. For the resurrection of Lazarus it was that made the crowd believe i | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The crowd was disturbed by the crowd. But why grudge that blind crowd, that the world should go after Him, by Whom the world was made? | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The world means here the crowd. This seems to be the speech of that part who were sound in their faith, but dared not profess it. They try to deter the rest by exposing the insuperable difficulties  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if they said, The more you attack Him, the more will His power and reputation increase. What use then of these attempts? | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The temple at Jerusalem was so famous, that on the feast days, not only the people near, but many Gentiles from distant countries came to worship in it; as that eunuch of Candace, Queen of the Ethio | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The time being now near, when they would be made proselytes. They hear Christ talked of, and wish to see Him: The same came therefore to Philip, which was of Bethsaida of Galilee, and desired: him,  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo! the Jews wish to kill Him, the Gentiles to see Him. But they also were of the Jews who cried, Blessed is He that comes in the name of the Lord. So behold them of the circumcision, and them of th | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As being the elder disciple. He had heard our Savior say, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and therefore he communicates with his fellow-disciple, and the refer the matter to their Lord: And aga | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Listen we to the voice of the corner stone: And Jesus answered them, saying, The hour is come, that the Son of man should be glorified. Did He think Himself glorified, because the Gentiles wished to | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , He Himself, of the seed of the Patriarchs, was sown in the field of this world, that by dying, He might rise again with increase. He died alone; He rose again with many. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He illustrates His discourse by an example from nature. A grain of corn produces fruit, after it has cried. How much more then must the Son of God? The Gentiles were to be called after the Jews had  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This may be understood in two ways: 1. If you love it, lose it: if you would preserve your life in Christ, fear not death for Christ. 2. Do not love your life here, lest you lose it hereafter. The l | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He loves his life in this world, who indulges its inordinate desires; he hates it, who resists them. It is not, who cloth not yield to, but, who hates. For as we cannot bear to hear the voice or see | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It were harsh to say that a man should hate his soul; so He adds, in this world: i.e. for a particular time, not forever. And we shall gain in the end by so doing: shall keep it to life eternal. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But think not for an instant, that by hating your soul, is meant that you may kill yourself. For wicked and perverse men have sometimes so mistaken it, and have burnt and strangled themselves, throw | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This present life is sweet to them who are given up to it. But he who looks heavenwards, and sees what good things are there, soon despises this life. When the better life appears, the worse is desp | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what is it to serve Christ? The very words explain. They serve Christ who seek not their own things, but the things of Jesus Christ, i.e. who follow Him, walk in His, not their own v ways, do al | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:26 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then death will be followed by resurrection. Where I am, He says; for Christ was in heaven before His resurrection. Thither let us ascend in heart and in mind. If any man serve Me, him will My Fa | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, My Father will honor him, not, I will honor him; because they had not yet proper notions of His nature, and thought Him inferior to the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To our Lord’s exhortation to His disciples to endurance, they might have replied that it was easy for Him, Who was out of the reach of human pain, to talk philosophically about death, and to recomme | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I hear Him say, He that hates his life in this world, shall keep it to life eternal; and I am ravished, I despise the world; the whole of this life, however long, is but a vapor in My sight; all tem | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As He draws near to the Cross, His human nature appears, a nature that did not wish to die, but cleaved to this present life. He shows that He is not quite without human feelings. For the desire of  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lastly, let the man who would follow Him, hear at what hour he should follow. A fearful hour has perhaps come: a choice is offered, either to do wrong, or suffer: the weak soul is troubled. Hear our | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. What but something to confirm My followers? Father, save Me from this hour. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He teaches you Whom you should call on, whose will prefer to your own. Let Him not seem to fall from His greatness, because He wishes you to rise from your meanness. He took upon Him man’s infirmity | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, I cannot say why I should ask to be saved from it; For this cause came I to this hour. However you may be troubled and dejected at the thought of dying, do not run away from death. I  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When God speaks audibly, as He does here, but no visible appearance is seen, He speaks through the medium of a rational creature: i.e. by the voice of an Angel. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I have glorified it, i.e. before I made the world; and will glorify it again, i.e. when you shall rise from the dead. Or, I have gloried it, when you were born of a Virgin, did work miracles, was ma | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The voice though loud and distinct, soon passed off from their gross, carnal, and sluggish minds; only the sound remaining. Others perceived an articulate voice, but did not catch what it said: Othe | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. It did not come to tell Him what He knew already, but them what they ought to know. And as that voice did not come for His sake, but for theirs, so His soul was not troubled for His sake, but f | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The voice of the Father proved what they were so fond of denying, that He was from God. For He must be from God, if He was glorified by God. It was not that He needed encouragement of such a voice H | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The judgment at the end of the world will be of eternal rewards and punishments. But there is another judgment, not of condemnation, but of selection, which is the one meant here; the selection of H | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What kind of judgment it is by which the devil is cast out, I will explain by an example. A man demands payment from his debtors, beats them, and sends them to prison. He treats with the same insole | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is this all that He draws, but that from which the devil is cast out? He does not say, All men, but, All things; for all men have not faith. He does not mean then all mankind, but the whole of  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why then did He say above, that the Father drew men? Because the Father draws, by the Son who draws. I shall draw, He says, as if men were in the grasp of some tyrant, from which they could not extr | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If I be lifted up from the earth, He says, i.e. when I shall be lifted up. He does not doubt that the work will be accomplished which He came to do. By His being lifted up, He means His passion on t | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews when they understood that our Lord spoke of His own death, asked how that could be: The people answered Him, We have heard out of the law that Christ abides for ever: and how say you, The S | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Hence we see, that they understood many of the things that He spoke in parables. As He had talked about death a little time before, they saw now what was meant by His being lifted up. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Or they interpreted the word by their own intended act. It was not wisdom imparted, but conscience disturbed, which disclosed its meaning to them. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And see how maliciously they put the question. They do not say, We have heard out of the law, that Christ cloth not suffer; for in many places of Scripture His passion and resurrection are spoken of | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yet a little while is the light with you. Hence it is that you understand that Christ abides forever. Wherefore walk while you have the light, approach, understand the whole, that Christ will both d | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not mean only the time before His crucifixion, but the whole of their lives. For many believed on Him after His crucifixion. Lest darkness come upon you. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. if you so believe in the eternity of Christ, as to deny His humiliation and death. For he that walks in darkness, knows not where he goes. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What things do the Jews now, and know not what they do; thinking, like men in the dark, that they are going the right road, while they are taking directly the wrong one. Wherefore He adds, While you | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. While you have any truth, believe in the truth, that you may be born again of the truth: That you may be the children of the light. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. My children. In the beginning of the Gospel it is said, Born of God, i.e. of the Father. But here He Himself is the Begetter. The same act is the act both of Father and Son. These things spoke  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:36 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not from those which began to believe in and love Him, but from those who saw and envied Him. When He hid Himself, He consulted our weakness, He did not derogate from His own power. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did He hide Himself, when they neither took up stones to cast at Him, nor blasphemed? Because He saw into their hearts, and knew the fury they were in; and therefore did not wait till they b | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And thus the Evangelist tacitly explains it, when he adds, But though He had done so many miracles before them, yet they believed not on Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He means the miracles related above. It was no small wickedness to disbelieve against such miracles as those. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why then did Christ come? Did He not know that they would not believe in Him? Yes: the Prophets had prohibited this very unbelief, and He came that it might be made manifest, to their confusion  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Who, i.e. So very few believed. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is evident here that the arm of the Lord is the Son of God Himself. Not that the Father has a human fleshly form; He is called the arm of the Lord, because all things v ere made by Him. If a man  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the saying of Esaias might be fulfilled: that here is expressive not of the cause, but of the event. They did not disbelieve because Esaias said they would; but because they would disbelieve, E | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what follows involved a deeper question: Therefore they could not believe, because that Esaias said again, He has blinded their eyes, and hardened their hearts, that they should not see with the | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is a common form of speech among ourselves. I cannot love such a man, meaning by this necessity only a vehement will. The Evangelist says could not, to show that it was impossible that the Prop | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But the Prophet, you say, mentions another cause, not their will; viz. that God had blinded their eyes, and hardened their heart. But I answer, that they well deserved this. For God hardens and blin | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For He does not leave us, except we wish Him, as He said in Hosea, Seeing you have forgotten the law of your God, I will also forget your children. Whereby it is plain that we begin to forsake first | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): And be converted, and I should heal them. Is not to be understood here, from the beginning of the sentence - that they should not see with their eyes, nor understand with their hearts, nor be converte | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His glory means the vision of Him sitting on His lofty throne: I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne. Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:43 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nevertheless, among the chief rulers also many believed on Him; but because of the Pharisees they did not confess Him, lest they should be put out of the synagogue. For they loved the praise of men  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As their faith grew, their love of human praise grew still more, and outstripped it. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because the love of human praise prevented the chief rulers from believing, Jesus cried and said, He that believes in Me, believes not on Me, but on Him that sent Me: as if to say, Why are you afrai | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He signifies to them that He is more than He appears to be, (for to men He appeared but a man; His Godhead was hid.) Such as the Father is, such am I in nature and in dignity; He that believes in Me | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that believes on Me, believes not in Me, but on Him that sent Me: as if He said, He that takes water from a stream, takes the water not of the stream, but of the fountain. Then to show that it is | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whereby it is evident, that He found all in darkness. In which darkness if they wish not to remain, they must believe in the light which is come into the world. He says in one place to His disciples | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And to show that He does not let His despisers go unpunished, from want of power, He adds, And if any man hear My words and believe not, I judge him not. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): i.e. I judge him not now. He does not say, I judge him not at I the last day, for that would be contrary to the sentence above, The Father has committed all judgment to the Son. And the reason follows | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But that this might not serve to encourage sloth, He warns men of a terrible judgment coming; He that rejects Me, and hears not My words, has one that judges him. | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mean time they waited to know who this one was; so He proceeds: The word that I have spoken, the same shall judge him at the last day. He makes it sufficiently clear that he Himself will judge at th | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I judge him not; the word that I have spoken shall judge him: for I have not spoken of Myself. The word which the Son speaks judges, because the Son did not speak of Himself: for I have not spoken o | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I ask then how we shall understand this, I will not judge, but the word which I have spoken will judge? Yet He Himself is the Word of the Father which speaks. Is it thus? I will not judge by My huma | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, I judge him not, i.e. I am not the cause of his destruction, but he is himself, by despising my words. The words that I have just said, shall be his accusers, and deprive him of all excuse; the  | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the Father gave the Son a commandment, He did not give Him what He had not: for in the Wisdom of the Father, i.e. in the Word, are all the commandments of the Father. The commandment is said to | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Since the Son is the Word of the Father, and reveals completely what is in the mind of the Father, He says He receives a commandment what He should say, and what He should speak: just as our word, i | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 12:50 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If life everlasting is the Son Himself, and the commandment is life everlasting, what is this but saying, I am the commandment of the Father? And in the same way in the following; Whatsoever I speak | src: CAJohn.htm#12
John 13:5 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord being about to depart out of this life, shows His great care for His disciples: Now before the feast of the Passover, when Jesus knew that His hour was come that He should depart out of thi | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews had many feasts, but the principal one was the passover; and therefore it is particularly said, Before the feast of the passover. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pascha is not a Greek word, as some think, but Hebrew: though there is remarkable agreement of the two languages in it. The Greek word to suffer being pascha has been thought to mean passion, as bei | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not know then for the first time: He had known long before. By His departure He means His death, Being so near leaving His disciples, He shows the more love for them: Having loved His own whi | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He loved them to the end, i.e. that they themselves too might pass out of this world, by love, to Him their head. For what is to the end, but to Christ? For Christ is the end of the law for righteou | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist inserts this as if in astonishment: our Lord being about to wash the feet of the very person who had resolved to betray Him. It shows the great wickedness too of the traitor, that eve | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist being about to relate so great an instance of our Lord’s humility, reminds us first of His lofty nature: knowing that the Father had given all things into His hand, not excepting the  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He knew that He had even His persecutors in His hand that He might convert them from malice to love of Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): The Father has given all things into His hands; i.e. into His power; for His hands hold all things; or to Him, for His work; My Father works hitherto, and I work (John 5:17). | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Had given all things into His hand. What is given Him is the salvation of the believers. Think not of this giving up in a human way. It signifies His honor for, and agreement with, the Father. For a | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Knowing too, that He was come from God, and went to God; not that He left God when He came, or will leave us when He returns. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father having given up all things into His hands, i.e. having given up to Him the salvation of the faithful, He deemed it right to show them all things that pertained to their salvation; and gav | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Since the Father had given all things into His hands, He washed not His disciples’ hands indeed, but their feet; and since He knew that He came from God, and went to God, He performed the work not o | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was a thing worthy of Him, Who came from God, and went to God, to trample upon all pride; He rises from supper, and laid aside His garment, and took a towel, and, girded Himself.; After that He p | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:5 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, dinner is the first meal, taken early in the spiritual day, and adapted to those who have just entered upon this day. Supper is the last meal, and is set before those who are farther adv | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He laid aside His garments, when, being in the form of God, He emptied Himself; He girded Himself with a towel, took upon Him the form of a servant; He poured water into a basin, out of which He was | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As a physician, who has many sick under his care, begins with those who want his attention most, so Christ, in washing His disciples’ feet, begins with the most unclean, and so comes at last to Pete | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What is the meaning of you and my feet? It is better to think than speak of this; lest one should fail in explaining adequately what might have been rightly conceived. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Though Peter was the first of the Apostles, yet it is possible that the traitor petulantly placed himself above him; and that this may be the reason why our Lord first began to wash, and then comes  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is plain that our Lord did not wash Peter first, but none other of the disciples would have attempted to be washed before him. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Some one will ask why none of them prevented Him, except Peter, this being a sign not of want of love, but of reverence. The reason seems to be, that He washed the traitor first, and came next to Pete | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: All the rest put out their feet, certain that so great a one would not want to wash them without reason: but Peter, looking only to the thing itself, and seeing nothing beyond it, refused o | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: We must not suppose that Peter was afraid and refused, when the others had willingly and gladly submitted to the washing. Our Lord did not go through the others first, and to the first of t | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. How useful a lesson of humility it teaches you, and how, directly this virtue leads to God. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or our Lord insinuates that this is a mystery. By washing and wiping, He made beautiful the feet of those who were to preach glad tidings (Isaiah 52:7), and to walk on that way of which He tells the | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not refuse, because our Lord’s act was above his understanding, but he could not bear to see Him bending at his feet: Peter says to Him, you shall not wash my feet; i.e. I will never suffer i | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is an instance, that a man may say a thing with a good intention, and yet ignorantly to His hurt. Peter, ignorant of our Lord’s deep meaning, at first, as if in doubt, says mildly, Lord, do you | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If I wash you not, He says, though it was only his feet that He was going to wash, just as we say, you tread on me; though it is only our foot that is trodden on. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Let those who refuse to allegorize these and like passages, say how it is probable that he who out of reverence for Jesus said, you shall never wash my feet, would have had no part with the Son of G | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say on what account He performs this act of washing, but only threatens him. For Peter was not persuaded by the first answer: you shall know hereafter he did not say, Teach me then that  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This saying we may use against those who make hasty and indiscreet resolutions. By strewing them, that if they adhere to these, they will have no part with Jesus, we disengage them from such resolve | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But he, agitated by fear and love, dreaded more the being denied Christ, than the seeing Him at His feet: Simon Peter said to Him, Lord, not my feet only, but also my hands and my head. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus was unwilling to wash hands, and despised what was said of Him in this respect: Your disciples wash not their hands when they eat bread (Matt 15:2). And He did not wish the head to be submerge | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Clean all except the feet. The whole of a man is washed in baptism, not excepting his feet; but living in the world afterwards, we tread upon the earth. Those human affections then, without which we | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was impossible that the lowest parts and extremities of a soul should escape defilement, even in one perfect as far as man can be; and many, even after baptism, are covered up to their head with  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . From what is here said, we understand that Peter was already baptized, indeed that He baptized by His disciples, shows that His disciples must have been baptized, either with John’s baptism, or, whi | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And you are clean, but not all: what this means the Evangelist immediately! explains: For He knew who should betray Him; therefore said He, you are not all clean. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . you are clean, refers to the eleven; but not all, to Judas. He was unclean, first, because he cared not for the poor, but was a thief; secondly, because the devil had put it into his heart to betray | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, the disciples when washed had only to have their feet washed; because while man lives in this world, he contracts himself with earth, by means of his human affections, which are as it were his f | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: When He calls them clean, you must not suppose that they were delivered from sin before the victim was offered. He means cleanness in respect of knowledge; for they were now delivered from  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, mindful of His promise to Peter that he should know the meaning of His act, you shall know here after, now begins to teach him: So after He had washed their feet, and had taken His garment | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Know you, is either interrogative, to show the greatness of the act, or imperative, to rouse their minds. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, when at our redemption we were changed by the shedding of His blood, He took again His garments, rising from the grave the third day, and clothed in the same body now immortal, ascended  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He speaks now not to Peter alone, but to all: you call Me Master and Lord. He accepts their judgment; and to prevent the words being set down merely to favor on their parts, adds, And you say well,  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is enjoined in the Proverbs, Let another man praise you, and not your own mouth. For it is dangerous for one to praise himself, who has to beware of pride. But He who is above all things, howsoev | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They do not say well, Lord, to whom it shall be said, Depart from Me, you that work iniquity. But; the Apostles say well, Master and Lord, for wickedness had not dominion over them, but the Word of  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He shows us the greater, that we may do the less. For He was the Lord, but we, if we do it, do it to our fellow-servants: For I have given you an example, that you should do as I have done to you. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord first did a thing, then taught it: as it is said, Jesus began both to do and to teach (Acts 1:1). | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is, blessed Peter, what you were ignorant of; this you were told that you should know afterwards. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it is not necessary for one who wishes to do all the commandments of Jesus, literally to perform the act of washing feet. This is merely a matter of custom; and the custom is now generally dropp | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This act is done literally by many, when they receive one another in hospitality. For it is unquestionably better that it should be done with the hands, and that the Christian disdain not to do what | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: This spiritual washing of the feet is done primarily by Jesus Himself, secondarily by His disciples, in that He said to them, you ought to wash one another’s feet. Jesus washed the feet of  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): He continues to urge them to wash one another’s feet; Verily, verily, I Say to you, The servant is not greater than his lord, neither He that is sent greater than He that sent Him; as if to say, If I  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This was a necessary admonition to the Apostles, some of whom were about to rise higher, others to lower degrees of eminence. That none might exult over another, He changes the hearts of all. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To know what is good, and not to do it, tends not to happiness, but to condemnation; as James said, To him that knows to do good, and does it not, to him it is sin (James 4:17). Wherefore He adds, I | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For all know, but all do not do. He then rebukes the traitor, not openly, but covertly: I speak not of you all. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, There is one among you who will not be blessed, nor does these things. I know whom I have chosen. Whom, but those who shall be happy by doing His commandments? Judas therefore was not  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: I speak not of you all, does not refer to, Happy are you if you do them. For of Judas, or any other person, it may be said, Happy is he if he do them. The words refer to the sentence above, | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then, that He might not sadden them all, He c adds, But that the Scripture must be fulfilled, He that eats bread with Me, has lifted up his heel against Me: strewing that He knew who the traitor was | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Shall lift up his heel against Me, i.e. shall tread upon Me. The traitor Judas is meant. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that eats bread with Me; i.e. who was fed by Me, who partook of My table. So that if injured ever by our servants or inferiors, we need not be offended. Judas had received infinite benefits, and  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They then who were chosen ate the Lord; he ate the bread of the Lord, to injure the Lord; they ate life, he damnation; for he that eats unworthily, eats damnation to himself (1 Cor 11:27). Now I tel | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That you may believe, is not said, as if the Apostles did not believe already, but is equivalent to saying, Do as you believe, and persevere in your belief, seeking for no occasion of falling away.  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As the disciples were about to go forth and to suffer many things, He consoles them by promising His own assistance and that of others; His own, when He says, Happy are you if you do them; that of o | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:20 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For he that receives him whom Jesus sends, receives Jesus who is represented by him; and he that receives Jesus, receives the Father. Therefore he that receives whom Jesus sends, receives the Father | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Arians, when they hear this passage, appeal immediately to the gradations in their system, that as far as the Apostle is from the Lord, so far is the Son from the Father. But our Lord has left u | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord after His twofold promise of assistance to the Apostles in their future labors, remembers that the traitor is cut off from both, and is troubled at the thought: When Jesus had thus said, He | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This did not come into His mind then for the first time; but He was now about to make the traitor known, and single him out from the rest, and therefore was troubled in spirit. The traitor too was n | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . His being troubled in spirit, was the human part, suffering under the excess of the spiritual. For if every Saint lives, acts, and suffers in the spirit, how much more is this true of Jesus, the Rew | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Away then with the reasonings of the Stoics, who deny that perturbation of mind can come upon a wise man; who, as they take vanity for truth, so make their healthy state of mind insensibility. It is | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As He did not mention Him by name, all began to fear: Then the disciples looked one on another, doubting of whom He spoke; not conscious of any evil in themselves, and entrusting to Christ’s words,  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They had a devoted love for their Master but yet so that human weakness made them doubt of one another. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They remembered too, that, as men, before they were matured, their minds were liable to change, so as to form wishes the very opposite to what they might have had before. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While all were trembling, and not excepting even Peter, their head, John, as the beloved disciple, lay upon Jesus’ breast. He then lying on Jesus’ breast said to Him, Lord, who is it? | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is John, whose Gospel this is, as he afterwards declares. It is the custom of the sacred writers, when they come to any thing relating to themselves, to speak of themselves, as if they were spe | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If you want to know the cause of this familiarity, it is love: Whom Jesus loved. Others were loved, but he was loved more than any. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I think this has a peculiar meaning, viz. that John was admitted to a knowledge of the more secret mysteries of the Word. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whom Jesus loved. This John says to show his own innocence, and also why it was that Peter beckoned to him, inasmuch as he was not Peter’s superior: Simon Peter therefore beckoned to him, that he sh | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe too his mode of speaking, which was not by word, but by beckoning; Beckoned and spoke, i.e. spoke by beckoning. If even thoughts speak, as when it is said, They spoke among themselves, much  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, at first he beckoned, and then not content with beckoning, spoke: Who is it of whom he speaks? He then lying on Jesus’ breast, said to Him, Lord, who is it? | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . On Jesus’ breast; the same as in Jesus’ bosom. Or, he lay first in Jesus’ bosom, and then ascended higher, and lay upon His breast; as if, had he remained lying in His bosom, and not ascended to lie | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That he lay in the bosom, and upon the breast, was not only an evidence of present love, but also a sign of the future, viz. of those new and mysterious doctrines which be was afterwards commissione | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For by bosom what else is signified but secret? Here is the hollow of the breast, the secret’ chamber of wisdom. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But not even then did our Lord expose the traitor by name; Jesus answered, He it is, to whom I shall give a sop when I have dipped it. Such a mode of declaring him, should itself have turned him fro | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not as some careless readers think, that then Judas received singly Christ’s body. For our Lord had already distributed the sacraments of His body and blood to all of them, while Judas was as there, | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, that at first Satan did not enter into Judas, but only put it into his heart to betray his Master. But after the bread, he entered into him. Wherefore let us beware, that Satan thrust not a | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So long as he was one of the twelve, the devil did not dare to force an entrance into him; but when he was pointed out, and expelled, then he easily leaped into him. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or entered into him, that he might have more full possession of him: for he was in him, when he agreed with the Jews to betray, our Lord for a sum of money, according to Luke: Then entered Satan int | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was proper that by the ceremony of the bread, that good should be taken from him, which he thought he had: whereof being deprived, he was laid open to admit Satan’s entrance. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But some will say, was his being given up to the devil the effect of his receiving the sop from Christ? To whom we answer, that they may learn here the danger of receiving amiss what is in itself go | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This may have been said either to Judas, or to Satan, either to provoke the enemy to the combat, or the traitor to do his part in bringing on that dispensation, which was to save the world; which He | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not however enjoin the act, but foretold it, not from g desire for the destruction of the perfidious, but to hasten on the salvation of the faithful. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That which you do, do quickly, is not a command, or a recommendation, but a reproof, meant to show too that He was not going to offer any hindrance to His betrayal. Now no man at the table knew for  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord then had bags, in which; He kept the oblations of the faithful, to supply the wants of His own followers, or the poor. Here is the first institution of ecclesiastical property. Our Lord sho | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . None of the disciples contributed this money, but it is hinted that it was certain women, who, it is said, ministered to Him of their means. But how was it that He Who forbade scrip, and staff, and  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord then said to Judas, That which you do, do quickly, and the traitor this once obeyed his Master. For having received the sop, he started immediately on his work: He then having received the  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It follows: And it was night, to show the impetuosity of Judas, in persisting in spite of the unseasonableness of the hour. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:30 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The time of night corresponded with the night which overspread the soul of Judas. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:32 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By the time of the day is signified the end of the action. Judas went out in the night to accomplish his perfidy, for which he was never to be pardoned. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:32 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After the glory of His miracles, and His transfiguration, the next glorifying of the Son of man began, when Judas went out with Satan, who had entered into him; Therefore when he was gone out, Jesus | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. by Himself, not by any other. And shall straightway glorify Him, i.e. not at any distant time, but immediately, while He is yet on the very cross shall His glory appear. For the sun was darkene | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The unclean went out: the clean remained with their cleanser. Thus will it be when the tares are separated from the wheat; The righteous shall shine forth as the sale in the kingdom of thei | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:32 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That God is glorified in Him, refers to the glory of the body, which glory is the glory of God, in that the body borrows its glory from its association with the Divine nature because God is glorifie | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The word glory is here used in a different sense from that which some Pagans attach to it, who defined glory to be the collected praises of the many. It is evident that glory in such a sens | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After He had said, And shall straightway glorify Him, that they might not think that God was going to glorify Him in such a way, as that He would no longer have any converse with them on earth, He s | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Little children, He says; for their souls were yet in infancy. But these little children, after His death, were made brethren; as before they were little children, they were servants. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may be understood too thus: I am as yet in this frail flesh, even as you are, until I die and rise again. He was with them after His resurrection, by bodily presence, not by participation of huma | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But may there not be a deeper meaning in the words, yet a little while &c. After a little while He was not with them. In what sense not with them? Not because He was not with them according to the f | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He means that they were not yet fit to follow Him to death for righteousness’ sake. For how could they, when they were not ripe for martyrdom? Or how could they follow our Lord to immortality, th | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, I say it to you, but with the addition of now, The Jews, who He foresaw would die in their sins, would never be able to follow Him; but the disciples were unable only for a little tim | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And therefore He said, little children; for He did not mean to speak to them, as He had to the Jews. you cannot follow Me now, He says, in order to rouse the love of His disciples. For the departure | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And now He teaches them how to fit themselves to follow. Him: A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. But does not the old law say, you shall love your neighbor as yourself (Lev  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, as I have loved you: for My love has not been the payment of something owing to you, but had its beginning on My side. And you ought in like manner to do one another good, though you may not owe | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But; do not think that that greater commandment, viz. that we: should love the Lord our God, is passed by. For, if we understand the two precepts aright, each is implied in the other. He who loves G | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:35 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Passing over the miracles, which they were to perform, He makes love: the distinguishing mark of His followers; By this stall all men know that you are My disciples’ if you have love one to another. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Other gifts are shared with you by those who are not mine; birth, life, sense, reason, and such good things as belong alike to man and brutes; nay, and tongues, sacraments, prophecy,  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Great is love, and stronger than fire; nothing can stop its course. Peter the most ardent of all, as soon as he hears our Lord say, Where I go you cannot follow Me now, asks, Lord, where do you go? | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciple asks this, as if he were ready to follow. But our Lord saw his heart; Jesus answered him, Where I go, you cannot follow Me now; He checks his forwardness, but does not destroy his hope; | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peter, on receiving this answer, does not check his desire, but hastily conceives favorable hopes from it, and having got rid of the fear of betraying our Lord, feels secure, and becomes himself the | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which sentence may be read in two ways: either as affirming, you shall lay down your life for My sake, but now through fear of the death of the body, you shall incur spiritual death: or as mocking;  | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Will you do that for Me, which I have not done yet for you? Can you go before, who can not come after? Why presume you so? Hear what you are: Verily, verily, I say to you, The cock shall not crow, t | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nevertheless, should any one fall, let the example of Peter save him from despair, and teach him that he can without delay obtain pardon from God. | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is manifest that our Lord permitted Peter’s fall. He might have recalled him to begin with, but as he persisted in his vehemence, though He did not drive him to a denial, He let him go without as | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That took place in the soul of Peter, which he offered in the body; though differently from what he meant. For before the death and resurrection of our Lord, he both died by his denial, and lived ag | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 13:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This speech, The cock shall not crow, occurs in all the Evangelists, but not at the same time in all. Matthew and Mark: introduce it after they have left the house, in which they were eating; Luke a | src: CAJohn.htm#13
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord consoles His disciples, who, as men, would be naturally alarmed and troubled at the idea of His death, by assuring them of His divinity: Let not your heart be troubled: you believe in God,  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Faith, too, in Me, and in the Father that begat Me, is more powerful than anything that shall come upon you; and will prevail in spite of all difficulties. He shows His divinity at the same time by  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And as the disciples were afraid for themselves, when Peter, the boldest and most zealous of them, had been told, The cock shall not crow, till you have denied Me thrice, He adds, In My Father’s hou | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The many mansions agree w with the one penny, because, though one may rejoice more than another, yet all rejoice with one and the same joy, arising from the vision of their Maker. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And thus God will be all in all; that is, since God is love, love will bring it to pass, that what each has, will be common to all. That which one loves in another is one’s own, though one have it n | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Nor is there any sense of deficiency in consequence of such inequality; for each will feel as much as suffices for himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But they are rejected by the Christians, who infer from there being many mansions that there is a place outside the kingdom of heaven, where innocent souls, that have departed this life without bapt | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Our Lord having said above to Peter, Where I go, you can not follow Me now, but you shall follow Me afterwards, that they might not think that this promise was made to Peter only, He says,  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He means evidently that there are already many mansions, and that there is no need of His preparing one. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, you can not follow Me now, that they might not think that they were cut off for ever, He adds: And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again area receive you to Myself, tha | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And if not, I would have told you: I go to prepare, &c. As if He said; Either way you should not be troubled, whether places are prepared for you, or not. For, if they are not prepared, I will very  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why does He go and prepare a place, if there are many mansions already? Because these are not as yet so prepared as they will be. The same mansions that He has prepared by predestination, He pre | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:4 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says then, I go, by the absence of the flesh, I shall come again, by the presence of the Godhead; or, I shall come again to judge the quick and dead. And as He knew that they would ask where He w | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He show them that He is aware of their curiosity to know His meaning, and thus excites them to pelt questions to Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the Jews, who wished to be separated from Christ, asked where He was going, much more would the disciples, who wished never to be separated from Him, be anxious to know it. So with much love, and | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord had said that they knew both, Thomas says that they knew neither. Our Lord cannot lie; they knew not that they did know. Our Lord proves that they did: Jesus said to Him, I am the way, the  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As it He said, I am the way, whereby you would go; I am the truth, whereto you would go; I am the life, in which you would abide. The truth and the life every one understands; but not everyone has f | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For He who is the way does not lead us into devious courses out of the way; nor does He who is the truth deceive us by falsehoods; not does He who is the life leave us in the darkness of death. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When you art engaged in the practical, He is made your way; when in the contemplative, He is made your truth. And to the active and the contemplative is joined life: for we should both act and conte | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They knew then the way, because they knew He was the way. But what need to add, the truth, and the life? Because they were yet to be told whither He went. He went to the truth; He went to the life.  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For if, He says, you have Me for your guide to the Father, you shall certainly come to Him. Nor call you come by any other way. Whereas He had said above, No man can come to Me, except the Father dr | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:7 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How can our Lord say, If you had known Me, you should have known My Father also; when He has just said, Where I go you know, and the way you know? We must suppose that some of them knew, and others  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: When it is said that the Son is the way to the Father, is it meant that He is so by His teaching, or by His nature? We shall be able to see from what follows: If you had known Me, you shoul | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A declaration so new startled Philip. Our Lord is seen to be man. He confesses Himself to be the Son of God, declares that, if He were known, the Father would be known, that, if He is seen, the Fath | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For to that joy of beholding His face, nothing can be added. Philip understood this, and said, Lord, show us the Father, and it suffices us. But he did not yet understand that he could in the same w | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how is this, when our Lord said that they knew where He was going, and the way, because they knew Him? The question is easily settled by supposing that some of them knew, and others not; among t | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He reproves the ignorance of Philip in this respect. For whereas his actions had been strictly divine, such as walking on the water, commanding the winds, remitting sins, raising the dead, He compla | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When two persons are very like each, we say, If you have seen the one, you have seen. n the other. So here, He that has seen Me, has seen the Father; not that He is troth the Father, and the Son, bu | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not mean the sight of the bodily eye: for His fleshly part, born of the Virgin, does not avail towards contemplating the form and image of God in Him; but the Son of God being known with the | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But is he to be reproved, who, when he has seen the likeness, wishes to see the man of whom he is the likeness? No, our Lord rebuked the question, only with reference to the mind of the asker. Phili | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For what excuse was there for ignorance of the Father, or what necessity to show Him, when the Father was seen in the Son by His essential nature, while by the identity of unity, the Begotten and th | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He wished him to live by faith, before he had sight, and therefore says, Believe you not? Spiritual vision is the reward of faith, vouchsafed to minds purified by faith. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father, not by a conjunction of two harmonizing essences, nor by a nature grafted into a more capacious substance as in material bodies, in which it  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The unchangeable God follows, so to speak, His own nature, by begetting unchangeable God. Nor does the perfect birth of unchangeable God from unchangeable God forsake His own nature. We understand t | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Philip, because [he thought] he had seen c the Son with his bodily eye, wished to see the Father in the same way; perhaps too remembering what the Prophet said, I saw the Lord (Isaiah 6:1), | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then addresses all of them, not Philip only: The word that I speak to you, I speak not of Myself. What is, I speak not of Myself, I but, I that speak am not of Myself? He attributes what He does  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein He neither desires Himself to be the Son, nor hides the existence of His Father’s power in Him. In that He speaks, it is Himself that speaks in His own person; in that He speaks not of Himse | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mark the abundant proof of the unity of substance. For He continues; But the Father that dwells in Me, He does the works. As if He said, My Father and I act together, not differently from each other | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For he that edifies his neighbor by speaking, does a good work. These two sentences are brought against us by different sects of heretics; the Arians saying that the Son is unequal to the Father, be | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the Father dwells in the Son, show that He is not single, or solitary; that the Father works by the Son, shows that He is not different or alien. As He is not solitary who does not speak from H | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Philip alone was reproved before. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if this does not suffice to show my consubstantiality, at least learn it from My works: Or else believe Me for the very works’ sake. You have seen My miracles, and all the proper signs of My div | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Believe then for My works’ sake, that I am in the Father, and the Father in Me; for, were we separated, we could not be working together. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, Believe for the works’ sake, our Lord goes on to declare that He can do much greater than these, and what is more wonderful, give others the power of working them. Verily, verily, I say | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what are these greater works? Is it that the shadow of the Apostles, as they passed by, healed the sick; It is indeed a greater thing that a shadow should heal, than that the border of a garment | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. I shall not perish, but shall remain in My proper dignity, in heaven. Or He means: It is your part henceforth to work miracles, since I am going. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And that no one might attribute the merit to himself, He shows, that even those greater works were His own doing: And whatsoever you shall ask in My name, that will I do. Before it was, He shall do, | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In My name, He says. Thus the Apostles, In the name of Jesus of Nazareth, arise and walk., All the miracles that they did, He did: the hand of the Lord was with them. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is an explanation of the doctrine of miracles. It is by prayer, and invocation of His name, that a man is able to work miracles. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Whatsoever you shall ask. Then why do we often see believers asking, and not receiving? Perhaps it is that they ask amiss. When a man would make a bad use of what he asks for, God in His mercy does  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For when the great power of c the Son is manifested, He that begot Him is glorified. He introduces this last, to confirm the truth of what He has said. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe the order in which the glorifying of the Father comes. In the name of Jesus miracles were done, by which men were made to believe the Apostles’ preaching. This brought them to the knowledge  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having said, Whatsoever you shall ask in My name, that I will do; that they might not think simply asking would be enough, He adds, If you love Me, keep My commandments. And then I will do  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein He shows too that He Himself is the Comforter. Paraclete means advocate, and is applied to Christ: We have an Advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous (1 Jn 2:1). | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Paraclete, i.e. Comforter. They had then one Comforter, who comforted and elevated them by the sweetness of His miracles, and His preaching. DIDYMUS. But the Holy Ghost was another Comforter: differ | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, I will ask the Father, to make them believe Him: which they could not have done, had He simply said, I will send. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Yet to show that His works are inseparable from His Father’s, He says below, When I shall go, I will send Him to you. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what had He more than the Apostles, if He could only ask the Father to give others the Spirit? The Apostles did this often even without praying. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I will ask - He says, as being the inferior in respect of His humanity - My Father, with Whom I am equal and consubstantial in respect of My Divine nature. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That He may abide with you for ever. The Spirit does not depart even at death. He intimates too that the Holy Ghost will not suffer death, or go away, as He has done. But that the mention of the Com | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is the Holy Ghost in the Trinity, Whom the Catholic faith professes to be consubstantial and coeternal with the Father and the Son. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Spirit of truth He calls Him, because He unfolds the figures of the Old Testament. The world are the wicked, seeing is certain knowledge; sight being the most certain of the senses. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Note too, that when He calls the Holy Spirit the Spirit of truth, He shows that the Holy Spirit is His Spirit: then when He says He is given by the Father, He declares Him to be the Spirit of the Fa | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Holy Spirit kindles in every one, in whom He dwells, the desire of things invisible. And since worldly minds love only things visible, this world receives Him not, because it rises not to the lo | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus the world, i.e. the lovers of the world, cannot, He says, receive the Holy Spirit: that is to say, unrighteousness cannot be righteous. The world, i.e. the lovers of the world, cannot receive H | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, He will not dwell with you as I have done, but will dwell in your souls. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To be in a place is prior to dwelling. Be in you, is the explanation of dwell with you: i.e. shows that the latter means not that He is seen, but that He is known. He must be in us, that the knowled | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if the Holy Spirit abides in the disciples, how is it a special mark of the Mediator that He abides in Him. We shall better understand if we distinguish between the different gifts of the Spirit | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This speech levels at a stroke, as it were, the opposite heresies. The word another, shows the distinct personality of the Spirit: the word Paraclete, His consubstantiality. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Comforter, the title of the Holy Spirit, the third Person in the Trinity, the Apostle applies to God: God that comforts those that are cast down, comforted us. The Holy Spirit therefore Who comforts | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But when the love of God is shed abroad in our hearts by the Holy Ghost which is given unto us (Rom 5:5), how shall we love and keep the commandments of Christ, so as to receive the Spirit, when we  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When He had cleansed His disciples by the sacrifice of His passion, and their sins were remitted, and they were sent forth to dangers and trials, it was necessary that they should receive the Holy S | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That no one might think, because our Lord was about to give the Holy Spirit, that He would therefore not be present Himself in Him, He adds, I will not leave you comfortless. The Greek word signifie | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At the first He said, Where I go you shall come; but as this was a long time off, He promises them the Spirit in the interval. And as they knew not what that was, He promises them that they most des | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the world saw Him then with the carnal eye, manifest in the flesh, though it did not see the Word hidden under the flesh. But after the resurrection He was unwilling to show even His flesh, exce | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Though I shall die, I shall rise again. And you shall live also, i.e. when you see Me risen again, you will rejoice, and be as dead men brought to life again. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To me however he seems to refer not only to the present life, but to the future; as if He said, The death of the cross shall not separate you from Me for ever, but only hide Me from you for a moment | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why does He speak of life as present to Him, future to them? Because His resurrection preceded, theirs was to follow. His resurrection was about so soon to take place, that He speaks of it as pr | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, in that day, on which I shall rise again, you shall know. For His resurrection it was that established their faith. Then the powerful teaching of the Holy Spirit began. His saying, I am in the F | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He means by this, that whereas He was in the Father by the nature of His divinity, and we in Him by means of His birth in the flesh; He on the other hand should be believed to be in us by the mys | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By love, and the observance of His commandments, that will be perfected in us which He has begun, viz. that we should be in Him, and He in us. And that this blessedness may be understood to be promi | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that has them in, and keeps them in life; he that has them in words, and keeps them in works; he that has them by hearing, and keeps them by doing; he that has them by doing, and keeps them by pe | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, You think that by sorrowing, as you do, for my death you prove your affection; but I esteem the keeping of My commandments the evidence of love. And then He shows the privileged state | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I will love him, as if now He did not love him. What means this? He explains it in what follows: And will manifest Myself to him, i.e. I love him so far as to manifest Myself to him; so that, as the | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He promises to show Himself to them that love Him as God with the Father, not in that body which He bore upon earth, and which the wicked saw. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For, as after the resurrection He was to appear to them in a body more assimilated to His divinity, that they might not take Him then for a spirit, or a phantom, He tells them now beforehand not to  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having said, A little while, and the world sees Me no more: but you shall see Me: Judas, not the traitor named Iscariot, but he whose Epistle is read among the Canonical Scriptures, asks Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If you would prove your love, show your works. The love of God is never idle; whenever it is, it does great things: if it do not work, it is not. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Love distinguishes the saints from the world: it makes men to be of one mind in an house; in which house the Father and the Son take their abode; who give that love to those, to whom in the end they | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Into some hearts He comes, but not to make His abode with them. For some feel compunction for a season and turn to God, but in time of temptation forget that which gave them compunction, and return  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But while the Father and the Son make Their abode with the loving, soul, is the Holy Spirit excluded? What means that which is said of the Holy Spirit above: He dwells with you, and shall be in you, | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In proportion as a man’s love rests upon lower things, in that proportion is he removed from heavenly love: He that loves Me not, keeps not My sayings. To the love then of our Maker, let the tongue, | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus Judas thought that he should, see Him, as we see the dead in sleep: How is it, that you will manifest Yourself to us, and not to the world? meaning, Alas, as you art to die, you will appear  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And perhaps there is a distinction at bottom, since He speaks of His sayings, when they are His own, in the plural number; as when He says, He that loves Me not, keeps not My sayings: when they are  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These things have I spoken to you, being yet present with you. Some of these things were obscure, and not understood by the disciples. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): The abode He promised them hereafter is altogether a different one from this present abode He now speaks of. The one is spiritual and inward, the other outward, and perceptible to the bodily sight and | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To enable them to sustain His bodily departure more cheerfully, He promises that that departure shall be the source of great benefit; for that while He was then in the body, they could never know mu | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Paraclete is Advocate, or Comforter. The Advocate then intercedes with the Father for sinners, when by His inward power He moves the sinner to pray for himself. The Comforter relieves the sorrow of  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He often calls Him the Comforter, in allusion to the affliction in which they then were. DIDYMUS. The Savior affirms that the Holy Spirit is sent by the Father, in His, the Savior’s, name; which nam | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Unless the Spirit be present to the mind of the hearer, the word of the teacher is vain. Let none then attribute to the human teacher, the understanding which follows in consequence of his teaching: | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So then the Son speaks, the Holy Spirit teaches: when the Son speaks we take in the words, when the Holy Spirit teaches, we understand those words. The whole Trinity indeed both speaks and teaches,  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why is it said of the Spirit, He shall suggest all stings to you: to suggest being the office of an inferior? The word is used here, as it is used sometimes, in the sense of supplying secretly.  | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Suggest, i.e. bring to your remembrance. Every wholesome hint to remember that we receive is of the grace of the Spirit. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Holy Spirit then was both to teach and to bring to remembrance: to teach what Christ had forborne to tell His disciples, because they were not able to bear it; to bring to remembrance what Chris | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peace I leave with you, My peace I give to you: He says this to console His disciples, who were now troubled at the prospect of the hatred and opposition which awaited them after His departure. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He left no peace in this world; in which we conquer the enemy, and have love one to another: He will give us peace in the world to come, when we shall reign without an enemy, and where we shall be a | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . External peace is often even hurtful, rather than profitable to those who enjoy it. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But there is a peace which is serenity of thought, tranquillity of mind, simplicity of heart, the bond of love, the fellowship of charity. None will be able to come to the inheritance of the Lord wh | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After saying, Peace I leave with you, which was like taking farewell, He consoles them: Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid: the two feelings of love and fear were now the upper | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Though He was only going for a time, their hearts would be troubled and afraid for what might happen before He returned; lest in the absence of the Shepherd the wolf might attack the flock: you have | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: If the Father is greater by virtue of giving, is the Son less by confessing the gift? The giver is the greater, but He to whom unity with that giver is given, is not the less. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The Apostles did not yet know what the resurrection was of which He spoke when He said, I go, and come again to you: or what they ought to think of it. They only knew the great power of the | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But is not the time for belief before a thing takes place? Is it not the praise of faith, that it believes what it does not see? according to what is said below to Thomas: Because you have seen, you | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He next alludes to the approach of the time when He would resume His glory. Hereafter 1 will not talk much with you. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says this because the time was now approaching for His being taken, and given up to death: For the Prince of this world comes. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. the devil; the prince of sinners, not of creatures; as the Apostle said, Against the rulers of this world. Or, as He immediately adds by way of explanation, this darkness, meaning, the ungodly. | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the Son is obedient to the will and commandment of the Father, no more shows a difference in the two, than it would in a human father and son. But over and above this comes the consideration th | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 14:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Arise, let us go hence, is the beginning of the sentence which l, follows. The time and the place (they were in the midst of a town, and it was night time) had excited the disciples’ fears to such a | src: CAJohn.htm#14
John 15:3 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He rises in haste to perform the sacrament of His final passion in the flesh (such is His desire to fulfill His Father’s commandment) and therefore takes occasion to unfold the mystery of His assump | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says this as being the Head of the Church, of which we are the members, the Man Christ Jesus; for the vine and the branches are of the same nature. When He says, I am the true vine, He does not m | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But He wholly separates this humiliation in the flesh from the form of the Paternal Majesty, by setting forth the Father as the diligent Husbandman of this vine: And My Father is the Husbandman. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For we cultivate God, and God cultivates us. But our culture of God does not make Him better: our culture is that of adoration, not of plowing: His culture of us makes us better. His culture consist | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And forasmuch as Christ was sufficient for Himself, but His disciples needed the help of the Husbandman, of the vine He says nothing, but adds concerning the branches, Every branch in Me that bears  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The useless and deceitful branches He cuts down for burning. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And inasmuch as even the best of men require the work of the husbandman, He adds, And every branch that bears fruit, He purges it, that it may bring forth more fruit. He alludes here to the tribulat | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:3 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And who is there in this world so clean, that he cannot be more and more changed? Here, if we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves. He cleans then the clean, i.e. the fruitful, that the cle | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You are clean through the word which I have spoken to you, i.e., you have been enlightened by My doctrine, and been delivered from Jewish error. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said that they were clean through the word which He had spoken to them, He now taught them that they must do their part. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Abide in Me, and I in you: not they in Him, as He in them; for both are for the profit not of Him, but them. The branches do not confer any advantage upon the vine, but receive their support from it | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All the fruit of good works proceeds from this root. He who has delivered us by His grace, also carries us onward by his help, so that we bring forth more fruit. Wherefore He repeats, and explains w | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But lest any should suppose that a branch could bring forth a little fruit of itself, He adds, For without Me you can do nothing. He does not say, you can do little. Unless the branch abides in the  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Son then contributes no less than the Father to the help of the disciples. The Father changes, but the Son keeps them in Him, which is that which makes the branches fruitful. And again, the clea | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And men gather them, i.e., the reapers, the Angels, and cast them into the fire, everlasting fire, and they are burned. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the branches of the vine are as contemptible, if they abide not in the vine, as they are glorious, if they abide. One of the two the branch must be in, either the vine, or the fire: if it is not | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:7 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then He shows what it is to abide in Him. If you abide in Me, and My words abide in you, you shall ask what you will and it shall be done to you. It is to be shown by their works. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For then may His words be said to abide in us, when we do what He has commanded, and love what He has promised. But when His words abide in the memory and are not found in the life, the branch is no | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord showed above, that those who plotted against them should be burned, inasmuch as they abode not in Christ: now He shows that they themselves would be invincible, bringing forth much fruit; H | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The fruit of the Apostles are the Gentiles, who through their teaching were converted to the faith, and brought into subjection to the glory of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Made bright or glorified; the Greek word may be translated in either way. In Greek it signifies glory; not our own glory, we must remember, as if we had it of ourselves: it is of His grace that we h | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If then I love you, be of good cheer; if it is the Father’s glory that you bring forth good fruit, bear no evil. Then to rouse them to exertion, He adds, Continue you in My love; and then shows how  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Who doubts that love precedes the observance of the commandments? For who loves not, has not that whereby to keep the commandments. These words then do not declare whence love arises, but how it is  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Even as 1 have kept My Father’s commandments. The Apostle explains what these commandments were: Christ became obedient to death, even the death of the cross (Phil 2:8). | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then because the Passion was now approaching to interrupt their joy, He adds, These things have I spoken to you, that my joy may remain in you: as if He said, And if sorrow fall upon you, I will tak | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And what is Christ’s joy in us, but that He deigns to rejoice on our account? And what is our joy, which He says shall be full, but to have fellowship with Him? He had perfect joy on our account, wh | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, If you keep My commandments, you shall abide in My love, He shows what commandments they are to keep: This is My commandment, That you love one another. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But when all our Lord’s sacred discourses are full of His commandments, why does He give this special commandment respecting love, if it is not that every commandment teaches love, and all precepts  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Where then love is, what can be wanting? Where it is not, what can profit? But this love is distinguished from men’s love to each other as men, by adding, As I have loved you. To what end did Christ | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The highest, the only proof of love, is to love our adversary; as did the Truth Himself, who while He suffered on the cross, showed His love for His persecutors: Father, forgive them, for they know  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said, This is My commandment: that you love one another, even as I have loved you (1 Jn 3); it follows, as John said in his Epistle, that as Christ laid down His life for us, so we should lay | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But whoever in time of tranquillity will not give up his time to God, how in persecution will he give up his soul? Let the virtue of love then, that it may be victorious in tribulation, be nourished | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): From one and the same love, we love God and our neighbor, but God for His own sake, our neighbor for God’s. So that, there being two precepts of love, on which hang all the Law and the Prophets, to lo | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A friend is as it were a keeper of the soul. He who keeps God’s commandments, is rightly called His friend. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Great condescension! Though to keep his Lord’s commandments is only what a good servant is obliged to do, yet, if they do so, He calls them His friends. The good servant is both the servant and the  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, The servant knows not the counsels of his lord; but since I esteem you friends, I have communicated my secrets to you. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how did He make known to His disciples all things that He had heard from the Father, when He forebore saying many things, because He knew they as yet could not bear them? He made all things know | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or all things which He heard from the Father, which He wished to be made known to His servants: the joys of spiritual love, the pleasures of our heavenly country, which He impresses daily on our min | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All things, i.e., all things that they ought to hear. I have heard, shows that what He had taught was no strange doctrine, but received from the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But let no one who has attained to this dignity of being called the friend of God, attribute this superhuman gift to his own merits: You have not chosen Me, but I have chosen you. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Ineffable grace! For what were we before Christ had chosen us, but wicked, and lost? We did not believe in Him, so as to be chosen by Him: for had He chosen us believing, He would have chosen us cho | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They are chosen then before the foundation of the world, according to that predestination by which God foreknew His future acts. They are chosen out of the world by that call whereby God fulfills wh | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe, He does not choose the good; but those, whom He has chosen, He makes good: And I have ordained you that you should go, and bring forth fruit. This is the fruit which He meant, when He said, | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:16 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I have set you, i.e., have planted you by grace, that you should go by will: to will being to go in mind, and bring forth fruit, by works. What kind of fruit they should bring forth He then shows: A | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Love then is one fruit, now existing in desire only, not yet in fullness. Yet even with this desire whatever we ask in the name of the Only-Begotten Son, the Father gives us: That whatsoever you sha | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord had said, I have ordained that you should walk and bring forth fruit. Love is this fruit. Wherefore, He proceeds: These things I command you, that you love one another. Hence the Apostle sa | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: I have said that I lay down My life for you, and that I first chose you. I have said this not by way of reproach, but to induce you to love one another. Then as they were about to suffer pe | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For why should the members exalt themselves above the head? You refuse to be in the body, if you are not willing, with the head, to endure the hatred of the world. For love’s sake let us be patient; | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if Christ’s suffering were not consolation enough, He consoles them still further by telling them, the hatred of the world would be an evidence of their goodness; so that they ought rather to gri | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said this to the whole Church which is often called the world; as God was in Christ, reconciling the world to Himself (2 Cor 5:19). The whole world then is the Church, and the whole world hates t | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the dispraise of the perverse, is our praise. There is nothing wrong in not pleasing those who do not please God. For no one can by one and the same act please God, and the enemies of God. He pr | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord, in exhorting His servants to bear patiently the hatred of their world, proposes to them an example than which there can be no better and higher one, viz. Himself: Remember the word that I  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They observed it in order to calumniate it, as we read in the Psalms, The ungodly sees the righteousness. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: If, He says, they have persecuted your Lord, much more will they persecute you; if they had persecuted Him, but kept His commandments they would keep yours also. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, you must not be disturbed at having to share My sufferings; for you are not better than I. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The servant is not greater than his lord. Here the servant is the one who has the purified fear, which abides for ever. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then follows another consolation, viz. that the Father is despised and injured with them: But all these things will they do unto you for My name’s sake, because they know not Him that sent Me. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All these things, viz. what He had mentioned, that the world would hate them, persecute them, despise their word. For My Name’s sake, i.e., in you they will hate Me, in you persecute Me, your word t | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then by way of another consolation, He declares the injustice of these persecutions both towards Him had them. If I had not come and spoken to them, they had not had sin. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Christ spoke to the Jews only, not to any other nation. In them then was that world which hated Christ and His disciples; and not only in them, but in us also. Were the Jews then without sin before  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): As the Jews persecuted Him out of professed regard for the Father, He takes away this excuse: He that hates Me, hates My Father also. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For as he who loves the Son, loves the Father also, the love of the Father being one with that of the Son, even as their nature is one: so he who hates the Son, hates the Father also. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But He has just said, Because they know not Him that sent Me. How could they hate one whom they did not know? For if they hated God, believing Him to be something else; and not God, this was not hat | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus then they have no excuse, He says; I gave them doctrine, I added miracles, which, according to Moses’ law, should convince all if the doctrine itself is good also: If l had not done among them  | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sin of not believing Him, notwithstanding His doctrine and His miracles. But why does He add, Which none other man did? Christ did no work greater than the raising of the dead, which we know the | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And that the disciples may not say, Why then have you brought us into such difficulties? Could not you foresee the resistance and hatred we should meet with, He quotes the prophecy: But this comes t | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Under the name of the Law, the whole of the Old Testament is included; and therefore our Lord says here, That is written in their law, the passage being in the Psalms. | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their law, He says, not as made by them, but as given to them. A man hates without a cause, who seeks no advantage from his hatred. Thus the ungodly hate God; the righteous love Him, i.e. looking fo | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is one thing not to do good, another to hate the teacher of goodness; as there is a difference between sudden and deliberate sins. Our state generally is that we love what is good, but from infir | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples might say, If they have heard words from Thee, such as none other has spoken, if they have seen works of Him, such as none other has done, and yet have not been convinced, but have hat | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Seeing Me, they hated and killed Me; but the Comforter shall give such testimony concerning Me as shall make them believe, though they see Me not. And because He shall testify, you sh | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls Him not the Holy Spirit, but the Spirit of truth, to show the perfect faith that was due to Him. He knew that He proceeds from the Father, for He knew all things; He knew where He Himself c | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Elsewhere He says that the Father sends the Spirit; now He says He does: Whom I will send to you, thus declaring the equality of the Father and the Son. That He might not be thought however to be op | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 15:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If it be asked here whether the Holy Ghost proceeds from the Son also, we may answer thus: The Son is the Son of the Father alone, and the Father is the Father of the Son only; but the Holy Spirit i | src: CAJohn.htm#15
John 16:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After the promise of the Holy Spirit, to inspire them with strength to give witness; He well adds, These things have I spoken to you, that you should not be offended. For when the love of God is she | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For the Jews had already agreed, if any confessed that He was Christ, that he should be put out of the synagogue. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Bu what evil was it to the Apostles to be put out of the Jewish synagogues, which they would have gone out of, even if none had put them out? Our Lord wished to make known to them, that the Jews wer | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then He consoles them: And all these things will they do to you, because they have not known the Father nor Me. As if He said, Let this consolation content you. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And He mentions these things beforehand, because trials, however soon to pass away, when they come upon men unprepared for them, are very overwhelming: But these things have I told you, that when th | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And He predicted these trials for another reason, viz. that they might not say that He had not foreseen them; That you may remember that I told you of them, or that He had only spoken to please them | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:4 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the other three Evangelists these predictions occur before the supper; John gives them after. Still if they relate them as given very near His Passion, that is enough to explain His saying, These | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He had foretold that they should suffer scourgings, but not that their death could be thought doing God service; which was the strangest thing of all. Or, He there told them what they would suff | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples, not as yet perfected, being overcome by sorrow, our Lord blames and corrects them, saying, But now I go My way to Him that sent Me; and none of you asks Me, Where do you go? They were | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or whereas they had asked Him above, where He was going, and He had replied that He was going where they would not come; now He promises that He will go in such a way that no one will ask Him where  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Though your grief be ever so great, you must hear how that it is profitable for you that I go away. What the profit is He then shows: For if I go not away, the Comforter will not come | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This He says not on account of any inequality between the Word of God and the Holy Ghost, but because the presence of the Son of man amongst them would impede the coming of the latter. For the Holy  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But could He not send Him while here: Him Who, we know, came and abode on Him at His baptism, yea Him from Whom we know He never could be separated? What means then, If I go not away, the Comforter  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said plainly, If I withdraw not My body from your eyes, I cannot lead you to the understanding of the Invisible, through the Comforting Spirit. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Holy Ghost the Comforter brought this, that the form of a servant which our Lord had received in the womb of the Virgin, being removed from the fleshly eye, He was manifested to the purified men | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What say they here, who entertain unworthy notions of the Spirit? Is it expedient for the master to go away, and a servant to come? He then shows the good that the Spirit will do: And when He is com | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how is it that Christ did not reprove the world? Is it because Christ spoke among the Jews only, whereas the Holy Spirit, poured into His disciples throughout the whole world, reproved not one n | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it makes a great difference whether one believes in Christ, or only that He is Christ. For that He was Christ, even the devils believed; but he believes in Christ who both hopes in Christ and lo | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The world is reproved of sin, because it believes not in Christ, and reproved of righteousness, the righteousness of those that believe. The very contrast of the believing, is the censure of the unb | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: They believed not, He went to the Father. Theirs therefore was the sin, His the righteousness. But that He came from the Father to us was mercy; that He went to the Father was righteousness | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, judged, i.e., is destined irrevocably for the punishment of eternal fire. And of this judgment is the world reproved, in that it is judged with its prince, the proud and ungodly one whom it imit | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Shall reprove the world of sin, i.e., cut off all excuse and show that they have sinned unpardonably in not believing in Me, when they see the ineffable gift of the Holy Ghost obtained by c | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In this way too the Holy Ghost reproved the world of sin, i.e. by the mighty works He did in the name of the Savior, Who was condemned by the world. The Savior, His righteousness retained, feared no | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. My going to tile Father will be a proof that I have led an irreproachable life, so that they will not be able to say, This man is a sinner; this man is not from God. Again inasmuch as I conquer | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The devils seeing souls go from hell to heaven, knew that the prince of this world was judged and, being brought to trial in the Savior’s cause, had lost all right to what he held. This was seen on  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having said above, It is expedient for you that I go away, He enlarges now upon it: I have yet many things to say to you, but you cannot bear them now. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All heretics, when their fables are rejected for their extravagance by the common sense of mankind, try to defend themselves by this text; as if these were the things which the disciples could not a | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having said then, you cannot bear them now, but then you shall be able, and, The Holy Spirit shall lead you into all truth; lest this should make them suppose that the Holy Spirit was the superior,  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is like what He said of Himself above, i.e., I can of My own Self do nothing; as I hear I judge. But that may be understood of Hi m as man; how must we understand this of the Holy Ghost, Who ne | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it does not follow from hence that the Holy Spirit is inferior; for it is only signified that He proceeds from the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor let the use of the future tense perplex you; that hearing is eternal, because the knowledge is eternal. To that which is eternal, without beginning, and without end, a verb of any tense may be a | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is certain that many filled with the grace of the Holy Spirit have foreknown future events. But as many gifted saints have never had this power, the words, He will show you things to come, may be | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In this way then He raised their spirits; for there is nothing for which mankind so long, as the knowledge of the future. He relieves them from all anxiety on this account, by showing that dangers w | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By pouring love into the hearts of believers, and making them spiritual, and so able to see that the Son Whom they had known before only according to the flesh, and thought a man like themselves, wa | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because He had said, You have one Master, even Christ (Matt 23:8), that they might not be prevented by this from admitting the Holy Ghost as well, He adds, For He shall receive of Mine, and shal | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:15 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But it is not true, as some heretics have thought, that because the Son receives from the Father, the Holy Ghost from the Son, as if by gradation, that therefore the Holy Ghost is inferior to the So | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord therefore has not left it uncertain whether the Paraclete be from the Father, or from the Son; for He is sent by the Son, and proceeds from the Father; both these He receives from the Son.  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord after having relieved the spirits of the disciples by the promise of the Holy Spirit, again depresses them: A little while, and you shall not see Me. He does this to accustom them to the me | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He said, A little while, and you shall not see Me alluding to His going to be taken that night by the Jews, His crucifixion the next morning, and burial in the evening, which withdrew Him from all h | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But then, if one examines, these are words of consolation: Because I go to the Father. For they show that His death was only a translation; and more consolation follows: And again, a little while, a | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The meaning of these words however was obscure, before their fulfillment; Then said some of His disciples among themselves, What is this that He said to us, A little while, and you shall not see Me; | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Either sorrow had confused their minds, or the obscurity of the words themselves prevented their understanding them, and made them appear contradictory. If we shall see Thee, they say, how do You go | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For above, because He did not say, A little while, but simply, I go to the Father, He seemed to speak plainly. But what to them was obscure at the time, but by and by manifested, is manifest to us.  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus, It will be a little time during which you will not see Me, i.e. the three days that He rested in the grave; and again, it will be a little time during which you shall see Me, i.e. the forty | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which must be understood thus: viz. that the disciples sorrowed at their Lord’s death, and then immediately rejoiced at His resurrection. The world (i.e. the enemies of Christ, who put Him to death) | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But this speech of our Lord’s is applicable to all believers who strive through present tears and afflictions to attain to the joys eternal. While the righteous weep, the world rejoices; for having  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then He shows that sorrow brings forth joy, short sorrow infinite joy, by an example from nature: A woman when she is in travail has sorrow, because her hour is come; but as soon as she is delivered | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): This comparison does not seem difficult to understand. It was one which lay near at hand, and He Himself immediately shows its application. And you now therefore have sorrow; but I will see you again, | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By this example He also intimates that He loosens the chains of death, and creates men anew. He does not say however that she should not have tribulation, but that she should not remember it; so gre | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To this joy it is better to refer what was said above: A little while and you shall not see Me, and again, a little while and you shall see Me. For the whole space of time that this world continues  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The woman is the holy Church, who is fruitful in good works, and brings forth spiritual children to God. This woman, while she brings forth, i.e. while she is making her progress in the world, amids | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor yet in this bringing forth of joy, are we entirely without joy to lighten our sorrow, but, as the Apostle said, we rejoice in hope (Romans 12:12); for even the woman, to whom we are compared, re | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But as soon as she is delivered, i.e. when her laborious struggle is over, and she has got the palm, she remembers no more her former anguish, for joy at reaping such a reward, for joy that a man is | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor should it appear strange, if one who departs from this life is said to be born. For as a man is said to be born when he comes out of his mother’s womb into the light of day, so may he be said to | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:22 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I will see you again, i.e. I will take you to Myself. Or, I will see you again, i.e. I shall appear again and be seen by you; and your heart shall rejoice. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This fruit indeed the Church now yearns for in travail, but then will enjoy in her delivery. And it is a male child, because all active duties are for the sake of devotion; for that only is free whi | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Again our Lord shows that it is expedient that He should go: And in that day shall you ask Me nothing. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The word ask here means not only to seek for, but to ask a question: the Greek word from which it is translated has both meanings. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, And in that day, i.e. when I shall have risen again, you shall ask Me nothing, i.e. not say to Me, Show us the Father, and, Where do You go? since you will know this by the teaching of the  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I or when your prayers shall be fully answered, then will your gladness be greatest. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These words being obscure, He adds, These things have I spoken to you in proverbs, but the time comes when l shall no more speak to you in proverbs; for forty days He talked with them as they were a | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He still cheers them with the promise that help will be given them from above in their temptations: At that day you shall ask in My Name. And you will be so in favor with the Father, that you will n | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But does He love us because we love Him; or rather do not we love Him, because He loved us; This is what the Evangelist says, Let us love God, because God first loved us (1 Jn 4:19). The Father then | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perfect faith in the Son, which believes and loves what has come forth from God, and deserves to be heard and loved for its own sake, this faith confessing the Son of God, born from Him, and sent by | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As it was consolatory to them to hear of His resurrection, and how He came from God, and went to God, He dwells again and again on these subjects: Again, I leave the world, and go to the Father. The | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He came forth from the Father, because He is of the Father; He came into the world, because He showed Himself in the body to the world. He left the world by His departure in the body, and went to th | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is His meaning then: In the world to come, you shall ask me nothing: but in the mean time while you are traveling on this wearisome road, ask what you want of the Father, and He will give it yo | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The word whatsoever must not be understood to mean anything, but something which with reference to obtaining the life of blessedness is not nothing. That is not sought in the Savior’s name, which is | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And this is that full joy, than which nothing can be greater, viz. to enjoy God, the Trinity, in the image of Whom we are made. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Whatsoever then is asked, which appertains to the getting this joy, this must be asked in the name of Christ. For His saints that persevere in asking for it, He will never in His divine mercy disapp | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:28 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When He declares that He will show them plainly of the Father, He alludes to the manifestation about to take place of His own majesty which would troth show His own equality With the Father and the  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But this sense seems to be interfered with by what follows: At that day you shall ask in My name. What shall we have to ask for in a future life, when all our desires shall be satisfied; Asking impl | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples were so refreshed with the thought of being in favor with the Father, that they say they are sure He knows all things: His disciples said to Him, Now you speak plainly, and speak no pr | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why do they say so, when the hour in which He was to speak without proverbs was yet future, and only promised? Because, our Lord’s communications still continuing proverbs to them, they are so f | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But since His answer met what was in their minds, they add, Now we are sure that you know all things. See how imperfect they yet were, after so many and great things now at last to say, Now we are s | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why this remark? To one Who knew all things, instead of saying, you need not that any man should ask You; it would have been more appropriate to have said, you need not to ask any man; yet we know t | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They believe that He came forth from God, because He does the works of God. For whereas our Lord had said both, I came forth from the Father, and, I am come into the world from the Father, they test | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lastly, He reminds them of their w weak tender age in respect of the inner man. Jesus answered them, Do you now believe? | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which can be understood in two ways, either as reproaching or affirming. If the former, the meaning is, you have awaked somewhat late to belief, for behold the hour comes, yea is now come, that you  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For they did not only with their bodies leave His body, when He was taken, but with their minds the faith. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You shall be scattered; i.e. when I am betrayed, fear shall so possess you, that you will not be able even to take to flight together. But I shall suffer no harm in consequence: And yet I am not alo | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He wishes to advance them so far as to understand that He had not separated from the Father because He had come forth from the Father. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These things have I said to you, that you might have peace; i.e. that you may not reject Me from your minds. For not only when I am taken shall you suffer tribulation, but so long as you are in the  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if He said, Have Me within you to comfort you, because you will have the world without you. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The tribulation of which He speaks was to commence thus, i.e. in every one being scattered to his home, but was not to continue so. For in saying, And leave Me alone, He does not mean this to apply  | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. raise up your spirits again; when the Master is victorious, the disciples should not be dejected; I have overcome the world. | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 16:33 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the Holy Spirit was given them, they were of good cheer, and, in His strength, victorious. For He would not have overcome the world, had the world overcome His members. When He says, These thin | src: CAJohn.htm#16
John 17:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After having said, In the world you shall have tribulation, our Lord turns from admonition to prayer; thus teaching us in our tribulations to abandon all other things, and flee to God. | src: CAJohn.htm#17
