John 18:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They had heard at the first, I am He, but had not understood it; because He who could do whatever He would, willed not that they should. But had He never permitted Himself to be taken by them, they  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, Though you seek Me, you have nothing to do with these: lo, I give Myself up: thus even to the last hour does He show His love for His own. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He commands His enemies, and they do what He commands; they permit them to go away, whom He would not have design | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist, to show that it was not their design to do this, but that His power did it, adds, That the saying might be fulfilled which He spoke, Of them which you have given Me, have I lost none | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But were the disciples never to die? Why then would He lose them, even if they died then? Because they did not yet believe in Him in a saving way. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peter trusting to these last words of our Lord’s, and to what He had just done, assaults those who came to take Him: Then Simon Peter having a sword drew it, and smote the high priest’s servant. But | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, he had got one for sacrificing the lamb, and carried it away with him from the Supper. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how could he, who had been forbidden ever to strike on the cheek, be a murderer? Because what he had been forbidden to do was to avenge himself, but here he was not avenging himself, but his Mas | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The servant’s name was Malchus; John is the only Evangelist who mentions the servant’s name; as Luke is the only one who mentions that our Lord touched the ear and healed him. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He wrought this miracle both to teach us, that we ought to do good to those who suffer and to manifest His power. The Evangelist gives the name, that those who then read it might have the opportunit | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The name Malchus signifies, about to reign. What then does the ear cut off for our Lord, and healed by our Lord denote, but the abolition of the old, and the creating of a new, hearing in the newnes | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, the cutting off of the high priest’s servant’s right ear is a type of the people’s deafness, of which the chief priests partook most strongly: the restoration of the ear, of ultimate reenlighten | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord condemned Peter’s act, and forbade him proceeding further: Then said Jesus to Peter, Put up your sword into the sheath. He was to be admonished to have patience: and this was written for ou | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He not only restrained Him however by threats, but consoled him also at the same time: The cup that My Father gives Me, shall I not drink it? Whereby He shows that it was not by their power, but by  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In that He calls it a cup, He shows how pleasing and acceptable death for the salvation of men was to Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The cup being given Him by the Father, is the same with what the Apostle says, Who spared not His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all. But the Giver of this cup and the Drinker of it are the sa | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Every thing having been done that could be to dissuade the Jews, and they refusing to take warning, He suffered Himself to be delivered into their hands: Then the band and the captain and officers o | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They took Him Whom they did not draw nigh to; nor understood that which is written in the Psalms, Draw nigh to Him, and be you lightened. For had they thus drawn nigh to Him, they would have taken H | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In exultation, to show what they had done, as if they were raising a trophy. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why they did so, he tells us immediately after: For he was father in law to Caiaphas, which was the high priest that same year. Matthew, in order to shorten the narrative, says that He was led to Ca | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In order that, while our Lord was condemned by his colleague, he might not be guiltless, though his crime was less. Or perhaps his house lay in the way, and they were obliged to pass by it. Or it wa | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:14 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Josephus relates that this Caiaphas bought the high priesthood for this year. No wonder then if a wicked high priest judged wickedly. A man who was advanced to the priesthood by avarice would keep h | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That no one however might be disturbed at the sound of the chains, the Evangelist reminds them of the prophecy that His death would be the salvation of the world: Now Caiaphas was he which gave coun | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The temptation of Peter, which took place in the midst of the contumelies offered to our Lord, is not placed by all in the same order. Matthew and Mark put the contumelies first, the temptation of P | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He followed his Master out of devotion, though afar off, on account of fear. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Who that other disciple was we cannot hastily decide, as his name is not told us. John however is accustomed to signify himself by this expression, with the addition of, whom Jesus loved. Perhaps th | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He omits his own name out of humility: though he is relating an act of great virtue, how that he followed when the rest fled. He puts Peter before himself, and then mentions himself, in order to sho | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He stood without, as being about to deny his Lord. He was not in Christ, who dared not confess Christ. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But that Peter would have entered the palace, if he had been permitted, appears by what immediately follows: Then went out that other disciple who was known to the high priest, and spoke to her who  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But what wonder, if God foretold truly, man presumed falsely. Respecting this denial of Peter we should remark, that Christ is not only denied by him, who denies that He is Christ, but by him also w | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Therefore did Divine Providence permit Peter first to fall, in order that he might be less severe to sinners from the remembrance of his own fall. Peter, the teacher and master of the whole world, s | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some however foolishly favor Peter, so far as to say that he denied Christ, because he did not wish to be away from Christ, and he knew, they say, that if he confessed that he was one of Christ’s di | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was not winter, and yet it was cold, as it often is at the vernal equinox. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The fire of love was smothered in Peter’s breast, and he was warming himself before the coals of the persecutors, i.e. with the love of this present life, whereby his weakness was increased. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As they could bring no charge against Christ, they asked Him of His disciples: The high priest then asked Jesus of His disciples; perhaps where they were, and on what account He had collected them,  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He asks Him moreover of His doctrine, what it was, whether opposed to Moses and the law, that he might take occasion thereby to put Him to death as an enemy of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not ask in order to know the truth, but to find out some charge against Him, on which to deliver Him to the Roman Governor to be condemned. But our Lord so tempers His answer, as neither to  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There is a difficulty here not to be passed over: if He did not speak openly even to His disciples, but only promised that He would do so at some time, how was it that He spoke openly to the world?  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He refers here to the prophecy of Esaias; I have not spoken in, secret, in a dark place of the earth. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:21 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He spoke in secret, but not, as these thought, from fear, or to excite sedition; but only when what He said was above the understanding of the many. To establish the matter, however, upon supera | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For what they had beard and not understood was not of such a kind, as that they could justly turn it against Him. And as often as they tried by questioning to find out some charge against Him, He so | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When Jesus had appealed to the testimony of the people by, an officer, wishing to clear himself, and show that he was not one of those who admired our Lord, struck Him: And when He had thus spoken,  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This shows that Annas was the high priest, for this was before He was sent to Caiaphas. And Luke in the beginning of his Gospel says, that Annas and Caiaphas were both high priests. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here is fulfilled the prophecy, I gave my cheek to the smiters. Jesus, though struck unjustly, replied gently: Jesus answered him, If I have spoken evil, bear witness of the evil: but if well, why s | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, If you have any fault to find with what I have said, show it; if you have not, why do you rage? Or thus: If I taught any thing unadvisedly, when I taught in the synagogues, give proof  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What can be truer, gentler, kinder, than this answer? He Who received the blow on the face neither wished for him who struck it that fire from heaven should consume him, or the earth open its month  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What should they do then but either disprove, or admit, what He said? Yet this they do not do: it is not a trial they are carrying on, but a faction, a tyranny. Not knowing what to do further, they  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thinking that as he was more cunning, he might find out something against Him worthy of death. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was the one to whom they were taking Him from the first, as Matthew says; he being the high priest of this year. We must understand that the pontificate was taken between them year by year altern | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Sent Him bound, not that He was bound now for the first time, for they bound Him when they took Him. They sent Him bound as they had brought Him. Or perhaps He may have been loosed from His bonds fo | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After the Evangelist has said that they sent Jesus bound from Annas to Caiaphas, he returns to Peter and his three denials, which took place in the house of Annas: And Simon Peter stood and warmed h | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, He means that the once fervid disciple was now too torpid, to move even when our Lord was carried away: strewing thereby how weak man’s nature is, when God forsakes him. Asked again, he again de | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here we find Peter not at the gate, but at the fire, when he denies the second time: so that he must have returned after he had gone out of doors, where Matthew says he was. He did not go out, and a | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But neither did the garden bring back to his memory what he had then said, and the great professions of love he had made: Peter then denied again, and immediately the cock crew. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo, the prophecy of the Physician is fulfilled, the presumption of the sick man demonstrated. That which Peter had said he would do, he had not done. I will lay down my life for your sake, but what  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelists have all given the same account of the denials of Peter, not with any intention of throwing blame upon him, but to teach us how hurtful it is to trust in self, and not ascribe all to | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, by the first denial of Peter are denoted those who before our Lord’s Passion denied that He was God, by the second, those who did so after His resurrection. So by the first crowing of th | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist returns to the part where he had left off, in order to relate Peter’s denial: Then led they Jesus to Caiaphas to the hall of judgment: to Caiaphas from his colleague and father in law | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The praetorium is the place where the praetor sat. Praetors were called prefects and preceptors, because they issue decrees. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Where then for some urgent reason Caiaphas proceeded from the house of Annas, where both had been sitting, to the praetorium of the governor, and left Jesus to the hearing of his father in law: or P | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . According to Matthew, When the morning came, they led Him away, and delivered Him to Pontius Pilate. But He was to have been led to Caiaphas at first. How is it then that He was brought to him so la | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was led to Caiaphas before the cock crowed, but early in the morning to Pilate. Whereby the Evangelist shows, that all that night of examination, ended in proving nothing against Him; and that He | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And they themselves entered not into the judgment hall: i.e. into that part of the house which Pilate occupied, supposing it to be the house of Caiaphas. Why they did not enter is next explained: Le | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): For the Jews were then celebrating the passover; He Himself celebrated it one day before, reserving His own death for the sixth day; on which day the old passover was kept. Or, perhaps, the passover m | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The days of unleavened breed were beginning; during which time it was defilement to enter the house of a stranger. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The passover was strictly the fourteenth day of the month, the day on which the lamb was killed in the evening: the seven days following were called the days of unleavened bread, in which nothing le | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O impious blindness! They feared to be defiled by the judgment hall of a foreign prefect, to shed the blood of an innocent brother they feared not. For that He Whom they killed was the Lord and Give | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate however proceeds in a more gentle way: Pilate then went out to them. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was the custom of the Jews when they condemned any one to death, to notify it to the governor, by delivering the man bound. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate however seeing Him bound, and such numbers conducting Him, supposed that they had not unquestionable evidence against Him, so proceeds to ask the question: And said, What accusation bring you | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Ask the freed from unclean spirits, the blind who saw, the dead who came to life again, and, what is greater than all, the fools who were made wise, and let them answer, whether Jesus was a malefact | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But is not this account contradictory to Luke’s, who mentions certain positive charges: And they began to accuse Him, saying, We found this fellow perverting the nation, and forbidding to give tribu | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, Since you will only have such a trial as will suit you, and are proud, as if you never did any thing profane, take you Him, and condemn Him; I will not be made a judge for such a purpo | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or as if he said, you who have the law, know what the law judges concerning such: do what you know to be just. The Jews therefore said to him, It is not lawful for us to put any man to death. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But did not the law command not to spare malefactors, especially deceivers such as they thought Him? We must understand them however to mean, that the holiness of the day which the’ were beginning t | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:32 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, they were not allowed by the Roman law to put Him to death themselves. Or, Pilate having said, Judge Him according to your law, they reply, It is not lawful for us: His sin is not a Jewish one,  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As we read in Mark, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem; and the Son of man shall be delivered to the chief priests, and to the scribes; and they shall condemn Him to death, and shall deliver Him to the G | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate, wishing to rescue Him from the hatred of the c Jews, protracted the trial a long time. Then Pilate entered into the judgment hall, and called Jesus. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. Apart, because he had a strong suspicion that He was innocent, and thought he could examine Him more accurately, away from the crowd: and said to Him, Are you the King of the Jews? | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein Pilate shows that the Jews had charged Him with calling Himself King of the Jews. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or Pilate had heard this by report; and as the Jews had no charge to bring forward, began to examine Him himself with respect to the things commonly reported of Him. Jesus answered him, Say you this | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He intimates here that Pilate was judging blindly and indiscreetly: If you say this thing of yourself, He says, bring forward proofs of My rebellion; if you have heard it from others, make regular i | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord knew indeed both what He Himself asked, and what Pilate would answer; but He wished it to be written down n for our sakes. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He asks not in ignorance, but in order to draw from Pilate himself an accusation against the Jews: Pilate answered Bred, Am I a Jew? Your own nation and the chief priests have delivered you to me. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He rejects the imputation that He could have said it of Himself; Your own nation and the chief priests have delivered you to me: adding, what have you done? Whereby he shows that this charge had bee | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He then tries to bring round the mind of Pilate, not a very bad man, by proving to him, that He is not a mere man, but God, and the Son of God; and overthrowing all suspicion of His having aimed at  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is what the good Master wished to teach us. But first it was necessary to show the falsity of the notions of both Jews and Gentiles as to His kingdom, which Pilate had heard of; as if it meant  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or He means that He does not derive His kingdom from the same source that earthly kings do; but that He has his sovereignty from above; inasmuch as He is not mere man, but far greater and more glori | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , After showing that His kingdom was not of this world, He adds, But now My kingdom is not from here. He does not say, Not here, for His kingdom is here to the end of the world, having within it the t | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Or He says, from here, not, here; because He reigns in the world, and carries on the government of it, and disposes all things according to His will; but His kingdom is not from below, but from abov | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Heretics infer from these words that our Lord is a different person from the Creator of the world. But when He says, My kingdom is not from here, He does not deprive the world of His government and  | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not fear to confess Himself a King, but so replied as neither to deny that He was, nor yet to confess Himself a King in such sense as that His kingdom should be supposed to be of this w world | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or, to Pilate’s question whether He was a King our Lord answers, To this end was I born, i.e. to be a King, That I am born from a King. proves that I am a King. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If then He was a King by birth, He has nothing which He has not received from another. For this I came, that I should bear witness to the truth, i.e. that I should make all men believe it. We must o | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): But when Christ bears witness to the truth, He bears witness to Himself; as He said above, I am the truth. But inasmuch as all men have not faith, He adds, Everyone that is of the truth hears My voice | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:38 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These words have an effect upon Pilate, persuade him to become a hearer, d elicit from him the short inquiry, What is truth had almost said to Him, What is truth? | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it had almost vanished from the world, and become unknown In consequence of the general unbelief. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After Pilate had asked, What is truth? he remembered a custom of the Jews, of releasing one prisoner at the passover, and did not wait for Christ’s answer, for fear to losing this chance of saving H | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He knew that this question required time to answer, and it was necessary immediately to rescue Him from the fury of the Jews. So he went out. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): , Or, he did not wait to hear the reply, because he was unworthy to hear. And, says to them, I find no fault in Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not say, He has sinned and is worthy, of death; yet release Him at the feast; but acquitting Him in the first place, he does more than he need do, and asks it as a favor, our, that, if they a | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This custom was not commanded in the law, but had been handed down by tradition from the old fathers, viz. that in remembrance of their deliverance out of Egypt, they should release a prisoner at th | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He could not dismiss the idea from his mind, that Jesus was King of the Jews; as if the Truth itself, whom he had just asked what it was, had inscribed it there as a title. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate is judicious in replying that Jesus had done nothing wrong, and that there was no reason to suspect Him of aiming at a kingdom. For they might be sure that if He set Himself up as a King, and | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Upon this they cried out: Then cried they all again, saying, Not this man, but Barabbas. now Barabbas was a robber. We blame you not, O Jews, for releasing a guilty man at the passover, but for kill | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Inasmuch then as they abandoned the Savior, and sought out a robber, to this day the devil practices his robberies upon them. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 18:40 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The name Barabbas signifies, The son of their master; i.e. the devil; his master in his wickedness, the Jews’ in their perfidy. | src: CAJohn.htm#18
John 19:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When the Jews had cried out that they did not wish Jesus to be released on account of the passover, but Barabbas, Then Pilate therefore took Jesus, and scourged Him. Pilate seems to have done this f | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Pilate having called Him the King of the Jews, they put the royal dress upon Him, in mockery | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:5 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For instead of a diadem, they put upon Him a crown of thorns, and a purple robe to represent the purple robe which kings wear. Matthew says, a scarlet robe, but scarlet and purple are different name | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was not at the command of the governor that they did this, but in order to gratify the Jews. For neither were they commanded by him to go to the garden in the night, but the Jews gave them money  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:5 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thus were fulfilled what Christ had prophesied of Himself; thus were martyrs taught to suffer all that the malice of persecutors could inflict; thus that kingdom which was not of this world conquers | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:5 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That the Jews might cease from their fury, seeing Him thus insulted, Pilate brought out Jesus before them crowned: Pilate therefore went forth again and says to them, Behold, I bring Him forth to yo | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hence it is apparent that these things were not done without Pilate’s knowledge, whether he commanded, or only permitted them, for the reason we have mentioned, viz. that His enemies seeing the insu | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The envy of the Jews does not subside at Christ’s disgraces; yea, rather rises: When the chief priests therefore and officers saw Him, they cried out, saying, Crucify Him, crucify Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate saw then that it was all in vain: Pilate says to them, Take you Him, and crucify Him. This is the speech of a man abhorring the deed, and urging others to do a deed which he abhors himself. T | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lo, another greater outbreak of envy. The former was lighter, being only to punish Him for aspiring to a usurpation of the royal power. Yet did Jesus make neither claim falsely; both were true: He w | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While they disputed with each other, He was silent, fulfilling the prophecy, He opens not His mouth; He was taken from prison and from judgment. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This agrees: with Luke’s account, We found this fellow perverting the nation, only with the addition of, because He made Himself the Son of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Then Pilate begins to fear that what had been said might be true, and that he might appear; to be administering justice improperly: When Pilate therefore heard that saying, he was the more afraid. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:8 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was not the law that he was afraid of, as he was a stranger: but he was more afraid, lest he should slay the Son of God | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They were not afraid to say this, that He made Himself the Son of God: but they kill Him for the very reasons for which they ought to have worshipped Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate, agitated with fear, begins again examining Him: And went again into the judgment hall, and says to Jesus, Where are you? He no longer asks, What hast you done? But Jesus gave him no answer.  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In comparing the accounts of the different Evangelists together, we find that this silence was maintained more than once; viz. before the High Priest, before Herod, and before Pilate. So that the pr | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He remaining thus silent, Then says Pilate to Him, Speak you not to me? know you not that I have power to crucify you, and have power to release you? See how he condemns himself. If all depends upon | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So He answers. When He was silent, He was silent not as guilty or crafty, but as a sheep: when He answered, He taught as a shepherd. Let us hear what He says; which is that, as He teaches by His Apo | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:12 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that delivered Me to you, i.e. Judas, or the multitude. When Jesus had boldly replied, that unless He gave Himself up, and the Father consented, Pilate could have had no power over Him, Pilate wa | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate had sought from the first to release: so we must understand, from thence, to mean from this cause, i.e. lest he should incur guilt by putting to death an innocent person. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews thought they could alarm Pilate more by the mention of Caesar, than by telling him of their law, as they had done above; We have a law, and by that law He ought to die, because He made Hims | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how can you prove this? By His purple, His diadem, His chariot, His guards? Did He not wall; about with His twelve disciples only, and every thing mean about Him, food, dress, and habitation? | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate was before afraid not of violating their law by sparing Him, but of killing the Son of God, in killing Him. But he could not treat his master Caesar with the same contempt with which he treat | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): He went out to examine into the matter: his sitting down on the judgment seat shows this. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The tribunal is the seat of the judge, as the throne is the seat of the king, and the chair the seat of the doctor. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Lithostraton, i.e. laid with stone; the word signifies pavement. It was an elevated place. And it was the preparation of the Passover. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Parasceve, i.e. preparation. This was a name for the sixth day, the day before the Sabbath, on which they prepared what was necessary for the Sabbath; as we read, On the sixth day they gathered twic | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why then does Mark say, And it was the third hour, and they crucified Him? Because on the third hour our Lord was crucified by the tongues of the Jews, on the sixth by the hands of the soldiers. So  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some suppose it to be a fault of the transcriber, who for the letter y, three, put s, six. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate, despairing of moving them, did not examine Him, as he intended, but delivered Him up. And he says to the Jews, Behold your King! | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, See the kind of Man whom you suspect of aspiring to the throne, a humble person, who cannot have any such design. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A speech that should have softened their rage; but they were afraid of letting Him go, lest He might draw away the multitude again. For the love of rule is a heavy crime, and sufficient to condemn a | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Pilate still tries to overcome their apprehensions on Caesar’s account; Pilate says to them, Shall I crucify your King? He tries to shame them into doing what he had not been able to soften them int | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:16 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They voluntarily brought themselves under punishment, and God gave them up to it. With one accord they denied the kingdom of God, and God suffered them to fall into their own condemnation; for they  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But Pilate is at last overcome by fear: Then delivered he Him therefore to them to be crucified. For it would be taking part openly against Caesar, if when the Jews declared that they had no king bu | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By the command of the governor, the soldiers took Christ to be crucified. And they took Jesus, and led Him away. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They, i.e. the soldiers, the guards of the governor, as appears more clearly afterwards; Then the soldiers when they had crucified Jesus; though the Evangelist might justly have attributed the whole | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They compel Jesus to bear the cross, regarding it as unholy, and therefore avoiding the touch of it themselves. And He bearing His cross went forth into a place called the place of a skull, which is | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But as there Isaac was let go, and a ram offered; so here too the Divine nature remains impassible, but the human, of which the ram was the type, the offspring of that straying ram, was slain. But w | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Both bore it; first Jesus, as John says, then Simon, as the other three Evangelists say. On first going forth, He bore His own cross. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Great spectacle, to the profane a laughing-stock, to the pious a mystery. Profaneness sees a King bearing a cross instead of a scepter; piety sees a King bearing a cross, thereon to nail Himself, an | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He carried the badge of victory on His shoulders, was conquerors do. Some say that the place of Calvary was where Adam died and was buried; so that in the very place on where death reigned, there Je | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Jerome, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . An apt connection, and smooth to the ear, but not true. For the place where they cut off the heads of men condemned to death, called in consequence Calvary, was outside the city gates, whereas we re | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They crucified Him with the thieves: And two others with Him, on either side one, and Jesus in the midst; thus fulfilling, filling the prophecy, And He was numbered with the transgressors. What they | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Yea, even the cross, if you consider it, was a judgment seat: for the Judge being the middle, one thief, who believed, was pardoned, the other, who mocked, was damned: a sign of what He would once d | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As letters are inscribed on a trophy declaring the victory, so Pilate wrote a title on Christ’s cross. And Pilate wrote a title, and put it on the cross: thus at once distinguishing Christ from the  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein was strewn that His kingdom was not, as they thought, destroyed, but rather strengthened. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But was Christ the King of the Jews only? or of the Gentiles too? Of the Gentiles too, as we read in the Psalms, Yet have I set My King upon My holy hill of Sion; after which it follows, Demand of M | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is probable that many Gentiles as well as Jews had come up to the feast. So the title was written in three languages, that all might read it: And it was written in Hebrew, and Greek, and Latin. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . These three were the languages most known there: the Hebrew, on account of being used in the worship of the Jews: the Greek, in consequence of the spread of Greek philosophy: the Latin, from the Rom | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The title written in three languages signifies that our Lord was King of the whole world; practical, natural, and spiritual. The Latin denotes the practical, because the Roman empire; was the most p | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:22 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But the Jews grudged our Lord this title: Then said the chief priests of the Jews to Pilate, Write not, The King of the Jews; but that He said, I am King of the Jews. For as Pilate wrote it, it was  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . O ineffable working of Divine power even in the hearts of ignorant men. Did not some hidden voice sound from within, and, if we may say so, with clamorous silence, saying to Pilate in the prophetic  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist describes the tunic, to show that it was of an inferior kind, the tunics commonly worn in Palestine being made of two pieces. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Others say that they did not weave in Palestine, as we do, the shuttle being driven upwards through the warp; so that among them the woof was not carried upwards but downwards. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Why they cast lots for it, next appears: They said therefore among themselves, Let us not rend it, but cast lots for it whose it should be. It seems then that the other garments were made up of equa | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Behold the sureness of prophecy. The Prophet foretold not only what they would part, but what they would not. They parted the raiment, but cast lots for the vesture. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Matthew in saying, They parted His garments, casting lots, means us to understand the whole division of the garments, including the tunic also for which they cast lots. Luke says the same: They part | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . According to some, The tunic without seam, woven from above throughout, is an allegory strewing that He who was crucified was not simply man, but also had Divinity from above. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:24 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The garment without seam denotes the body of Christ, which was woven from above; for the Holy Ghost came upon the Virgin, and the power of the Highest overshadowed her. This holy body of Christ then | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor let any one say that these things had no good signification, because they were done by wicked men; for if so, what shall we say of the cross itself; For that was made by ungodly men, and yet cer | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While the soldiers were doing their cruel work, He was thinking anxiously of His mother: These things therefore the soldiers did. Now there stood by the cross of Jesus His mother, and His mother’s s | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Jerome, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Mary which in Mark and Matthew is called the mother of James and Joses was the wife of Alpheus, and sister of Mary the mother of our Lord: which Mary John here designates of Cleophas, either fro | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Observe how the weaker sex is the stronger; standing by the cross when the disciples fly. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If Matthew and Mark had not mentioned by name Mary Magdalene, we should have thought that there were two parties, one of which stood far off, and the other near. But how must we account for the same | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Though there were other women by, He makes no mention of any of them, but only of His mother, to show us that v, e should specially honor our mothers. Our parents indeed, if they actually oppose the | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By the disciple whom Jesus loved, the Evangelist means himself; not that the others were not loved, but he was loved more intimately on account of his estate of chastity; for a Virgin our Lord calle | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Heavens! what honor does He pay to the disciple; who however conceals his name from modesty. For had he wished to boast, he would have added the reason why he was loved, for there must have been som | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This truly is that hour of the which Jesus, when about to change the water into wine, said, Mother, what have I to do with you? Mine hour is not yet come. Then, about to act divinely, He repelled th | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The shameless doctrine of Marcion is refuted here. For if our Lord were not born according to the flesh, and had not a mother, why did He make such provision for her? Observe how imperturbable He is | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:27 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does this to provide as it were another son for His mother in his place; And from that hour that disciple took her to his own. To his own what? Was not John one of those who said, Lo, we have lef | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Another reading is, Accepy eam disciplus in suam, his own mother some understand, but to his own care seems better. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who appeared man, suffered all these things, He who was God, ordered them: After this Jesus knowing that all things were now accomplished; i.e. knowing the prophecy in the Psalms, And when I was  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They were not softened at all by what they saw, but were the more enraged, and gave Him the cup to drink, as they did to criminals, i.e. with a hyssop. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The hyssop around which they put the sponge full of vinegar, being a mean herb, taken to purge the breast, represents the humility of Christ, which they hemmed in and thought they had circumvented.  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some say that the hyssop is put here for reed, its leaves being like a reed. When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, He said, It is finished. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . viz. what prophecy had foretold so long before. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may be asked here, why it is said, When Jesus had received the vinegar, when another Evangelists says, He would not drink. But this is easily settled. He did not receive the vinegar, to drink it, | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Then as there was nothing left Him to do before He died, it follows, And He bowed His head, and gave up the ghost, only dying when He had nothing more to do, like Him who had to lay down His life, a | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Ghost is put here for soul: for had the Evangelist meant any thing else by it, though the ghost departed, in the soul might still have remained. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He did not bow His head because He gave up the ghost, but He gave up the ghost because at that moment He bowed His head. Whereby the Evangelist intimates that He was Lord of all. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:30 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For whoever had such power to sleep when he wished, as our Lord had to die when He wished? What power must He have, for our good or evil, Who had such power dying? | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord gave up His ghost to God the Father, showing that the souls of the saints do not remain in the tomb, but go into the hand of the Father of all while sinners are reserved - for the place of  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Jews who strained at a gnat and swallowed a camel after their audacious wickedness, reason scrupulously about the day: The Jews therefore because it was the preparation, that the bodies should n | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Parasceue, i.e. preparation: the sixth day was so called because the children of Israel prepared twice the number of loaves on that day. For that Sabbath day was an high day, i. e. on account of the | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Not in order to take away the legs, but to cause death, that they might be taken down from the cross, and the feast day not be defiled by the sight of such horrid torments. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For it was commanded in the Law that the sun should not set on the punishment of anyone; or they were unwilling to appear tormentors and homicides on a feast day. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . How forcible is truth: their own devices it is that accomplish the fulfillment of prophecy: Then came the soldiers and broke the legs of the first, and of the other which was crucified with Him. But | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To please the Jews, they pierce Christ, thus insulting even His lifeless body. But the insult issues in a miracle: for a miracle it is that blood should flow from a dead body. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist has expressed himself cautiously; not struck, or wounded, but opened His side: whereby was opened the gate of life, from whence the sacraments of the Church flowed, without which we c | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This being the source whence the holy mysteries are derived, when you approach the awful cup, approach it as if you were about to drink out of Christ’s side. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Shame then upon them who mix not water with the wine in the holy mysteries: they seem as if they believed not that the water flowed from the side. Had blood flowed only, a man might have said that t | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As if to say, I did not hear it from others, but saw it with mine own eyes. And his record is true, he adds, not as if he had mentioned something so wonderful that his account would be suspected, bu | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:37 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He that saw it knows; let him that saw not believe his testimony. He gives testimonies from the Scriptures to each of these two things he relates. After, they brake not His legs, He adds, For these  | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Jerome, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This testimony is taken from Zacharias. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Joseph thinking that the hatred of the Jews would be appeased by His crucifixion, went with confidence to ask permission to take charge of His burial: And after this Joseph of Arimathea besought Pil | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Arimathea is the same as Ramatha, the city of Elkanah, and Samuel. It was providentially ordered that he should be rich, in order that he might have access to the governor, and just, in order that h | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was not of the twelve, but of the seventy, for none of the twelve came near. Not that their fear kept them back, for Joseph was a disciple, secretly for: fear of the Jews. But Joseph was a person | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In performing this last office to our Lord, he showed a bold indifference to the Jews, though he had avoided our Lord’s company when alive, for fear of incurring their hatred. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Their ferocity being appeased for the time by their success, he sought the body of Christ. He did not come as a disciple, but simply to perform a work of mercy, which is due to the evil as well as t | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must not read the words, at the first, first bringing a mixture of myrrh, but attach the first to the former clause. For Nicodemus at the first came to Jesus by night, as John relates in the form | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They bring the spices most efficacious for preserving the body from corruption, treating Him as a mere man. Yet this show great love. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must observe however that it was simple ointment; for they were not allowed to mix many ingredients together. Then took they the body of Jesus, and wound it in linen clothes with the spices, as t | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein the Evangelist intimates, that in paying the last offices of the dead, the custom of the nation is to be followed. It was the custom of the Jewish nation to embalm their dead bodies, in orde | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Nor does John here contradict the other Evangelists, who, though they are silent about Nicodemus, yet do not affirm that our Lord was buried by Joseph alone. Nor because they say that our Lord was w | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Hence hath come down the custom of the Church, of consecrating the Lord’s body not on silk or gold cloth, but in a clean linen cloth. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But as they were pressed for time, for Christ died at the ninth hour, and after that they had gone to Pilate, and taken away the body, so that the evening was now near, they lay Him in the nearest t | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As no one before or after Him was conceived in the womb of the Virgin Mary, so in this grave was there none buried before or after Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In that it was a new sepulcher, we are given to understand, that we are all renewed by Christ’s death, and death and corruption destroyed. Mark too the exceeding poverty that He took up for our sake | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Implying that the burial was hastened, in order to finish it before the evening, when, on account of the preparation, n which the Jews with us call more commonly in the Latin, Cena pure, it was unla | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sepulcher was near, that the disciples might approach it more easily, and be better witnesses of what took place there, and that even enemies might be made the witnesses of the burial, being pla | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, the name Joseph means, apt for the receiving of a good work; whereby we are admonished that we should make ourselves worthy of our Lord’s body, before we receive it. | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 19:42 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Even now in a certain sense Christ is put to death by the avaritious, in the person of the poor man suffering famine. Be therefore a Joseph, and cover Christ’s nakedness, and, not once, but continua | src: CAJohn.htm#19
John 20:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Sabbath being now over, during which it was unlawful to be there, Mary Magdalene could rest no longer, but came very early in the morning, to seek consolation at the grave: The first day of the  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary Magdalene, undoubtedly the most fervent in love, of all the women that ministered to our Lord; so that John deservedly mentions her only, and says nothing of the others who were with her, as we | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Una sabbati is the day which Christians call the Lord’s day, after our Lord’s resurrection. Matthew calls it prima sabbati. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Una sabbati, i.e. one day after the sabbath. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: The Jews called the days of the week sabbath, and the first day, one of the sabbaths, which day is a type of the life to come; for that life will be one day not cut short by any night, sinc | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . What Mark says, Very early in the morning, at the rising of the sun, does not contradict John’s words, when it was yet dark. At the dawn of day, there are yet remains of darkness, which disappear as | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is well said, When it was yet dark: Mary was seeking the Creator of all things in the tomb, and because she found Him not, thought He was stolen. Truly it was yet dark when she came to the sepulc | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Now took place what Matthew only relates, the earthquake, and rolling away of the stone, and fight of the guards. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord rose while the stone and seal were still on the sepulcher. But as it was necessary that others should be certified of this, the sepulcher is opened after the resurrection, and so the fact c | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And therefore she ran to tell the disciples, that they might seek Him with her, or grieve with her: Then she runs, and comes to Simon Peter, and to the other disciple, whom Jesus loved. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is the way in which he usually mentions himself. Jesus loved all, but him in an especial and familiar way. And says to them, They have taken away the Lord out of the sepulcher, and we know not  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She puts the part for the whole; she had come only to seek for the body of our Lord, and now she laments that our Lord, the whole of Him, is taken away. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some of the Greek copies have, taken away my Lord, which is more expressive of love, and of the feeling of an handmaiden. But only a few have this reading. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist does not deprive the woman of this praise, nor leaves out from shame, that they had the news first from her. As soon as they hear it, they hasten to the sepulcher. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But Peter and John before the others, for they loved most; Peter therefore went forth, and that other disciple, and came to the sepulcher. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But how came they to the sepulcher, while the soldiers were guarding it? an easy question to answer. After our Lord’s resurrection and the earthquake, and the appearance of the angel at the sepulche | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . After saying, came to the sepulcher he goes back and tells us how they came: So they ran both together: and the other disciple did outrun Peter, and came first to the sepulcher; meaning himself, but | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . On coming he sees the linen clothes set aside: And he stooping down, and looking in, saw the linen clothes lying. But he makes no further search: yet went he not in. Peter on the other hand, being o | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. That Jesus had risen again, some think: A but what follows contradicts this notion. He saw the sepulcher empty, and believed what the woman had said: For as yet they knew not the Scripture, tha | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:9 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But this account of the Evangelist must not be thought to be without some mystical meaning. By John, the y younger of the two, the synagogue; by Peter, the elder, the Gentile Church is represented:  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or thus: Peter is practical and prompt, John contemplative and intelligent, and learned in divine things. Now the contemplative man is generally beforehand in knowledge and intelligence, but the pra | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary Magdalene, who had been the sinner in the city, and who had washed out the spots of her sins by her tears, whose soul burned with love, did not retire from the sepulcher when the others did: Th | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. To the place where they were lodging, and from which they had ran to the sepulcher. But though the men returned, the stronger love of the woman fixed her to the spot. But Mary stood without at  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. Outside of the place where the stone sepulcher was, but yet within the garden. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Be not astonished that Mary wept for love at the sepulcher, and Peter did not; for the female sex is naturally tender, and inclined to weep. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The eyes then which had sought our Lord, and found Him not, now wept without interruption; more for grief that our Lord had been removed, than for His death upon the cross. For now even all memorial | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She then saw, with the other women, the Angel sitting on the right, on the stone which had been rolled away from the sepulcher, at whose words it was that she looked into the sepulcher. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The sight of the sepulcher itself was some consolation. Nay, behold her, to console herself still more, stooping down, to see the very place where the body lay: And as she wept, she stooped down, an | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . For to have looked once is not enough for love. Love makes one desire to look over and over again. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In her too great grief she could believe neither her own eyes, nor the disciples. Or was it a divine impulse which caused her to look in? | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She sought the body, and found it not; she persevered in seeking; and so it came to pass that she found. Her longings growing the stronger, the more they were disappointed, at last found and laid ho | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As her understanding was not so raised as to be able to gather from the napkins the fact of the resurrection, she is given the sight of Angels in bright apparel, who soothe her sorrow. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why did one sit at the head, the other at the feet? To signify that the glad tidings of Christ’s Gospel was to be delivered from the head to the feet, from the beginning to the end. The Greek wo | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Angel sits at the head when the Apostles preach that in the beginning was the Word: he sits, as it were, at the feet, when it is said, The Word was made flesh. By the two Angels too we may under | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Angels who appear say nothing about the resurrection; but by degrees the subject is entered on. First of all they address her compassionately, to prevent her from being overpowered by a spectacl | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The very declarations of Scripture which excite our tears of love, wipe away those very tears, by promising us the sight of our Redeemer again. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But she, thinking that they wanted to know why she wept, tells them the reason: She says to them, Because they have taken away my Lord. The lifeless body of her Lord, she calls her Lord, putting the | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As yet she knew nothing of the resurrection, but thought the body had been taken away. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here the Angels must be understood to rise up, for Luke describes them as seen standing. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The hour was now come, which the Angels announced, when sorrow should be succeeded by joy: And when she had thus said, she turned herself back. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why, when she is talking to the Angels, and before she has heard any thing from them, does she turn back? It seems to me that while she was speaking, Christ appeared behind her, and that the Ang | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must observe that Mary, who as yet doubted our Lord’s resurrection, turned back to see Jesus. By her doubting she turned her back, as it were, upon our Lord. Yet inasmuch as she loved, she saw Hi | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To the Angels He appeared as their Lord but not so to c the woman, for the sight coming upon her all at once, would have stupefied her. She was not to be lifted suddenly, but gradually to high thing | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus says to her, Woman, why do you weep? He asks the cause of her grief, to set her longing still more. For the mere mentioning His name whom she sought would inflame her love for Him. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Because He appeared as a common person, she c thought Him the gardener: She, supposing Him to be the gardener, says to Him, Sir, if you have borne Him from here, tell me where you have laid Him, and | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She was afraid that the Jews might vent their rage even on the lifeless body, and therefore wished to remove it to some secret place. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Perhaps, however, the woman was right, in believing Jesus to be the gardener. Was not He the spiritual Gardener, who by the power of His love had sown strong seeds of virtue in her breast? But how i | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Just as He was sometimes in the midst of the Jews, and they did not know Him till He pleased to make Himself known. But why does she turn herself; when she had turned herself before? It seems to me  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or she first turned her body, but thought Him what He was not; now she was turned in heart, and knew who He was. Let no one however blame her, because she called the gardener, Lord, and Jesus, Maste | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): The Evangelist does not add what she did upon recognizing Him, but we know from what our Lord said to her: Jesus says to her, Touch Me not. Mary then had tried to embrace His feet, but was not allowed | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if standing upon the earth, He is not touched, how shall He be touched sitting in heaven? And did He not before His ascension offer Himself to the touch of the disciples: Handle Me and see, for  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Touch is as it were the end of knowledge and He was unwilling that a soul intent upon Him should have its end, in thinking Him only what He seemed to be. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mary wished to be as familiar with Christ now, as she was before His Passion; forgetting, in her joy, that His body was made much more holy by its resurrection. So, Touch Me not, He says, to remind  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Hilary, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Heretics, among their other impieties, misinterpret these words of our Lord’s, and say, that if His Father is their Father, His God their God, He cannot be God Himself. But though He remained in the | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He does not say, Our Father, but, My Father and your Father: Mine therefore and yours in a different sense; Mine by nature, yours by grace. Nor does He say, Our God, but My God - under Him I am man  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . She then went away from. the sepulcher, i.e. from that part of the garden before the rock which had been hollowed out, and with her the other women. But these, according to Mark, were seized with tr | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . So the sin of mankind is buried in the very place whence it came forth. For whereas in Paradise the woman gave the man the deadly fruit, a woman from the sepulcher announced life to men; a woman del | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:18 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While she was going with the other women, according to Matthew, Jesus met them, saying, All hail. So we gather that there were two visions of Angels; and that our Lord too was seen twice once when M | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, Mary, which name signifies, mistress, enlightened, enlightener, star of the sea, stands for the Church, which is also Magdalene, i.e. towered, (Magdalene being Greek for tower) as we rea | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The disciples, when they heard what Mary told them, were obliged either to disbelieve, or, if they believed, to grieve that He did not count them worthy to have the sight of Him. He did not let them | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Wherein is strewn the infirmity of the Apostles. They assembled with doors shut, through that same fear of the Jews, which had before scattered them: Came Jesus, and stood in the midst. He came in t | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or because He waited till all were assembled: and with shut doors, that he might show how that in the very same way he had risen again, i.e. with the stone lying on the sepulcher. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some are strongly indisposed to believe this miracle, and argue thus: If the same body rose again, which hung upon the Cross, how could that body enter through shut doors? But if you comprehend the  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The shut door did not hinder the body, wherein Divinity resided. He could enter without open doors, who was as born without a violation of His mother’s virginity | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is wonderful that they did not think him a phantom. But Mary had provided against this, by the faith she had wrought in them. And He Himself too showed Himself so openly, and strengthened their w | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And because their faith wavered even with the material body before them, He showed them His hands and side: And when He had said this, He showed them His hands and His side. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The nails had pierced His hands, the lance had pierced His side. For the healing of doubting hearts, the marks of the wounds were still preserved. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . And what He had promised before the crucifixion, I shall see you again, and you, heart shall rejoice, is now fulfilled: Then were the disciples glad when they say the Lord. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The glory, wherewith the righteous shall shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father, i.e. in Christ’s body, we must believe to have been rather veiled than not to have been there at all. He a | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . All these things brought them to a most confident faith. As they were in endless war with the Jews, He says again, Then said Jesus to them again, Peace be to you. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A repetition is a confirmation: whether He repeats it because the grace of love is twofold, or because He it is who made of twain one. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . At the same time He shows the efficacy of the cross, by which He undoes all evil things, and gives all good things; which is peace. To the women above there was announced joy; for that sex was in so | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Father sent the Son, appointed Him to the work of redemption. He says therefore, As My Father has sent Me, even so send I you; i.e. I love you, now that I send you to persecution, with the same  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We have learnt that the Son is A equal to the Father: here He shows Himself Mediator; He Me, and I you. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Having then given them confidence by His own miracles, and appealing to Him who sent Him, He uses a prayer to the Father, but of His own authority gives them power: And when He had said this, He bre | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That corporeal breath was not the substance of the Holy Ghost, but to show, by meet symbol, that the Holy Ghost proceeded not only from the Father, but the Son. For who would be so mad as to say, th | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why is He first given too the disciples on earth, and afterwards sent from heaven? Because there are two commandments of love, to love God, and to love our neighbor. The spirit to love our neigh | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some say that by breathing He did not give them the Spirit, but made them meet to receive the Spirit. For if Daniel’s senses were so overpowered by the sight of the Angel, how would they have been o | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The love of the Church, which is shed abroad in our hearts by the Holy Spirit, remits the sins of those who partake of it; but retains the sins of those who do not. Where then He has said, Receive y | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must understand that those who first received the Holy Ghost, for innocence of life in themselves, and preaching to a few others, received it openly after the resurrection, that they might profit | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A priest though he may have ordered well his own life, yet, if he have not exercised proper vigilance over others, is sent to hell with the evil doers. Wherefore, knowing the greatness of their dang | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Didymus, double or doubtful, because he doubted in believing: Thomas, depth, because with most sure faith he penetrated into the depth of our Lord’s divinity. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It was not an accident that that particular disciple was not present. The Divine mercy ordained that a doubting disciple should, by feeling in his Master the wounds of the flesh heal in us the wound | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:25 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But why does this Evangelist say that Thomas was absent, when Luke writes that two disciples on their return from Emmaus found the eleven assembled? We must understand that Thomas had gone out, and  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As to believe directly, and any how, is the mark of too easy a mind, so is too much inquiring of a gross one: and this is Thomas’s fault. For when the Apostle said, We have seen the Lord, he did not | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Consider the mercy of the Lord, how for the sake; of one soul, He exhibits His wounds. And yet the disciples deserved credit, and He had Himself foretold the event. Notwithstanding, because one pers | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . You ask; If He entered by the shut door, where is the nature of His body? And I reply; If He walked on the sea, where is the weight of His body? The Lord did that as the Lord; and did He, after His  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): Jesus then comes Himself, and does not wait till Thomas interrogates Him. But to show that He heard what Thomas said to the disciples, He uses the same words. And first He rebukes him; Then says He to | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He might, had He pleased, have wiped all spot and trace of wound from His glorified body; but He had reasons for retaining them. He showed them to Thomas, who would not believe except he saw and tou | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We are, as I know not how, afflicted with such love for the blessed martyrs, that we would wish in that kingdom to see on their bodies the marks of those wounds which they have borne for Christ’s sa | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord gave that flesh to be touched which He had introduced through shut doors: wherein two wonderful, and, according to human reason, contradictory things appear, viz. that after the resurrectio | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our body also in that resurrection to glory will be subtle by means of the action of the Spirit, but palpable by its true nature, not, as Eutychius says, impalpable, and subtler than the winds and t | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thomas saw and touched the man, and confessed the. God whom he neither saw nor touched. By means of the one he believed the other undoubtingly: Thomas answered and said to Him, My Lord and My God. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who had been before unbelieving, after touching the body showed himself the best divine; for he asserted the twofold nature and one Person of Christ; by saying, My Lord, the human nature by sayin | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says not, has touched me, but, has seen me; the sight being a kind of general sense, and put in the place often of the other four senses; as when we say, Hear, and see how well it sounds; smell,  | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But when the Apostle says, Faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen, it is plain that things which are seen, are objects not of faith, but of knowledge. Why then i | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He uses the past tense, in the future to His knowledge having already taken place by His own predestination. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If any one then says, Would that I had lived in those times, and seen Christ doing miracles! let him reflect, Blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Here He means the disciples who had believed without seeing the print of the nails, and His side. | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 20:31 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John having related less than the other Evangelists, adds, And many other signs truly did Jesus in the presence of His disciples, which are not written in this book. Yet neither did the others relat | src: CAJohn.htm#20
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The preceding words of the Evangelist seem to indicate the end of the book, but He goes on farther to give an account of our Lord’s appearance by the sea of Tiberias: After these things Jesus showed | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, Afterwards, because He did not go continually with His disciples as before; and, manifested Himself, because His body being incorruptible, it was a condescension to allow Himself to be seen | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist, after his wont, first states the thing itself, and then says how it took place: And on this wise showed He Himself. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . As our Lord was not with them regularly, and the Spirit was as not given them, and they had received no commission, and had nothing to do, they followed the trade of fishermen: And on this wise show | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may be asked, why Peter, who was a fisherman before his conversion, returned to fishing, when it is said, No man putting his hand to the plow, and looking back, is fit for the kingdom of God. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . If the disciples had done this after the death of Jesus, and before His resurrection, we should have imagined that they did it in despair. But now after that He has risen from the grave, after seein | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The craft which was exercised without sin before conversion, was no sin after it. Wherefore after his conversion Peter returned to fishing; but Matthew sat not down again for the receipt? of custom. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The other disciples followed Peter: They say to him, We also go with you; for from this time they were all bound together; and they wished too to see the fishing: They went forth and entered into a  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The fishing was made to be very unlucky, in order to raise their astonishment at the miracle after: And that night they caught nothing | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the midst of their labor and distress, Jesus presented Himself to them: But when the morning was now come, Jesus stood on the shore: but the disciples knew not that it was Jesus. He did not make  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist alludes to himself here the same way he always does. He recognized our Lord either by the miracle, or by the sound of His voice, or the association of former occasions on which He fou | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peter’s girding himself is a sign of modesty. He girt himself with a linen coat, such as Thamian and Tyrian fishermen throw over them, when they have nothing else on, or even over their other clothe | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He went to Jesus with the ardor with which he did every thing: And cast himself into the sea. And the other disciples came in a little ship. We must not understand here that Peter walked on the top  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Parenthesis; for it follows, dragging the net with fishes. The order is, The other disciples came in a little ship, dragging the net with fishes. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Another miracle follows: As soon then as they were come to land, they saw a fire of coals there, and fish laid thereon, and bread. He no longer works upon already existing materials, but in a still  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We must not understand that the bread was laid on the coals, but read it as if it stood, They saw a fire of coals there, and fish laid on the coals; and they saw bread. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To show that it was no vision, He bade them take of the fish they had caught. Jesus says to them, Bring of the fish which you have now caught. Another miracle follows of viz. that the net was not br | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, in the draught of fishes He signified the mystery of the Church, a such as it will be at the final resurrection of the dead. And to make this clearer, it is put near the end of the book. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the night time before the presence of the sun, Christ, the Prophets took nothing; for though they endeavored to correct the people, yet these often fell into idolatry. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It may be asked, why after His resurrection He stood on the shore to receive the disciples, whereas before He walked on the sea? The sea signifies the world, which is tossed about with various cause | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The shore is the end of the sea, and therefore signifies the end of the world. The Church is here typified as she will be at the end of the world, just as other draughts of fishes typified her as sh | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Bede, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By the two hundred cubits is signified the twofold grace of love; the love of God and the love of our neighbor; for by them we approach to Christ. The fish broiled is Christ who suffered. He deigned | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To Peter was the holy Church committed; to him is it specially said, Feed My sheep. That then which is afterwards declared by word, is now signified by act. He it is who draws the fishes to the firm | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . In the draught before, the number of the fishes is not mentioned, as if in fulfillment of the prophecy in the Psalm, If I should declare them, and speak of them, they should be more than l am able t | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:11 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Seven and ten multiplied by three make fifty-one. The fiftieth year was a year of rest to the whole people from all their work. In unity is true rest; for where division is, true rest cannot be. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is not then signified that only a hundred and fifty-three saints are to rise again to eternal life, but this number represents all who partake of the grace of the Holy Spirit. which number too co | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The fishing being over, our Lord invites them to dine: Jesus says to them, Come and dine. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John does not say that He ate with them, but Luke does. He ate however not to satisfy the wants of nature, but to show the reality of His resurrection. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The bodies of the just, when they rise again, shall need neither the word of life that they die not of disease, or old age, nor any bodily nourishment to prevent hunger and thirst. For they shall be | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . No one dared to doubt that it was He, much less deny it; so evident was it. Had any one doubted, he would have asked. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He means that they had not confidence to talk to Him, as before, but sat looking at Him in silence and awe, absorbed in regarding His altered and now supernatural form, and unwilling to ask any ques | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Mystically, the fried fish is Christ Who suffered. And He is the bread that came down from heaven. To Him the Church is united to His body for participation of eternal bliss. Wherefore He says, Brin | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Gregory the Great, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . By holding this last feast with seven disciples, he declares that they only who are full of the sevenfold grace of the Holy Spirit, shall be with Him in the eternal feast. Time also is reckoned by p | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Inasmuch, however, as He did not converse with them regularly, or in the same way as before, the Evangelist adds, This is now the third time that Jesus showed Himself to His disciples, after that He | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:14 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Which has reference not to manifestations, but to days; i.e. the first day after He had risen, eight days after that, when Thomas saw and believed, and this day at the draught of fishes; and thencef | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . We find in the four Evangelists then occasions mentioned; on which our Lord was seen after His resurrection: one at the sepulcher by the women; a second by the one omen returning from the sepulcher; | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The dinner being ended, He commits to Peter the superintendence over the sheep of the world, not to the others: So when they had dined, Jesus says to Simon Peter, Simon, son of Jonas, Do love you Me | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord asked this, knowing it: He knew that Peter not only loved Him, but loved Him more than all the rest. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He is called Simon, son of John, John being his natural father. But mystically, Simon is obedience, John grace, a name well befitting him who was so obedient to God’s grace, that he loved our Lord m | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . While our Lord was being condemned to death, he feared, and denied Him. But by His resurrection Christ implanted love in his heart, and drove away fear. Peter denied, because he feared to die: but w | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That which most of all attracts the Divine love is care and love for our neighbor. Our Lord passing by the rest, addresses this command to Peter: he being the chief of the Apostles, the mouth of the | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . A third time He asks the same question, and gives the same command; to show of what importance He esteems the superintendence of His own sheep, and how He regards it as the greatest proof of love to | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Thence is taken the custom of threefold confession in baptism. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The question asked for the third time disturbed him: Peter was grieved because He said to him the third time, Love you Me? He was afraid perhaps of receiving a reproof again for professing to love m | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He was grieved because he was asked so often by Him Who knew what He asked, and gave the answer. He replies therefore from his inmost heart; you know that I love You. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says no more, He only replies what he knew himself; he knew he loved Him; whether any else loved Him he could not tell, as he could not see into another’s heart: Jesus says to him, Feed My sheep; | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . There is a difference perhaps between lambs and sheep. The lambs are those just initiated, the sheep are the perfected. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Alcuin, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . To feed the sheep is to support the believers in Christ from falling from the faith, to provide earthly sustenance for those under us, to preach and exemplify withal our preaching by our lives, to r | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:17 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . They who feed Christ’s sheep, as if they were their own, not Christ’s, show plainly that they love themselves, not Christ; that they are moved by lust of glory, power, gain, not by the love of obeyi | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But unfaithful servants arose, who divided Christ’s flock, and handed down the division to their successors: and you hear them say, Those sheep are mine, what seek you with my sheep, I will not let  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having made Peter declare his love, informs him of his future martyrdom; an intimation to us how we should love: Verily, verily, I say to you, When you were young, you girded yourself, and  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:19 | Origen, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . It is not easy to find any ready to pass at once from this life; and so he says to Peter, When you are old, you shall stretch forth your hand. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:19 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . That is, shall be crucified. And to come to this end, Another shall gird you, and carry you where you would not. First He said what would come to pass, secondly, how it would come to pass. For it wa | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:19 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He says, Where you would not, with reference to the natural reluctance of the soul to be separated from the body; an instinct implanted by God to prevent men putting an end to themselves. Then raisi | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He who denied and loved, died in perfect love for Him, for Whom he had promised to die with wrong haste. It was necessary that Christ should first die for Peter’s salvation, and then Peter die for C | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Our Lord having foretold to Peter by what hat death he should glorify God, bids him follow Him. And when He had spoken this, He says to him, Follow Me. Why does He say, Follow Me, to Peter, and not  | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Peter hearing that he was to suffer death for Christ, asks whether John was to die: Then Peter, turning about, sees the disciple whom Jesus loved following; which also leaned on His breast at supper | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . He calls himself the disciple whom Jesus loved, because Jesus had a greater and more familiar love for him, than for the rest; so that He made him lie on His breast at supper. In this way John the m | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . i.e. Shall he not die? | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Jesus says to him, What is that to you? and He then repeats, Follow you Me, as if John would not follow Him, because he wished to remain till He came; Then went this saying abroad among the disciple | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or let him say, Christ did not deny that John was to die, for whatever is born cries; but said, I will that he tarry till I come, i.e. to live to the end of the world, and then he shall suffer marty | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Or perhaps he will allow that John still lies in his sepulcher at Ephesus, but asleep, not dead; and will give us a proof, that the soil over his grave is moist and watery, owing to his respiration. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Gloss, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . I will that he tarry, i.e. I will not that he suffer martyrdom, but wait for the quiet dissolution of the flesh, when I shall come and receive him into eternal blessedness. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . When our Lord says to Peter, Follow Me, He confers upon him the superintendence over all the faithful, and at the same time bids him imitate Him in every thing, word and work. He shows too His affec | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . But if it be asked, How then did James assume the see of Jerusalem? I answer, that our Lord enthroned Peter, not as Bishop of this see, but as Doctor of the whole world: Then Peter, turning about, s | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:23 | Theophylact, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . Some have understood, Till I come, to mean, Till I come to punish the Jews who have crucified Me, and strike them with the Roman rod. For they say that this Apostle lived up to the time of Vespasian | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The Evangelist then corrects the opinion taken up by the disciples. | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . John appeals to his own knowledge of these events, having been witness of them: This is the disciple which testifies of these things. When we assert any undoubted fact in common life, we do not with | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:25 | Augustine, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . The which, if they should be written every one, I suppose that even the world itself could not contain the books that should, be written; meaning not the world had not space for them, but that the c | src: CAJohn.htm#21
John 21:25 | Chrysostom, Catena Aurea on John (Aquinas): . This is said to show the power of Him Who did the miracles; i. e that it was as easy for Him to do them, as it is for us to speak of them, seeing He is God over all, blessed for ever. THE END | src: CAJohn.htm#21
